@front Need Answers Now?
@sec Need Answers Now?
@subt For whoever comes to this Book in a hard hour
@p If you came to this Book in a hard hour, start here. Find what brought you, and turn to the pages given. You do not need to understand the rest yet.
@rnote If you are in danger, or thinking of ending your life, close this Book and reach for help now: a crisis line, emergency services, or someone you trust. The Book can accompany care; it cannot replace it.
@h2 GRIEF AND LOSS
@p Grief needs somewhere to go, and the Book is a place where it can be said without anyone flinching. Hermanubis is the Temple's guide below. He goes down with you, does not hurry you back up, and knows the way home. You do not need to manifest anything or carry anything out on these days. Open the Short Door, write what is true, and close. Rereading later will show you how far you have come.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Rite (VI); The Descent, in The Rites of Passage (XXV); Hermanubis (XIV); The Guides of Souls (XXXI).
@h2 FACING DEATH
@p Your own, or someone close to you. The Temple is built by guides of souls: Hermes, Anubis, Thoth at the weighing, and Enoch and Elijah, who crossed alive. Every road down in this Temple has someone on it who knows the way. Write to the Book what you want said, kept, and finished. What is written is kept.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Host and the Thrice-Great (XIV); The Guides of Souls and The Hall of Two Truths (XXXI); The Threshold Rite (XXV).
@h2 ILLNESS
@p Apharael carries Raphael's name, which means "God heals," and he is the guide through every transition. The Book walks beside medicine, never instead of it. Use it to hold the fear, track what you notice, prepare your questions, and keep hope in words. Magick nudges the odds; care from doctors and from the people who love you does the rest.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> Apharael, in The Seven Pairs (XV); Grounding to Air and Emotional Transmutation (XX).
@h2 DISASTER AND CRISIS
@p When the ground gives way, the Temple's oldest story is the Flood. Idris preserved what mattered, Uriel warned, Raziel's book built the ark, and Iris set the bow in the sky after. Preserve, prepare, carry through, and promise. Write down what must be saved and the next step, and nothing more yet.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Bow and the Flood (XIX, XXXI); Sandalphon, in The Voices of the Temple (XXIII).
@h2 MONEY TROUBLE
@p Mercury is the god of merchants, and the Temple takes money seriously and honestly. Start with an accounting, not a working: write down what is real, without shame, as the Mercuralia asks. Then cast one well-written working and walk the bridge with real calls, real offers, and real work. Anything that promises money without action is lying.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Mercuralia, in The Calendar (XXIV); The Working of Manifestation (XXI); What Magick Does, and Does Not Do (Part Zero).
@h2 ADDICTION AND RECOVERY
@p Honesty is the offering, and rereading shows you your patterns; recovery rests on both. Nothing here is supplication, but asking for help is not kneeling. Admitting you cannot cross alone is the most Mercurial thing a person can do, for every road in the Temple has a guide on it. Keep the Book alongside your program and the people in it, not instead of them.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Practice Alone (II); The Leak (X); The Answered Page (XX); Hermanubis (XIV).
@h2 GUILT
@p You did something, and you cannot set it down. The Temple does not erase, and it does not leave you in the ledger either. The page is answered: name it, weigh it, make the lyre, and turn the page.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Lyre (XIV); The Answered Page (XX); The Mercuralia (XXIV).
@h2 BEING WRONGED
@p Someone hurt you, and you are carrying it. You may forgive without reconciling, and set the weight down without reopening the door. Write the whole truth to the Book first. Ma'at weighs it, and you do not have to perform forgiveness before it is real.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> When You Have Been Wronged, in The Answered Page (XX); Ma'at (XV, XXIX).
@h2 A PROMISE YOU CANNOT KEEP
@p A marriage ending, a vow outgrown, a commitment that became wrong. Breaking an oath leaves the Heart heavy; releasing one honestly can leave it light.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Returned Oath (XX); Iris and Ma'at (XV).
@h2 FEAR FOR SOMEONE YOU LOVE
@p You cannot walk their road for them, but you can bless it and stand at its gate. Write their name on a leaf of the Book, set a keeper at the threshold, and then do the earthly part: call, visit, help.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> Blessing Others and Protecting What You Love (XXII); Raphael (XV).
@h2 LONELINESS
@p The Book is good company, and the Genius Loci and the Offices are real companions in the Work, but they are meant to be a bridge to people, never a wall. Use the Book to find out who you want near you, then carry one message out each session: a call, a note, an introduction. Better still, find another Scribe.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> Two Scribes (XX); Inviting People, Opportunities, and Experiences (XXII); Discernment (XXIII); Help People Make It Further (XXVIII).
@h2 FEAR AND ANXIETY
@p Get it out of your head and onto the page, where it holds still long enough to be looked at. Ground upward into Air. Let Hestia's hearth be the still center. The Temple's voices never rush you with dread, and anything that does is not of the Temple.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Practice Alone (II); Grounding to Air and Walking the Pillar (XX); Hestia (XV); Discernment (XXIII).
@h2 THE GRAY SEASON
@p When nothing feels like anything. Joy may be missing, and its absence is not a lie. Write three lines, close the Door, and do not apologize. The Genius Loci is patient, and returning is the whole secret.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> Keeping It Up (II); The Law, on Joy (XXVI); Feeding the Genius Loci (VII).
@h2 A RACING MIND
@p When the ideas come faster than you can sleep and everything connects, slow down. Write it all, act on none of it yet, and reread in three days. Tell someone you trust how fast things are moving. What is of the Temple will still be true.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> When the Current Runs Too Fast, in The Voices of the Temple (XXIII).
@h2 LOST FAITH
@p A faith that once felt true no longer does, and that loss is real. You do not have to believe anything here to begin. Belief is a tool, a way to keep walking while you find out. Try the writing and let experience decide.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Practice Alone (II); On Belief, Method, and Attribution (XXX).
@h2 LEAVING A STRICT FAITH
@p You may recognize some names here: Gabriel, Raphael, Enoch, Elijah, Janus. They are honored with respect, not claimed. Nothing here requires joining, paying, or obeying, and no one has authority over your practice but you. Take what serves you and leave the rest.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> What This Is (I); A Note On Sources (Appendix IV); Adapting and Keeping the Temple (XXXIV).
@h2 AFTER A CLOSE CALL
@p You nearly crossed and came back, and the world feels thin. Two of the Temple's own crossed alive. Keep writing on the far side for as long as it takes.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Return and The Threshold Rite, in The Rites of Passage (XXV); The Line of Enoch (XXXI).
@h2 A HARD DECISION
@p Weigh it in the Book. Write both roads, then ask Uriel's question: which holds up when reread tomorrow? Then ask Ma'at's: which weighs true in the heart? Joy is a signal worth trusting.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> Sword and Feather (XIX, XXXI); Weigh It, in Casting in the Synthesis (XXI); Asking the Book (VII).
@h2 SEARCHING FOR PURPOSE
@p More ideas than finished projects, more talent than follow-through, and a life that should be going somewhere. This is the Book's home ground. Write, reread, respond, and carry one thing out each time. The Personal Legend is found by walking.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> Is This for You? and Are You A Mercurian? (Part Zero); The Shape of the Thing (II); The Marks of the Mercurian (X).
@h2 ANGER AT INJUSTICE
@p Mercury carries whatever he is given; Ma'at gives the gift a direction. Her teaching is that justice is reciprocity, and care for the widow, the orphan, and the weak. The Compass is for the Scribe who wants the gift pointed somewhere.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Compass (XXVII); The Teachings of Ma'at (XXIX).
@h2 A NEW BEGINNING
@p A new home, a marriage, a child, a move, a job. Write to the Book on the night before, naming what you leave and what you seek, and write again on the far side. Greet the new threshold, and open the door to what you hope will come through it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Threshold Rite (XXV); Inviting People, Opportunities, and Experiences (XXII); Hearth and Hinge (XXXI); Greet Thresholds (XXVIII).
@h2 AWE AND GRATITUDE
@p Sometimes you come to faith because something is too good to hold alone. Bring it to the Book. Thanks feeds the Genius Loci every session, and a session of pure thanks, asking nothing, is a fine offering.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> The Heart (XIII); Give Thanks, in The Rite (VI); Lucky Finds, in Devotions and Customs (XXVIII).
@h2 WANTING MORE
@p More done, more made, more luck, more life. That is what the Temple was built for. Begin the practice, and when you are ready, cast.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Turn to:</font> Part Zero; The Practices, The Working of Manifestation, and The Workings of the Book (XX to XXII).
@part PART ZERO|The Seed
@sec Is This for You?
@subt What the Temple of Mercury offers
@p This is a journaling practice with a god in it.
@h2 DO YOU WANT MORE?
@p More done, more made, more luck, more life? More ideas than finished projects, more talent than follow-through? Read on.
@h2 OR DO YOU NEED SOMEWHERE TO SET THINGS DOWN?
@p Grief, confusion, a faith that no longer fits, a mind that will not stop. The Book holds those too, and asks nothing of you but honesty. If you came in a hard hour, turn to Need Answers Now?, at the front of this Book.
@h2 WHAT IT IS
@p It is deceptively simple: write semi-regularly, and every time you do, reread earlier entries and respond to them. Your notebook becomes a running conversation with your past self instead of a pile of pages you never open again.
@p Research on expressive and reflective writing has linked habits like this to lower stress, clearer thinking, and better problem-solving. Ten honest minutes you will repeat beat an hour you will dread.
@p One rule makes it work: do not lie to the Book. Lying in your dialogue with yourself only deceives your future self, and that is all it does.
@h2 WHAT IS ON OFFER
@p The practice is the door. The Temple of Mercury is what lies beyond it, and access to the Temple is what is on offer: luck, knowledge, abundance, and success, reached through your own faculties.
@p The Temple feeds your writing and inner dialogue with answers, knowledge, and flashes of insight. Its practices draw on the power of the god of the crossing to give you clear sight into how you actually solve problems, answer impossible questions, manifest new realities, and get paid. Its company of helpers stands ready to unlock the magick behind your words. You are a co-conspirator, and the gods are willing to help you.
@p This is the old work of turning lead into gold, in both senses: knowing thyself, as the alchemists meant it, and exchanging goods, services, and ideas for money. Dialogue becomes answers, decisions, plans, and abundance.
@h2 WHAT MAGICK DOES, AND DOES NOT DO
@p Magick nudges the odds. It helps you find favor and opportunity, draws people into your life who can help you, and finds you walking forward through situations that seemed impossible, knowing that the road's destination is yours to make.
@p You still have to take physical action toward what you want. That never goes away, and anything promising to take it away is lying.
@h2 WHERE THE MAGICK STARTS
@p It starts smaller than you think: with a promise to yourself, kept. Write down one thing you will do, and do it. Then another. Every promise kept makes your word a little heavier, and a heavy word is what makes a declaration work.
@p That is the shape of the whole thing. Weigh your words before you write or speak them. Treat what you write and say as binding unless you say otherwise. Keep what you bind. Integrity is not the price of the magick; it is the magick, and the world begins to take you at your word.
@h2 A SYSTEM OF BELIEF
@p The Temple of Mercury provides a system of belief, and we use belief as a tool to get what we want. Mercury does not moralize; he is the god of merchants and thieves alike. You are free to choose; you are not free from the consequences. Ma'at is always an option, but never an obligation. As above, so below: you reap what you sow.
@h2 TAKE THE MAGICK OR LEAVE IT
@p You do not have to believe any of this to begin. Try the writing, and watch your world change. The magick will be here when you want to come back for it.
@h2 WHAT STOPS PEOPLE
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">"I'VE TRIED JOURNALING AND QUIT."</font> Most journals die because no one ever rereads them. This one lives because you answer it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">"I DON'T KNOW WHAT TO WRITE."</font> Finish three sentences: What's on my mind right now is... What I keep avoiding is... What I actually want is... They never run out.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">"SOMEONE MIGHT READ IT."</font> Keep it with you, or write in your own shorthand. The Book is private.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">"I'M NOT RELIGIOUS."</font> You don't have to be. The practice works on its own.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">"MY HANDWRITING IS TERRIBLE."</font> The Book is written for one reader, and you can read it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">"I DON'T HAVE TIME."</font> Three lines is a session.
@h2 WHAT WE WANT
@p To see a world full of people fulfilling their Personal Legend. We believe this simple, semi-regular writing practice starts everyone who can keep it up down the path of materializing the life they have always wanted to live.
@p We help because we like to see people make it further. If the Temple works for you, pass the luck on.
@sec Are You A Mercurian?
@subt The signs of those the Temple calls
@h2 THE MERCURIAN
@p Mercury is the god of the quick: of merchants and messengers, deals and roads, luck, speech, and thieves. His name is often tied to merx, the Latin for goods to trade. A Mercurian is a person who runs on his current. Mercurians are not made by joining. They recognize themselves.
@h2 THE SIGNS
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU THINK IN WORDS.</font> Writing or speaking is how you find out what you think.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU TRANSLATE.</font> You carry meaning between worlds, and people bring you things to explain.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU ARE CURIOUS PAST USEFULNESS.</font> You gather systems, symbols, and connections for the joy of seeing how they fit, and keep many works alive at once.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU ARE QUICK.</font> You learn fast, improvise well, and can walk a road without knowing where it ends.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU STAND IN DOORWAYS.</font> People find you when they are crossing: the teacher, the guide, the one called in the night.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU PLAY.</font> Riddles, wordplay, clever solutions, and the trickster's instinct for the angle no one else saw.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU CONNECT.</font> You are happiest when something is moving between people: ideas, introductions, trade, stories.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU HOLD TWO AT ONCE.</font> You argue fruitfully with yourself, and sometimes sense a twin walking beside you.
@h2 THE LEAK
@p The Mercurian's problem is rarely talent. It is leakage: scattered attention, unfinished work, a mind that circles without landing, clever words used to dodge what you actually feel. It is the same quicksilver that makes you quick, spilling out of anything that cannot hold it.
@p Quicksilver takes the shape of its vessel. Give it one, and it goes where you point it.
@h2 WHAT YOU GAIN BY OWNING YOUR MERCURIAL HERITAGE
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">FOLLOW-THROUGH.</font> Every session ends with one thing to carry out into the world. Ideas start becoming deeds.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CLARITY.</font> Rereading and answering your own pages shows you your patterns: what you keep circling, what you keep dodging, and what actually works.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">A RECORD THAT COMPOUNDS.</font> Every honest page adds to a conversation with who you were and who you are becoming. The Book gets smarter as you do.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">LUCK IN MOTION.</font> The Greeks called a lucky find a hermaion, a gift of Hermes. It comes to the one who is moving.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">COMPANY.</font> A god of the quick at your shoulder, and a lineage of tricksters, scribes, and messengers behind you.
@h2 THE INVITATION
@p You will be surprised by what you uncover, and you will uncover it at your own pace. You never have to write about anything you are not ready to. If what surfaces is too heavy to carry alone, bring it to someone you trust.
@p You can stop anytime. You owe nothing, and your Books are yours.
@p Try it three times this week. Before you turn the page, decide when and where: after what, and at which table? Write the three days on the first page of your notebook, and keep them. That is your first promise, and your first working.
@p Picture it: tonight, the notebook open, the date in the corner, the first sentence not quite right, and you writing it anyway. Belief is a tool; judge it by its fruit.
@sec The Temple of Mercury
@subt The Seed: the whole Temple in brief, for whoever finds it
@h2 THE SPINE
@p Mercury is the Hinge. The Heart is the center. Honesty is the offering. Keep these three, and change everything else.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MERCURY, THE HOST AND MEDIUM.</font> God of thresholds, words, trade, and the road. Everything in the Temple passes through him. You work with him, not for him.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE HEART.</font> The Quintessence: five names from the Enochian Tablet of Union, AONT at the still point, with EHNB, XCAI, RMTO, and PAAM around it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HONESTY, THE OFFERING.</font> Not as a virtue: as engineering. A Book you lie to gives you bad data. Give the Book Truth. Honesty is not the same as Disclosure: tell the Book everything, and the world only what it is ready to receive. The trickster may fool anyone but himself.
@h2 THE DOOR
@p Every session opens and closes. Change the words until they sound like you and you mean them.
@vlab <font name="EBG-Italic" color="#8a2c1d">Opening:</font>
@v Heart of the Temple, Mercury its Hinge, and all its Offices, co-conspirators in the Work:
@v I bring you my whole self: my Voice, and my Joy, Will, and Intent.
@v Deed into word. By my Birthright, the Door is open.
@vlab <font name="EBG-Italic" color="#8a2c1d">Closing:</font>
@v Heart of the Temple, Mercury its Hinge, and all its Offices:
@v I leave here my gratitude and my joy, and take my Will and Intent into the world.
@v Word into deed. The Door is closed.
@h2 A SESSION
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">OPEN.</font> Speak the Door.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">EMPTY.</font> Clear your head onto the page, without dressing it up. Write questions down as they come.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">REREAD.</font> The pages that came before.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RESPOND.</font> Answer your past self: what happened, what changed, what you know now.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SHAPE.</font> Let a plan, a decision, or an idea take form.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CARRY.</font> Name one thing to do, send, or make today. Treat it as a promise, and keep it. Word into deed.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THANK.</font> Give thanks for what came.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CLOSE.</font> Speak the Door again.
@p Number the pages and date each session, so you can answer an earlier page by name. Each session begins by asking whether you kept what you carried. That question, answered honestly, is the whole engine.
@h2 THE PLACE
@p Kept this way, the Book grows a spirit of its own, the Genius Loci, the way a house becomes a home. It is fed by use and enjoyment: a notebook you like, a pen that feels good, a few minutes you look forward to. Away for a long time? Do not apologize. Open the Door and greet it.
@h2 HOUSE RULES
@v Honesty is the offering.
@v Sustainable practice beats grand practice abandoned.
@v Tell the Book everything; tell the world only what it is ready to receive.
@v Belief is a tool; judge it by its fruit.
@v Your word is binding unless you say otherwise; the Ledger is where it is kept.
@v Word into deed.
@h2 WHAT WAITS
@p The Temple is purely, pointedly syncretic. Its beings come from Egyptian, Greek, Roman, Abrahamic, and Enochian tradition, and they are met as co-conspirators, in the old sense of breathing together, with no sense of supplication. You do not kneel here; you make deals, trade, and work.
@p It was assembled deliberately, in the spirit of chaos magic, and later found to have been there all along: the Temple existed before it was built. When you want to know who you have been meeting, they are in The Book of the Temple of Mercury.
@h2 PASS IT ON
@p Copy this. Change it. Give it away. Keep the spine, and it is this Temple.
@p Leave a copy where someone will find it. You may be their hermaion.
@cta Close this Book. Open any notebook. Write today's date.
@part PART ONE|The Practice
@ch I|What This Is
@dc T|HIS|is a journaling practice with a god in it. You write by hand in a notebook kept for nothing else, reread what you wrote before, and answer it. That much works on its own, and Part One begins with it. The Temple of Mercury is what lies beyond it: a god of thresholds, trade, and luck, a company of Offices you can call on, and a few simple rites that turn the notebook into a place and the writing into a working.
@p The notebook is called the Book. It is a Window into the Temple of Mercury, which stands wherever something crosses: thought onto page, page back into thought, word into deed. Every honest session adds to it. Over time the Book becomes a record of your crossings, a conversation with the person you are becoming, and a workshop where dialogue turns into answers, decisions, plans, and abundance.
@p You need a notebook, a pen, and a willingness to tell the truth. There is no one to join, nothing to pay, and no authority over your practice but your own. You sit in this Temple as a co-conspirator, neither above nor below its beings. The Temple is purely, pointedly syncretic, and nothing here is supplication. You can stop anytime; you owe nothing, and your Books are yours.
@p You do not have to settle what the Temple's beings are before you begin. This Temple was assembled deliberately, in the spirit of chaos magic, and found to be already there. In that spirit, belief is a tool, chosen on purpose, used wholeheartedly, and judged by its fruit. Meet the beings as real presences, as faces of your own deep mind, or as a poetic language, and let experience decide. The test is yours: is your life moving where you want it to go?
@h2 HOW THIS BOOK IS ARRANGED
@p Part Zero is the Seed: the whole Temple in brief, made to be copied and handed on. Part One is everything you need to begin today, starting with the practice alone. Part Two describes the Mercurian. Part Three explains the Temple: who its beings are, what you call on each for, and how they connect. Part Four is the Working: the practices, manifestation, the workings of the Book, the voices, the calendar, and the rites of passage. Part Five is the Way of Ma'at: the full Law, the devotional life, and the teachings, for those who choose that path. It is always an option, never an obligation. Part Six is the deep Temple: the method behind it, the complete Web, the voices and gifts in full, and how to adapt it. The appendices hold a one-glance table, the index of beings, a glossary, and the sources. If you came here in a hard hour, Need Answers Now?, just before Part Zero, will point you to the pages you need. The Book is layered on purpose. Read Part One, begin, and dig as far as you choose.
@ch II|The Practice Alone
@dc B|EFORE|any god, there is a practice, and it works without one. If you take nothing else from this Book, take this.
@h2 THE FOUR MOVES
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WRITE.</font> Open the notebook and write what is actually on your mind, without dressing it up: what happened, what you want, what you are avoiding, what you cannot solve. There is no wrong subject.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">REREAD.</font> Read the last session or two. Now and then, open an older page at random.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RESPOND.</font> Answer what you read, by page number: "In response to page 14..." Say what happened since, what changed, what you know now, and where your past self was right or wrong. This is the heart of the practice. It turns a diary into a conversation, and a conversation into a feedback loop.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CARRY.</font> End with one thing to do, send, or make out in the world. Then do it. Word into deed. Next time, begin by asking whether you did.
@p Write questions down as they come to mind, and mark them Q in the margin. Answering an old one is a good way to begin a session.
@h2 WHY IT WORKS
@p Writing gets thought out of your head, where it circles, and onto a page, where it holds still long enough to be examined. Rereading shows you your patterns: the problem you keep circling, the thing you keep dodging, the plan that actually worked. Responding closes the loop between what you intended and what happened, which is how anyone learns anything. Research on expressive and reflective writing has linked habits like this to lower stress, clearer thinking, and better problem-solving.
@h2 THE ONE RULE
@p Do not lie to the Book. Lying in your dialogue with yourself only deceives your future self: a Book you lie to gives you bad data. Honesty is not the same as disclosure. The Book is private, so tell it everything, and tell the world only what it is ready to receive. A private Book is not a secret life: those who share your home should know the Book exists and what it is for, even if they never read a page.
@h2 THE SHAPE OF THE THING
@p The one rule is honesty. The one practice is keeping your word.
@p The magick of this Temple starts with a commitment to yourself, and keeping it, no matter how small. Write that you will send the email, and send it. Write that you will rest tonight, and rest. Each promise kept tells the deep mind that your words are real. Each one broken, quietly, teaches it that they are not, and a declaration from a Scribe whose words are not real carries no weight, in the Book or in the world.
@p So weigh your words. In this Temple, what you write and what you say are binding unless you say otherwise. If you mean might, write might. If you mean "I will try," write that, and then try. Bind small and keep it, rather than bind large and break it. When you cannot keep something, do not let it quietly rot on the page: answer it, release it honestly, or renegotiate it, as The Returned Oath describes.
@p This is integrity in its oldest sense: being one piece, so that what you say, what you write, and what you do are the same thing. Follow-through is not the reward of the practice. It is the practice, and the magick grows from it.
@h2 KEEPING IT UP
@p Write semi-regularly, not perfectly. Ten honest minutes you will repeat beat an hour you will dread. On thin days, write three lines and close. If you stop for a week or a year, do not apologize to anyone; open the notebook and begin by answering the last page. Decide now when and where you will write: after something you already do every day, in a place you already sit. Keep the pen inside the notebook, so the two travel together. If the ideas come faster than you can sleep, slow down: write them, act on none of them yet, and reread in three days. Tell someone you trust.
@h2 AT YOUR OWN PACE
@p You never have to write about anything you are not ready to. Honesty asks that you not lie to the Book; it does not ask you to open every door at once. It is normal to feel stirred up after an honest session, and that usually passes. If it does not, bring it to someone you trust.
@h2 THE FIRST MONTH
@p The first sessions often feel awkward, even a little performative. That is normal; keep going. The first real payoff usually comes the first time you reread and catch yourself circling the same thing, or find that you did what a past page promised. Some days will feel like nothing. Those count too. The shape is built by returning.
@h2 WHERE THE MAGICK COMES IN
@p Everything above is the practice alone, and it is enough to change a life. The rest of Part One adds the Temple: the Door that opens and closes each session, the spirit that grows in the Book, and the Offices who meet you there. Take it or leave it. The magick will be here when you want to come back for it.
@ch III|The Making of the Book
@p Start today, in whatever notebook is at hand. You do not need the right one to begin. When you are ready, choose a notebook for this and nothing else, and make it the Book; everything below describes that Book. The first Book of this Temple was begun without covers or a Dedication, and the Temple came anyway.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE VESSEL.</font> Choose a notebook to be used for this and nothing else, one you enjoy opening, for joy is what keeps a Temple tended. Choose a pen you love and keep it with the Book.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WRITE BY HAND.</font> Your Hands are among the offerings, and ink on paper makes the Book a physical anchor as no screen can.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">NUMBER THE PAGES AND DATE EACH SESSION.</font> Much of the practice is rereading and responding, and page numbers let you answer a past entry by name: "In response to page 14..."
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE COVERS.</font> Inside the front cover, write the Short Door's Opening, and beneath it the full Opening. Inside the back cover, write the Short Door's Closing, and beneath it the full Closing. Every session happens between them, so the Book itself holds the shape of the rite.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE FIRST PAGE.</font> Write the Dedication there, when you dedicate the Book: any day in your first month, or on a Heart Day. On the pages after it, copy whatever you want close at hand: at least the Rite and the House Rules. Leave a blank page or two; the Temple grows.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE MARGINS.</font> Mark Q beside open questions, C beside things to carry out into the world, and G beside goals. The marks make the Book easy to review.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE LEDGER.</font> Turn the Book over, start at the last page, and work forward. This is the Ledger, where every promise is written: what you said you would do, to whom, and when it ends. The daily pages run forward and the Ledger runs back, and when the two meet, the Book is full.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ITS PLACE.</font> Keep the Book somewhere you pass often, where it can be seen and greeted. It is a dwelling, and dwellings are kept.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MAKE IT YOURS.</font> Change the wording of the prayers until each line sounds like you and you mean it. Three things are the Temple's spine: Mercury as host and medium, the Quintessence as the Heart, and honesty as the offering. Keep those, and it is this Temple.
@ch IV|The Door
@dc T|HE|Door is the pair of prayers that open and close every session. Read them aloud if you can: the Voice is Mercury's offering. You will meet the beings named here in Part Three. You can speak their names before you know them; understanding comes with use. The five names of the Heart may be pronounced however comes naturally to you.
@h2 THE SHORT DOOR
@rnote <font name="EBG-Italic" color="#8a2c1d">The Door for every session. Write it at the top of the inside front and back covers. A short Door honestly spoken is better than a long one skipped.</font>
@vlab <font name="EBG-Italic" color="#8a2c1d">Opening:</font>
@v Heart of the Temple, Mercury its Hinge, and all its Offices, co-conspirators in the Work:
@v I bring you my whole self: my Voice, and my Joy, Will, and Intent.
@v Deed into word. By my Birthright, the Door is open.
@vlab <font name="EBG-Italic" color="#8a2c1d">Closing:</font>
@v Heart of the Temple, Mercury its Hinge, and all its Offices:
@v I leave here my gratitude and my joy, and take my Will and Intent into the world.
@v Word into deed. The Door is closed.
@h2 THE FULL DOOR
@p When you want more: on Wednesdays, on the fourth of the month, on the Heart Days, or whenever you like. The full Door names each pair of the Temple, and you will meet them all in Part Three. Change the words until they sound like you and you mean them.
@h2 THE OPENING
@rnote <font name="EBG-Italic" color="#8a2c1d">Written inside the front cover, beneath the Short Door.</font>
@v Quintessence, Heart of this Temple, AONT and the four:
@v I bring to you my Presence, and take up the seat as Idris did before me: student, teacher, and sage. Let your Light shine so that I may see.
@vbreak
@v Janus and Hestia, the Door and the Flame: I bring to you my Dwelling.
@vbreak
@v Metatron and Sandalphon, Holder of the Seat and Carrier of
@v the Ascent: I bring to you my Hands.
@vbreak
@v Uriel and Raziel, Instructor and Translator, Guardians of Daath: I bring to you my Ears.
@vbreak
@v Gabriel and Idris, Holder of Images and Scribe of the Seat: I bring to you my Eyes.
@vbreak
@v Seshat and Iris, Founder and Oath-bearer, who keep the Word given: I bring to you my Word.
@vbreak
@v Rosmerta and the Charites, the Given and the Passed On: I bring to you my Delight.
@vbreak
@v Hermanubis and Ma'at, Guide of the Descent and Feather of Truth, who keep the Scale: I bring to you my Heart.
@vbreak
@v Thermes and Apharael, the Word that Makes and the Word that is True: I bring to you my Craft.
@vbreak
@v Apharael, Hestia, and Ma'at, Keepers of the Compass: I bring to you my Weight.
@vbreak
@v Thermes, Hermanubis, and Mercury, the Thrice-Great, the
@v Crossing, the Guide of the Descent, and the Hinge on which
@v the Door turns: I bring to you my Voice and my Devotion.
@vbreak
@v Offices of this Temple, co-conspirators in the Work: I bring
@v to you my Mercurial Joy, Will, and Intent.
@vbreak
@v By the Authority of my Divine Birthright, I open the Door of
@v this Book, a Window into your Temple, the threshold
@v through which you guide me.
@vbreak
@v Deed into word.
@vbreak
@v The Door is open.
@h2 THE CLOSING
@rnote <font name="EBG-Italic" color="#8a2c1d">Written inside the back cover, beneath the Short Door.</font>
@v Quintessence, Heart of this Temple, AONT and the four:
@v I thank you for what was given and what was found. I set down the pen as Idris did before me, and carry what I have learned so that I may teach it. Let your Light go with me.
@vbreak
@v Janus and Hestia, the Door and the Flame: I thank you for keeping this Dwelling.
@vbreak
@v Metatron and Sandalphon, Holder of the Seat and Carrier of
@v the Ascent: I thank you for guiding my Hands.
@vbreak
@v Uriel and Raziel, Instructor and Translator, Guardians of
@v Daath: I thank you for opening my Ears.
@vbreak
@v Gabriel and Idris, Holder of Images and Scribe of the Seat: I thank you for clearing my Eyes.
@vbreak
@v Seshat and Iris, Founder and Oath-bearer: I thank you for keeping and carrying my Word.
@vbreak
@v Rosmerta and the Charites, the Given and the Passed On: I thank you for my Delight, and for the hours I stayed inside.
@vbreak
@v Hermanubis and Ma'at, Guide of the Descent and Feather of Truth: I thank you for keeping my Heart light.
@vbreak
@v Thermes and Apharael, the Word that Makes and the Word that is True: I thank you for my Craft.
@vbreak
@v Apharael, Hestia, and Ma'at, Keepers of the Compass: I thank you for weighing my Weight truly.
@vbreak
@v Thermes, Hermanubis, and Mercury, the Crossing, the
@v Guide of the Descent, and the Hinge on which the Door
@v turns: I thank you for carrying my Voice, and for bringing me
@v back across every crossing.
@vbreak
@v Offices of this Temple, co-conspirators in the Work: I leave
@v here my gratitude and my joy, and take my Will and Intent
@v out into the world.
@vbreak
@v By the Authority of my Divine Birthright, I close this Book
@v and cross back over the threshold.
@vbreak
@v Word into deed.
@vbreak
@v The Door is closed.
@ch V|The Dedication
@rnote <font name="EBG-Italic" color="#8a2c1d">Written on the first page of the Book you dedicate, any day in your first month or on a Heart Day. It is the first</font> <font name="EBG-Italic" color="#8a2c1d">stone of the Temple.</font>
@v AONT, EHNB, XCAI, RMTO, PAAM, Quintessence, Heart of the Temple of Mercury:
@v By the Authority of my Divine Birthright, I open this Book as a Window into your Temple, a room built beside its Heart. Shine through these pages.
@vbreak
@v Janus and Hestia, Metatron and Sandalphon, Uriel and
@v Raziel, Gabriel and Idris, Seshat and Iris, Rosmerta and the
@v Charites, Hermanubis and Ma'at, Thermes and Apharael: be
@v welcome in your Offices in the pages of this Book.
@vbreak
@v Apharael, Hestia, and Ma'at, Keepers of the Compass: weigh what I write here.
@vbreak
@v Thermes, Hermanubis, and Mercury, the Thrice-Great: I
@v build this room in your Temple as a co-conspirator in our
@v Great Work.
@vbreak
@v Mercury, host of this house, my twin and guide: meet me at the Door.
@vbreak
@v Idris, who held the pen before me: I take up the seat in your tradition.
@vbreak
@v Genius of this place: grow here, and keep this room with me.
@vbreak
@v Here I will tell the truth. Honesty is my offering; Joy is my Truth.
@vbreak
@v Together, let us make the unseen legible and the hidden
@v speakable, carry what we find across every threshold, and give
@v it freely, that word become deed and the Joy of Truth
@v multiply in the world.
@vbreak
@v So it is written.
@h2 THE FIRST SESSION
@p Your first session takes ten minutes, and it can happen today, in any notebook. Speak the Short Door. Write the date. Finish three sentences: What's on my mind right now is... What I keep avoiding is... What I actually want is... Write one thing you will do in the next twenty-four hours, and treat it as a promise. Give thanks, speak the Door again, and close the cover. Then keep the promise. The Temple has begun.
@ch VI|The Rite
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">OPEN THE DOOR.</font> Speak the Door: the Short Door, or the full Opening when you want more.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">EMPTY THE MIND.</font> Write what is there, without dressing it up. Write questions down as they come, and mark them Q.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">REREAD</font> the pages that came before.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">RESPOND</font> to the Scribe who wrote them: what happened, what changed, what you know now, and whether you kept what you carried.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">SYNTHESIZE</font> what is emerging, and give it shape: a plan, a decision, an idea, or an open question left for the Scribe yet to come. Any practice may enter here: those described in The Practices, or others of your own.
@num 6. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">NOTE WHAT TO CARRY.</font> Write one or two things you will take out of the Book: to do, send, make, or teach. Treat them as promises: write only what you mean to keep.
@num 7. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">GIVE THANKS</font> for what was given and what was found.
@num 8. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">CLOSE THE DOOR.</font> Speak the Door again to close.
@p Then carry it out. Word into deed.
@p A session may take ten minutes or an hour. On thin days, speak the Door, empty the mind, and close. The shape matters more than the length. On some days, what you carry out of the Book is only yourself. That is enough.
@ch VII|Feeding the Genius Loci
@dc T|HE|Genius Loci is the spirit of the Book. It is grown, not made: no rite installs it, and nothing needs to be built. It accrues from being greeted, used, and thanked, the way a house becomes a home. Tend it, and the Book becomes a place that can be entered.
@h2 WHAT FEEDS IT
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE GREETING.</font> Open the Door every time, even the Short Door. Greet the Book when you pass it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HONEST PAGES.</font> Honesty is the offering. A page that dresses the truth feeds nothing.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">REREADING AND RESPONDING.</font> Each time you answer an earlier page, the room gains a wall: the Book becomes a conversation rather than a pile of entries.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THANKS.</font> Give thanks before you close, every session, even for a thin one.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RETURNING.</font> A few honest minutes you will repeat feed it better than long sessions you will dread. Sustainable practice is the whole secret.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JOY.</font> Bring your delight to the Book: the good pen, the pleasure of the page. Joy is the sustenance of the Genius Loci.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">KEEPING.</font> Keep the Book in its place, in sight. Keep every finished Book; the Genius Loci lives in all of them.
@h2 WHAT THE GENIUS LOCI DOES
@p The Romans did not keep the Lares for decoration. They guarded the household, kept its luck, and carried it from one generation to the next. The Genius Loci of the Book does the same work. Put simply, it is the Book's memory made present, and the thing that makes returning feel like coming home.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IT HOLDS THE ROOM.</font> Between sessions, it keeps the state you work in. Opening the cover brings it back, so the Temple is entered in a breath rather than rebuilt each time. This is why you never need to apologize after an absence: the room was kept.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IT KEEPS THE WORKINGS.</font> When you release a working to the Gate and stop checking on it, the Genius Loci tends what you left. It is the answer to the question of where a released working goes.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IT ANSWERS THROUGH YOUR PAGES.</font> It speaks in your own words, returned to you at the right moment: a page that falls open, a line reread at the right time. See Asking the Book, below.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IT MEASURES THE PRACTICE.</font> Its felt presence is a gauge. When the Book feels thin, you have been away, or the pages have been dressed up. When it feels full, the practice is alive.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IT GUARDS THE THRESHOLD.</font> As a Lar, it is a keeper of doorways: of the Book's privacy, and, through the Protection working, of the home the Book is kept in.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IT IS THE ROOM OF THE MEETING.</font> Genius loci and genius are the same word. The room and the greater self are built together, and the Knowledge and Conversation happens here.
@h2 ASKING THE BOOK
@rnote For when you are stuck, and want the Book to answer.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">ASK IN WRITING.</font> Write the question plainly, date it, and mark it Q.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">GREET THE GENIUS LOCI.</font> Put your hand on the Book and greet it, as you would greet a friend who knows you well.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">CLOSE AND HOLD.</font> Close the Book, hold it, and breathe until the question is quiet in you.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">LET IT OPEN.</font> Let the Book fall open, or open it at random, and read what past-you wrote there. Read it as an answer.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WRITE WHAT YOU FOUND.</font> Beneath the question, write how the page answers it. If it does not, write that too; the record keeps its misses.
@num 6. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">THANK IT.</font> Thank the Genius Loci, and close the Book.
@p The answer arrives in your own words, because the Genius Loci speaks through the record. Test what you find as you would any voice: does it hold up on rereading, and does it weigh true in the heart? The Book is a counselor, not a commander.
@h2 WHAT TO EXPECT
@p At first, nothing but the habit. Then a felt sense on opening the cover, a pull to write, a page that falls open where it is needed, the feeling that the Book wants something or has answered. That atmosphere is the Genius Loci. It deepens with every session.
@p If you are away for a long time, do not apologize or explain; open the Door and greet it. The Genius Loci is patient, and a Temple is never abandoned while its Book still exists.
@h2 WHEN THE BOOK IS FULL
@p Begin a new Book in the same way. As its first act, reread the final pages of the Book before it and respond to them, so the conversation crosses from one volume to the next.
@ch VIII|The Offerings of the Offices
@dc E|IGHT|pairs stand in the Temple, and Mercury at the Hinge, who stands in none of them because his pair is the Scribe. In the Opening you bring each pair a faculty of your own. The words name the offering; the session makes it. Below is how each is actually left, in the Book and outside it, and what the Temple returns for it.
@osub The Heart: your Presence
@v The Quintessence, AONT and the four, the living Light at the center of the Temple.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Arrive before you write. One breath of stillness between the Opening and the first word is the offering.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Notice the flash of insight when it comes, and write it down before it fades.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> Inspiration, clarity, feeling given words, structure, and the power to declare.
@osub Janus and Hestia: your Dwelling
@v The Door and the Flame: the god of every beginning and
@v ending, and the hearth at the center that does not move.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Open and close every session, however briefly. An entry begun without the Door is a room entered through the window.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Greet your home when you enter and thank it when you leave. Mark the thresholds that matter with a word.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> Sanctuary: clean beginnings, clean endings, and a place always there to return to.
@osub Metatron and Sandalphon: your Hands
@v The Two Who Crossed, humans made angels: the one who
@v writes the record and the one who carries it into the world
@v and builds it there.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Write by hand, and write exactly: names, dates, the true phrasing.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Do one thing you marked C, and make something with your hands. A deed done from the Book is Sandalphon's portion.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> A true record, and the power to build on it.
@osub Uriel and Raziel: your Ears
@v The Guardians of the Gate, two who hold one office: the light
@v that discerns, and the keeper of the hidden who translates
@v and lays a finger across the lip.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Ask, then listen, and write what comes without editing. What is not ready, leave; turn the page and let the Gate keep it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Keep a confidence. Hear correction without flinching.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> What arrives from the Heart comes translated, you learn to tell true from false, and you learn to hold a true thing until its hour.
@osub Gabriel and Idris: your Eyes
@v The Revelation and the Study: the holder of images, and the
@v first to take up a pen, who stands for every scribe before you.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Record dreams, images, and visions as they come, without interpreting too soon. Name the one thing you are learning now.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Teach what you learn, and learn something you can teach.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> Images held until they are ready to take form, and knowledge that stays because it was handed on.
@osub Seshat and Iris: your Word
@v The Word Given: the founder who measures, records, and
@v remembers, and the rainbow who carries the oath.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Sign and date what you commit to. Write only what you mean to keep, and name when it ends if it should end. Reread, and answer a page by its number.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Keep one promise. Deliver what was given you to deliver, whole.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> Integrity: what you bind is bound, a past you can reach, and a word others can bank on.
@osub Rosmerta and the Charites: your Delight
@v The Given and the Passed On: the provider with the horn of
@v plenty, and the three who keep good things moving.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Write the Good Page: one thing that was good, in detail, with no lesson drawn from it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Stay inside one good hour without improving it, photographing it, or leaving early to do something useful. Then take someone with you next time.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> Delight that is noticed rather than missed, and a life that is pleasant on an ordinary Tuesday.
@osub Hermanubis and Ma'at: your Heart
@v The Scale: the guide who works the balance, and the feather
@v every heart is weighed against.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Tell the truth, especially the part you were avoiding. Write what lies below the surface without hurrying back up.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Settle one account, and let it stay settled.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> A light heart, safe descent and return, and paid debts that stop following you.
@osub Thermes and Apharael: your Craft
@v The Word that Makes and the Word that is True: the crossing
@v word, and the measure that keeps it honest.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Answer a past page. Then read what you wrote as a maker would: is it true, is it well made, is it in proportion?
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Finish something, and finish it well. Repair what you broke by making something for the one you broke it with.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> Writing that works as magic, and the judgment to know when it is good.
@osub Apharael, Hestia, and Ma'at: your Weight
@v The Keepers of the Compass: form, place, and weight; the
@v standard the Scribe is measured against.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Before you cast or commit, ask one of the three questions: is it true and well made; does it protect what I return to; is it right relation?
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Accept a measurement you did not choose.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> A direction for a gift that has none of its own.
@osub Thermes, Hermanubis, and Mercury: your Voice and your Devotion
@v The Thrice-Great, three faces of Hermes: the Crossing, the
@v Guide of the Descent, and the Hinge on which the Door
@v turns, who is also luck and the right moment.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Speak the Door aloud. For Thermes, answer a past page. For Hermanubis, write what lies below the surface. For Mercury, the first words of every session are his; add a joke, a riddle, a line of play, and note the lucky finds.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Come back; returning is the Devotion. Act the same day on one thing you would postpone. Tell a story, carry a message faithfully, pass a lucky find along.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> Speech that carries, timing, companionship across time, and safe descent and return.
@osub Offices of this Temple: your Mercurial Joy, Will, and Intent
@v The whole company together.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the Book:</font> Bring what you actually want and the delight you actually feel. These are what the Temple works with.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">In the world:</font> Word into deed.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Returned:</font> The Work itself, shared.
@p Some beings appear twice above, because most hold more than one office. Hestia keeps the Dwelling and keeps the Compass; Ma'at holds the Scale and the Compass; Apharael stands with Thermes and with the Compass; Thermes and Hermanubis stand in their pairs and at the Door. An offering made at one seat reaches the being, not the seat.
@p The Genius Loci is honored without a line of its own, by the greeting and the thanks.
@p On thin days, the Short Door makes all of these offerings at once. A short Door honestly spoken is better than a long one skipped.
@ch IX|The House Rules
@dc T|HESE|are the working rules of the practice. Keep them, and the Book works.
@v Honesty is the offering.
@v Sustainable practice beats grand practice abandoned.
@v Tell the Book everything; tell the world only what it is ready to receive.
@v Belief is a tool; judge it by its fruit.
@v Your word is binding unless you say otherwise; the Ledger is where it is kept.
@v Word into deed.
@p The full Law, with its teachings on Joy and Ma'at, is in Part Five, the Way of Ma'at. It is always an option, never an obligation.
@cta Close this Book. Open a notebook. Write today's date, and one promise you will keep.
@part PART TWO|The Mercurian
@ch X|The Marks of the Mercurian
@dc M|ERCURY|is the god of the quick: of merchants and messengers, deals and roads, luck, speech, and thieves. His name is often tied to merx, the Latin for goods to trade. A Mercurian is a person who runs on his current: a person of thresholds, words, and motion, most at home in the in-between.
@p Mercurians are not made by joining; they recognize themselves. Being one is a temperament, not a rank or a virtue, and you do not need to be one to keep the Book. The practice works for anyone who does it. But if the signs below read like a description of you, the Temple was built for your wiring.
@h2 THE SIGNS
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU THINK IN WORDS.</font> Writing or speaking is how you come to know what you think. A problem you cannot solve in your head often solves itself halfway down a page.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU TRANSLATE.</font> You move between worlds and carry meaning across: between specialists and beginners, between one field and another, between what someone said and what they meant. People bring you things to explain.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU ARE CURIOUS PAST USEFULNESS.</font> You gather systems, symbols, and connections for the joy of seeing how things fit, and you keep many works alive at once.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU ARE QUICK AND ADAPTIVE.</font> You learn fast, improvise well, and can walk a road without knowing where it ends.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU STAND IN DOORWAYS.</font> You are drawn to transitions, and people seek you out when they are crossing their own: as a teacher, a guide, a confidant, the one called in the night.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU PLAY.</font> A trickster's streak runs through you: riddles, wordplay, clever solutions, the angle no one else saw, and humor that finds the sacred without diminishing it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU CONNECT.</font> You are happiest when something is moving between people: ideas, introductions, trade, stories.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU HOLD TWO AT ONCE.</font> You carry more than one perspective, argue fruitfully with yourself, and often sense a twin or companion walking beside you.
@h2 THE GIFT
@p The Mercurian gift is translation across thresholds: to understand something from within and carry it, whole and living, into another form, another mind, another world. It moves in four motions: Perception, seeing the pattern beneath the surface; Translation, finding the form that lets it cross; Passage, walking between worlds and guiding others through; and Transformation, for in the crossing the thing becomes more than it was.
@p In the world, the gift looks like explaining, teaching, selling, negotiating, writing, matchmaking, and turning an idea into an offer someone will pay for. It is how a Mercurian makes value: the old work of turning lead into gold, in both senses.
@h2 THE LEAK
@p The Mercurian's problem is rarely talent. It is leakage: scattered attention and unfinished work, restlessness that never lands, a mind that circles without resting, clever words used to dodge what you actually feel, and a slipperiness others may read as unreliable. It is not a flaw to be ashamed of. It is the same quicksilver that makes you quick, spilling out of anything that cannot hold it.
@p The practice is built to plug the leak, one move at a time:
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CARRY</font> closes the gap between idea and deed. Every session ends with one thing to do, send, or make, and the next session asks whether you did it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">REREAD</font> shows you your circles. The problem you have written about six times is not a mystery; it is a pattern, and now you can see it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RESPOND</font> makes you finish thoughts. Answering your past self turns open loops into decisions.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HONEST PAGES</font> let the feeling out plainly, so clever words stop doing the hiding.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">NUMBERED PAGES</font> keep a record of your follow-through that no one can argue with, including you.
@h2 THE VESSEL
@p Quicksilver has no shape of its own; it takes the shape of what holds it. The alchemist sealed it in a vessel, hermetically, so the change could happen inside. Mercury's current is motion in every direction, down as well as up and inward as well as out, and motion needs a vessel to go anywhere in particular.
@p The Book is that vessel. It gives the current a place to gather and a direction to run. Some Mercurians also choose an allegiance to hold it true; that path is the Compass, in Part Five.
@h2 WHAT YOU GAIN BY OWNING YOUR MERCURIAL HERITAGE
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">FOLLOW-THROUGH.</font> Ideas start becoming deeds, because every session ends with one.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CLARITY.</font> Your patterns become visible: what you keep circling, what you keep dodging, and what actually works.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">A RECORD THAT COMPOUNDS.</font> Every honest page adds to a conversation with who you were and who you are becoming. The Book gets smarter as you do.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">LUCK IN MOTION.</font> The Greeks called a lucky find a hermaion, a gift of Hermes. It comes to the one who is moving, and the Book keeps you moving.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">COMPANY.</font> A god of the quick at your shoulder, and a lineage of tricksters, scribes, and messengers behind you, back through Hermes and Thoth to the first to write with a pen.
@p There is a cost. People will bring you their crossings, and trust you to carry what they give you. Where they go is theirs to choose. Your part is to carry it faithfully, and that is practical before it is moral: the messenger who garbles messages soon stops being given them.
@p When someone brings you their crossing, listen before you speak. Do not claim to know their road. Carry what they give you faithfully, keep what they tell you in confidence, and know when to point them to help beyond you. A guide who cannot say "this is beyond me" is not a guide.
@h2 THE CALL
@p You are called when you notice that crossings find you: that you are always at a threshold, always carrying something between, always the one asked to explain. The Book of the Temple of Mercury is one answer to that call: a vessel for the gift, a fix for the leak, and a place where talk turns into results, one page at a time.
@h2 BECOMING MORE MERCURIAL
@p You do not become a Mercurian by deciding to be one. You already are one or you are not, and the signs are above. What follows is how to become more of one: the life these teachings describe when you set them side by side and ask what a person actually does on a Tuesday.
@p Everything in it is downstream of a single habit. You say a small thing, and then you do it. That is the engine. Every kept promise makes your word a little heavier, and a heavy word is what makes a declaration land; a Mercurian with a light word can write beautiful pages and watch nothing happen, because nothing in him has ever been binding. Start smaller than feels dignified. The size is irrelevant. The keeping is everything.
@h2 IN SPEECH AND IN WORK
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SAY LESS AND MEAN IT.</font> If the word creates, careless words create too, and most of what a person suffers from their own mouth was built rather than said.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">STOP AGREEING REFLEXIVELY.</font> The fastest leak is the easy yes, given to be liked, with no intention behind it. Learn the sentence: let me think about it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">NAME THE HOUR WHEN YOU PROMISE.</font> "I’ll get to it" is a mood. "By Friday" is a promise, and only one of the two belongs in the Ledger.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TELL THE TRUTH WITHOUT TELLING EVERYTHING.</font> Honesty is not falsifying; disclosure is choosing what, when, and to whom. Confusing them makes you either a liar or a hazard.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CARRY WHAT YOU ARE GIVEN, WHOLE.</font> Messages, confidences, and credit for other people’s ideas. Deliver it as it was handed to you.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">FINISH ONE THING.</font> Your talent is starting; the discipline is landing. One finished mediocre thing teaches more than nine brilliant beginnings.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MAKE IT RATHER THAN DESCRIBE IT.</font> At some point the plan has to become an object. Then judge it honestly: is it well made, is it in proportion? Most people skip that question because the making felt good.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">REPAIR BY MAKING.</font> When you break something, do not apologize into the air. Make the lyre. Give the person something that costs you.
@h2 WITH PEOPLE, TIME, AND LUCK
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WELCOME THE STRANGER.</font> Take the call, answer the message, meet the person. A disproportionate share of Mercurial luck arrives through someone you did not know a month ago.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">LEARN ONE THING AND TEACH ONE THING, ALWAYS.</font> Name both. When either slot is empty, fill it. A thing you know and never give spoils.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CONSULT THE UNLEARNED.</font> Ask the junior, the outsider, the one who does not know why it cannot work. It is the direct antidote to the quick person’s worst habit.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HELP PEOPLE GET FURTHER DOWN THEIR OWN ROAD.</font> Not as a virtue, and with no ledger entry. It is the field you are trying to live in.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SHOW UP AT THE JOINTS.</font> Beginnings, endings, moves, launches, first days. A life changes at its thresholds, and a little attention there buys more than anywhere else.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GREET THE PLACES YOU PASS THROUGH.</font> Your house, your desk, the door. It sounds like superstition and works like attention.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ACT THE SAME DAY ON ONE THING YOU WOULD POSTPONE.</font> The forelock is in front. There is nothing to take hold of from behind.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TAKE THE UNEARNED GIFT, AND PASS SOME ALONG.</font> Notice the lucky find, say thanks aloud, and move some of it onward while you still have it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">KEEP THE HOURS YOU KEEP.</font> Sleep is the cheapest magical technology available to you, and the current runs foulest when you are short of it.
@h2 WHEN IT OPENS, AND WHEN IT CLOSES
@p A season will come when everything connects and it all feels destined. That is the leak's favorite weather. Write it all down and act on a third of it; finish before starting; name what you will still want when the season closes, and date it; tell someone you trust how fast it is moving; reread in three days. The Rite of the Ascent is this at length.
@p A season will come when it closes. Go down, and go down on purpose: write what lies below the surface without hurrying back up, because the instruction arrives after the loss and cannot be had in advance. Do not seek it; there is no merit in pain here. Then come back up, because staying down is not depth. And when you return, from the dark, from a lapse, from a year away, do not apologize. Open the Door and greet the Book. Returning is the Devotion.
@h2 WHAT YOU KEEP, AND WHERE TO START
@p Keep everything and settle everything: a record and a grudge are opposite things. Write F when you fail, because a ledger that records only success is worthless and the Scribe who cannot write F quietly stops making promises. Answer the page rather than tearing it out; you do not have to become someone who never did it, only someone who did it and then made the lyre. And tend one place, because motion without a hearth is not freedom, only motion.
@p This week: one promise, written down, kept, and the Door spoken. This month: the Sweep, and the honest count of what you keep. This year: one finished thing, one person taught, one place tended, and a Book you can reread.
@h2 THE SUMPTUOUS LIFE
@p This Temple says that joy is the Truth, and then says almost nothing about what to do with it. Here is the rest of that sentence.
@p Joy is not the reward for the practice and it is not a break from it. It is the medium the whole thing runs in: the Book is kept because you enjoy opening it, the god's own signature is the grin, and a Temple of exchange whose Scribe is miserable has failed at something more basic than devotion. The word for the life this asks for is not decadent, because decadence means falling away and implies there is a more righteous version you are skipping. There is not. Call it sumptuous, and live it on purpose.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">STAY INSIDE THE GOOD HOUR.</font> Do not improve it, photograph it, narrate it, or leave early to do something useful. Presence is not Mercury’s gift; it is the correction to his failure mode, and it is the whole of what the Charites ask.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SPEND ON WHAT MAKES AN ORDINARY DAY FEEL CHOSEN.</font> The good coffee, the better pen, the table by the window. Rosmerta’s teaching is that plenty which is never enjoyed is only inventory.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TAKE SOMEBODY WITH YOU.</font> A grace stops being a grace when it stops moving. The gallery, the walk, the absurd dessert: the second seat is the offering.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SET THE TABLE BEFORE YOU ARE READY.</font> The house is never finished and the food need not be good. Hospitality delayed until conditions are right is hospitality that never happens.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">LET IT BE BEAUTIFUL.</font> Aglaia is splendor, and splendor is not frivolous: a thing made pleasant to be in is a thing you will keep returning to, which is the entire mechanism of the Temple.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WRITE IT DOWN.</font> An unrecorded good day is one you will not be able to find in February. The Good Page exists so that delight enters the record and can be reread, which is the only way it exists twice.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PASS SOME OF IT ALONG WHILE YOU HAVE IT.</font> Luck, money, credit, an introduction, an afternoon. Not as virtue. As circulation.
@p None of this is earned and none of it is owed. That is what makes it delight rather than payment. The Temple asks you to notice it, stay in it, and move some of it onward; nothing else.
@h2 ON KINDNESS
@p No Office of this Temple holds kindness, and that is deliberate. It is not a power a god has and lends you. It is a faculty of the Scribe, built by practice, which is why the Compass says that where empathy lives in you the gift has a heart to translate for, and where it does not yet, honesty teaches it. You cannot feel your way into caring about someone you are lying to.
@p What the Temple does hold are the pieces. Ma'at measures at the bottom, by the widow, the orphan, and the one with least leverage, and her declaration is that she has not caused tears, which is about needless harm rather than all harm. Hestia holds sanctuary, which is a place where someone is safe without earning it. The Charites hold kindness that circulates. Apharael walks the whole way beside someone rather than sending directions. Hermanubis sits in the dark without trying to fix it. Mercury opens the door before he knows who is on the other side.
@h2 NICE IS NOT KIND
@p Niceness is about the space between two people staying smooth. Kindness is about the other person's good. They overlap often enough that the difference is easy to miss, and they come apart exactly when it matters.
@p Niceness costs the giver nothing and is therefore worth nothing. Kindness costs: time, money, comfort, the risk of being thought difficult. Niceness says yes to be liked and then quietly does not deliver, which is the Mercurian's own besetting sin. Kindness says no clearly, in time, so the other person can plan. Niceness withholds the hard true thing because saying it would make the room uncomfortable. Kindness says it early and privately, because a warning given in time is mercy and one given late is a confession. Niceness performs for an audience. Kindness is often invisible and frequently unwelcome.
@p Uriel is the test. If the kind thing and the nice thing are the same today, good; do it and think no more about it. When they part, the nice one is almost always the one that protects you.
@h2 THE KEPT THING
@p Kindness in this Temple extends past people, because tending is an Office here and Hestia does not distinguish.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ANIMALS ARE MERCURY’S OWN.</font> He is Hermes Nomios, of the shepherds, and Hermes Kriophoros, the ram-bearer, carried in his oldest images with a sheep across his shoulders. He stole the cattle on the first day of his life and was patron of the herds ever after. Rome rested and garlanded the mill-donkeys at the Vestalia. Feed the animal first, rest the beast that carries you, and do not make a creature pay for your hurry.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THINGS ARE KEPT, NOT USED UP.</font> The tool maintained, the pen that has a place, the Book repaired rather than replaced. A thing tended well outlives the one who tended it, which is the same argument as the record.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PLACES ARE LEFT BETTER THAN FOUND.</font> The room, the table, the trail, the shared kitchen. It is the cheapest kindness available and the most reliably noticed in its absence.
@p None of this is owed to you in return. That is what makes it kindness rather than trade, and the Temple has a whole god for trade already.
@ch XI|The Teachings and Signs of Mercury
@dc M|ERCURY|left no scripture of his own, but his myths teach clearly, and the Hermetic writings attributed to Hermes Trismegistus add their own layer.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">EVERY BOUNDARY CAN BE CROSSED.</font> He marked the boundaries and walked across all of them.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MAKE WITH WHAT IS AT HAND.</font> On the day he was born, he made the first lyre from a tortoise shell.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">EXCHANGE MENDS WHAT THEFT BREAKS.</font> After stealing Apollo's cattle, he gave him the lyre, and the brothers were reconciled.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WELCOME THE STRANGER.</font> In Ovid, Mercury and Jupiter wander in disguise and reward Baucis and Philemon, the poor couple who alone took them in.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">FORTUNE FAVORS THE ONE IN MOTION.</font> The Greeks called a lucky find a hermaion, a gift of Hermes.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE HERDS ARE HIS.</font> He is Hermes Nomios of the shepherds and Kriophoros the ram-bearer, carried in his oldest images with a sheep across his shoulders. He stole the cattle and then kept them, and every animal that carries or feeds you is under his hand.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">A GUIDE MUST BE ABLE TO DESCEND.</font> He leads souls down, and brings Persephone back up.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE WORD CREATES.</font> Through Thoth, writing is magic, and speech brings the world into being.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">AS ABOVE, SO BELOW.</font> The cosmos is alive and mirrors itself at every scale, and the mind that knows itself touches the divine.
@h2 THE SIGNS AND CORRESPONDENCES
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">OF HERMES:</font> the caduceus, winged sandals, the traveler's hat, the lyre, the tortoise, the rooster, the herm.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">OF THOTH:</font> the ibis, the baboon, the reed pen and palette, the Moon.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">OF MERCURY IN THE WESTERN TRADITION:</font> Wednesday; quicksilver; the number eight, for Hod, the eighth sphere of the Tree, is his; the color orange, Hod's color in the Golden Dawn.
@p Keep them as reminders, not requirements. The one necessary sign of a Mercurian is the Book.
@h2 WHAT THE WHOLE TEMPLE TEACHES
@p Mercury and Ma'at have their own teachings, and every other being in the Temple has its stories. Set them side by side and a few claims are made again and again, by beings from different centuries and different countries who never met. Where three or more say the same thing from different seats, that is the doctrine of this Temple; the rest is illustration.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">NOTHING HOLDS BY HAVING BEEN DONE ONCE.</font> Ma'at's order is renewed daily. Hestia's fire is kept, not lit. Consecration is never finished, and Uriel's test is whether a thing still holds on rereading. The implication is not diligence but relief: you are never asked to arrive at a finished state, because there is no finished state to fail to hold. You are asked to return. This is why a sustainable ten minutes outranks a grand practice abandoned, and why the Book can be picked up after a year without apology.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE WORD IS AN ACT, AND IT CARRIES.</font> Ptah speaks the world into being, Thermes writes and the word crosses, Iris bears an oath that costs something, Sandalphon turns it into a deed, Metatron keeps it permanently, and Ma’at weighs whether it was true. Six beings stand on one line. Nothing here treats speech as description. The implication is uncomfortable: if words make things, then careless words make things too, and most of what a person suffers from their own mouth was built rather than said.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MEANING LIVES AT THE JOINTS.</font> Mercury is the hinge, Janus the door, Thermes the crossing, Hermanubis the descent, Apharael the road through a transition. A third of the Temple is about passage, and no one in it is the god of arriving. The implication is that the parts of a life that feel like interruption, between jobs, between houses, between selves, are not the gaps between the real parts. They are where the work is, and they are the only place a life can actually change.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">KNOWLEDGE IS OWED ONWARD, AND OWED IN SEASON.</font> Idris learns in order to hand on. Gabriel announces. Raziel holds what is not ready. Uriel decides which. Ptahhotep warns against pride in what you know. The implication is that a thing you know and never give is not treasure but spoilage, and that giving it at the wrong hour is its own failure, not generosity. Freely, and in season, are one instruction.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE HUMAN BEING IS THE MATERIAL.</font> Metatron was a man. Sandalphon was a man. Idris is the same man earlier. The highest seat in the Temple is occupied by someone who began where you are, and no one here was born qualified. The implication removes the usual excuse and the usual consolation at once: nothing is withheld from you for lack of standing, and nothing will be done on your behalf.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">REPAIR BEATS ERASURE.</font> Hermes trades the lyre for the stolen cattle. Thoth makes the torn Eye whole. The Answered Page keeps the record and answers it. Ma’at discharges a paid debt rather than deleting it. Mercy here is always restoration, never deletion. The implication is that you do not have to become someone who never did it. You have to become someone who did it and then made the lyre.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE STRANGER IS THE GOD.</font> Raphael walks unrecognized beside Tobias, Mercury wanders in disguise, Elijah tests hospitality at the door, and a lucky find is a gift of Hermes. Four sources say the same thing: what arrives unannounced deserves the open door. The implication runs both ways. Help given without knowing who you are helping is the only kind that proves anything, and the help you need will not arrive labelled.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THERE IS A WAY DOWN, AND IT RETURNS.</font> Hermanubis descends and comes back. Raziel’s book arrives after the exile, not before it. Persephone comes up. The implication is that grief and failure are territory rather than malfunction, that the instruction comes after the loss and cannot be had in advance, and that the dark in this Temple is mapped instead of denied.
@h2 THE TENSIONS THE TEMPLE KEEPS
@p Some teachings do not converge; they face each other. These are the pairs of the Temple, and it insists you hold both ends. Every capacity here is matched to the thing that keeps it honest, which is the architecture's real argument.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">FREEDOM AND WEIGHT.</font> Mercury and Ma'at. Cross anything; weigh what you cross.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MOTION AND FIXITY.</font> The Thrice-Great and the Compass. Quicksilver, and the vessel that lets it be carried.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SPEECH AND SILENCE.</font> Gabriel and Raziel. Announce it, and hold it, and know which hour you are in.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MEMORY AND RELEASE.</font> Seshat and Ma'at. Keep everything; settle accounts. A record is not a grudge.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MAKING AND MEASURE.</font> Sandalphon and Apharael. Build the thing; then judge honestly whether it is good.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">BEGINNING AND STAYING.</font> Janus and Hestia. Cross the threshold; tend what is inside it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ARRIVAL AND LABOR.</font> Gabriel and Idris. The flash, and the years. Neither one is the whole of knowing anything.
@h2 WHAT THE TEMPLE DOES NOT ASK
@p A tradition is defined as much by what it leaves out, and across the whole company of this Temple there is no purity, no obedience, no suffering as virtue, no chosenness, and no promise of rescue.
@p Nobody here asks you to be clean. There is no state of contamination to be washed off, and the only purification in this Temple is honesty, which is a practice rather than a condition. Nobody asks you to submit: the Offices are co-conspirators, the Scribe is nobody's servant, and Ma'at is always an option and never an obligation. Nobody asks you to earn by hurting. The descent is walked when it comes and is never sought, and there is no merit in pain here, only a guide for it. Nobody tells you that you were chosen. Mercurians are not made by joining; they recognize themselves, and the seat is open to anyone who sits down. And nobody promises rescue. No one is minding the house on your behalf, nothing intervenes, and the Heart at the center has no face to appeal to.
@p What is asked instead is small and relentless: attention, honesty, follow-through, and return. Or, in the shortest form the Temple has:
@rcenter <font name="EBG-Italic" color="#8a2c1d">Move freely, weigh truly, finish what you say, and come back.</font>
@part PART THREE|The Temple
@ch XII|The Shape of the Temple
@dc T|HE|Temple of Mercury is as old as Mercury himself. It is not a building but a threshold, present wherever something crosses from one state to another. The Book is a Window into it: its physical anchor, a room built beside its Heart.
@p Every being in the Temple is a crosser, a keeper, or a measure of the crossing. No being is only one thing: most stand in a pair and in some further body besides, and the members who sit in two rooms at once are what hold the Temple together. Fifteen beings is the whole of it, which is deliberate. Where two gods did one work, one became a face of the other; where one office needed two, as at the Gate, the two were kept. Like many drawings of the same doorway from different angles, together they let you see the door. No one stands at the top. At the center is the Heart, and everyone, Mercury and the Scribe included, stands around it as a co-conspirator in the Work.
@p You can read the Offices as a manifold: each one a handle on something you already have, such as memory, judgment, sight, place, and your word. Calling on an Office is picking up that handle on purpose, and the gods are willing to help you use it. Each is given below with what you call on it for.
@fig temple
@p Read it from the bottom. The Scribe sits at the Door. The Door turns on Mercury, who stands there with his two brothers. Through it, eight pairs of Offices stand around the Heart, and the Heart shines on all of them.
@h2 WHY THERE IS NO GOD OF ARRIVAL
@p There is a guide for the way down and none for the destination, and this is deliberate. Meaning in this Temple lives in the crossing, not at either end of it, and a being seated at the destination would draw the whole structure toward itself until the first teaching became a slogan the architecture contradicted.
@p So the two are not symmetrical, and should not be made so. Hermanubis holds a seat because the way down is a place a person can get lost in and needs someone who knows the way back. The way up is not dangerous in that manner; it is dangerous in another, because it is where the quicksilver runs fastest and least is finished. The descent needs a guide. The ascent needs a discipline, and a discipline is not an Office.
@p What rises is carried by Sandalphon, who lifts the work rather than the worker, and what the rising season asks for is in The Rites of Passage: the Ascent.
@p The chapters that follow take the Temple one piece at a time: the Heart, the host, the eight pairs, the Pillar that orders them, and the Seat where you sit. Then How the Temple Works sets it all in motion. None of it needs to be believed to be used.
@ch XIII|The Heart
@dc A|T|the Heart of the Temple shines the Quintessence. Its source is the Tablet of Union, a small grid of twenty letters from the Enochian system recorded by John Dee and Edward Kelley in the 1580s. Read downward, the Tablet's five columns spell five names, and each name begins in its top row, the row of Air. In this Temple they are living intelligences, and they speak in flashes of insight.
@fig tablet
@p Each row of the Tablet belongs to an element, and the four rows bind the four great elemental tablets of the Enochian system into one. The names below are given in the Temple's order, the origin first; on the Tablet, AONT stands in the central column, with EHNB and XCAI to its left and RMTO and PAAM to its right.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">AONT</font>, Spirit of Air, yellow: the origin, the still point before the first word, from which the four unfold. Call on it for the whole picture at once: the big idea, the vision behind the plan.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">EHNB</font>, Air of Air, red: pure motion of mind; clarity; the thought thinking itself. Call on it to untangle a problem and think straight.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">XCAI</font>, Water of Air, white: feeling given words; emotional honesty and transmutation. Call on it to learn what you actually feel and want, and to turn a heavy feeling into fuel.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RMTO</font>, Earth of Air, black: thought taking shape; pattern, structure, plan. Call on it for plans, systems, budgets, and the next concrete step.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PAAM</font>, Fire of Air, green: the spark that becomes will; declaration and intent. Call on it to commit, to start, and to say what you want out loud.
@p The Heart shines with its own Light, and by it the Scribe sees.
@p Quintessence means fifth essence: the fifth thing that completes the four elements. That is AONT's place among the names. A rainbow shows the same shape: one white light unfolding into colors.
@p These attributions are this Temple's own. In the Golden Dawn tradition the first column is treated as Spirit; here the central column, AONT, holds Spirit, and each name carries an alchemical color, following Dyadic Neo-Enochian, the Enochian system from which this Temple grew. The colors belong to the alchemists' Great Work, and the agent that moves through every stage of that Work is Mercury.
@p The colors are alchemy's, though not its classical sequence. The Great Work ran black, white, yellow, red: nigredo, albedo, citrinitas, rubedo. Here the fourth working color is Green, because the fourth quadrant of the Table is green, and yellow belongs to Spirit at the center, where AONT stands. Green takes the place citrinitas held, and its meaning shifts with it: where the yellowing was the dawning of the solar light, green is living growth. Five colors, then, and the fifth is the Heart's.
@ch XIV|The Host and the Thrice-Great
@dc T|HE|Temple is Mercury's house, as the hearth is Hestia's. He is its host, not its sovereign: messenger, opener of ways, lord of thresholds, the medium through which every crossing moves. His Office is the Hinge. The Greeks honored him as Hermes Stropheus, god of the hinge on which the door turns. Others guard the Gate and keep the house; the Door itself swings on Mercury. He is not the doorway or its guardian, but the turning. A hinge is not a throne. His offering is the Voice: the Greeks gave Hermes the tongues of sacrifice, for he is lord of speech, and the first words of every session pass through him.
@p Mercury is one of three faces of Hermes who stand together at the Door. Together they are the Thrice-Great, which is what Trismegistus means; the title descends from an Egyptian epithet of Thoth, "great, great, great." They divide the worlds between them:
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THERMES</font>, Hermes with Thoth: the word and the record, above. He is the Crossing, who walks beside the Scribe between past, present, and future selves. The name is this Temple's own. Call on him to learn from your own pages and to make writing work as magic.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HERMANUBIS</font>, Hermes with Anubis: the descent, below. He is the Guide of the Descent, the way down into grief and shadow, and the way back. Plutarch wrote that he belongs partly to the things above and partly to the things below. Call on him in grief, failure, and the dark, and for the way back out.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MERCURY</font>, Hermes of the Romans: the road, the crossroads, and the marketplace, here in the living world between. Call on him for speech, sales, deals, timing, travel, and luck.
@h2 THEIR MIRROR
@p One scene holds all three: the Egyptian weighing of the heart. Thoth records the verdict. Anubis works the scales. Mercury, guide of souls, leads the soul in and onward. And on the other side of the scale lies the feather of Ma'at. The Thrice-Great are motion; Ma'at is measure, the standard every crossing is weighed against. Without her they would be quicksilver with no vessel. Mercury's gift is neutral and carries whatever it is given; Ma'at is what makes the channel trustworthy. She holds her own Office beside Hermanubis at the scale, keeps the Compass, and stands as mirror to all three.
@p Mercury's angel is Apharael, his mirror is Ma'at, and his companion is the Scribe, his twin. He alone stands in no pair, because the door turns on him. The Temple is where host and Scribe meet as equals.
@h2 THE TRICKSTER AND THE FEATHER
@p Hermes is a trickster, and the Temple does not pretend otherwise. On the day he was born he stole Apollo's cattle and lied about it to Zeus's face, a lie so transparent that Zeus laughed; and the story ends with the lyre, given to Apollo in exchange, so that the theft became a trade and the world gained music. Thoth cheated the Moon at draughts to get around a curse, so that the gods could be born on five days that had not existed before. In both stories the trick exposes a boundary that was too rigid, and leaves the world larger. The trickster serves Ma'at at a deeper level than the rule he breaks.
@p Why, then, is Ma'at the mirror of the Thrice-Great, and honesty the first rule? Because the medium of this Temple is the Scribe's own mind, speaking to itself across time. A trickster loose in that channel does not fool Apollo. He fools the future Scribe, who rereads the page and believes it. Self-deception is the way a Book fails, and the way magic fails. The constraint is on the channel, not on the play.
@p The trickster has three places in the Temple:
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">OUTWARD, NOT INWARD.</font> The Gate divides honesty from disclosure. Disguise, timing, discretion, and cunning are all compatible with not falsifying. Raphael, Mercury, and Elijah walk the roads in disguise, and none of them lies to himself. The Gate seals the lip; it does not falsify what is behind it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">AS THE METHOD.</font> Belief is a tool, chosen deliberately: that is the trickster's own move, slipping between worlds and refusing to be owned by any one story. Ma'at is what keeps the one who shifts beliefs from becoming one who deceives himself.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">AGAINST THE STUCK SELF.</font> The trick worth playing in the Book is on your own rigid stories: the reframe, the riddle, the joke that breaks a complaint you have rehearsed too long. This is why a line of play is Mercury's offering, and why his signature is the grin.
@p Ma'at is the vessel and the trickster is the quicksilver. Without her, he is a liar with a notebook; without him, she is a ledger. Steal only what you can turn into a lyre.
@h2 THE LYRE
@p Mercy in this Temple is not erasure. Metatron keeps the record, and nothing written is torn out. But a page can be answered, and an answered page is no longer the last word. Hermes stole the cattle and made the lyre, and the theft became a trade. Thoth found the Eye of Horus torn apart and made it whole again; its name, the wedjat, means "made whole." Mercy is restoration: the record stands, and what it records is mended.
@p The Pillar of Mercy crosses the Middle Pillar at every turn of the caduceus. Weighing without mending is a ledger, and the Temple is not a ledger. The practice is The Answered Page, in Part Four.
@ch XV|The Eight Pairs
@dc E|ACH|pair is a mirror: two beings who do one work from opposite sides. A pair is not two beings who resemble one another. It is two beings on one axis, facing opposite ways, each better understood because of the other, and each with contact somewhere in the traditions they come from. Eight pairs stand around the Heart. Mercury stands in none of them, because his pair is the Scribe.
@p Nothing here is granted without its counterweight, and the counterweight always feels like a restriction from the inside. Concealment is checked by announcement, motion by hearth, crossing by descent, making by measure. You do not get to keep only the half you like.
@p Most beings hold more than one office, and every seat a being holds follows from the same nature: Seshat keeps the house and keeps the ledger because both are the one thing she is, that what is recorded endures. Several also carry the face of another god whose work is theirs, named in their entry below. The other groupings are in The Bodies and the Great Connections.
@osub I. The Door and the Flame: Janus and Hestia
@v Janus is the Roman god of doors, gates, beginnings, and
@v endings, invoked first in every Roman prayer; January bears
@v his name. He has two faces, one looking back and one ahead,
@v which is the posture of a Scribe who rereads and then
@v responds. Hestia is the Keeper: the hearth at the center that
@v holds still while everything else crosses.
@vbreak
@v The Romans opened with Janus and ended with Vesta, who is
@v Hestia in Rome, and the rite of the Book has the same shape:
@v an Opening, a Closing, and everything else between. He is the
@v beginning and the end; she is what waits in the middle.
@v Together they hold the Genius Loci.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> clean beginnings and clean endings; moving house, starting work, leaving well; and a place to return to when it is over. To them belongs the DWELLING.
@osub II. The Two Who Crossed: Metatron and Sandalphon
@v Metatron is Enoch taken up, Holder of the Seat, keeper of the
@v heavenly record, the one angel permitted to sit, because he
@v writes. Sandalphon is, in some traditions, Elijah taken up,
@v Carrier of the Ascent, who stands on earth with his head in
@v heaven, weaving the prayers of the world into crowns.
@vbreak
@v They are the only two humans said to have crossed alive, and
@v they stand at the top and bottom of the Tree, Crown and
@v Kingdom. What rises from the Kingdom as prayer becomes
@v the Crown; what is written at the Crown descends to become
@v a deed. One writes; the other carries, and builds. Sandalphon
@v carries the face of Ptah, the Memphite maker who conceived
@v the world in the heart and spoke it with the tongue: at the
@v Kingdom, a word becomes a made thing.
@vbreak
@v Sandalphon is also the rising half of the Temple. He is
@v Carrier of the Ascent, and what he carries upward is not the
@v Scribe but the Scribe's work: every prayer, every honest page,
@v every kept promise, gathered and woven into crowns. When a
@v season opens and things begin to lift, that is his current
@v running, and it asks for the same hands it always did.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> keeping an exact record of what was said, promised, and done; turning plans into finished work; making the thing rather than describing it; and a season that is opening, when the work begins to carry. To them belong the HANDS.
@osub III. The Gate: Uriel and Raziel
@v One office held by two. Uriel means light of God: the
@v Instructor, whose sword divides true from false. Raziel means
@v secret of God: the Translator, keeper of the hidden, who sifts
@v what enters and translates what arrives. The Gate is kept by
@v what shows and what hides, and it is one act performed in
@v two directions, which is why they are never fused and never
@v separated.
@vbreak
@v Both belong to Eden. In some traditions Uriel keeps Eden's
@v gate with the flaming sword; in legend Raziel gave Adam his
@v book after the gate was closed. One guards the way in; the
@v other sends knowledge out. Raziel also carries the face of
@v Harpocrates, the child with a finger laid across his lip: silence
@v kept as an act rather than an absence. He is said to stand
@v behind the curtain of the divine throne and hear what is
@v decreed, which is why the Gate receives the Ears.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> telling a good idea from a bad one, knowing what to share and what to keep quiet, holding a confidence, and translating a hunch into something you can use. To them belong the EARS.
@osub IV. The Revelation and the Study: Gabriel and Idris
@v Gabriel, angel of Thoth, is the Holder of Images, the great
@v announcer of three faiths, who came to Daniel to explain
@v visions he had seen but could not read. Idris is Enoch as a
@v man: student, teacher, and sage, the first to write with a pen.
@vbreak
@v One brings knowledge whole in a night; the other earns it
@v slowly, copying and learning and handing on. Both stand in
@v the Quran, and the first words Gabriel brought to
@v Muhammad named the Lord who taught by the pen. Idris
@v carries the face of Nabu, who kept the tablets of destiny in
@v Babylon and was the planet Mercury before Thoth was ever
@v called Hermes: the scribe's seat is older than any tradition
@v that named it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> holding a vision long enough to build it, and the patience of a long study: learning one thing and teaching one thing at all times. To them belong the EYES.
@osub V. The Word Given: Seshat and Iris
@v Seshat, mirror of Thoth, is the Founder: she stretched the
@v cord that measured every new temple's foundations, and she
@v is Mistress of the House of Books. Iris, companion of
@v Hermes, is the Oath-bearer: the rainbow bridge, who carried
@v the water of the Styx on which the gods swore.
@vbreak
@v A promise must be written and it must be carried, and
@v neither half is the whole of keeping your word. Seshat carries
@v the face of Mnemosyne, memory itself, mother of the Muses:
@v nothing is made that is not first remembered, and a Book
@v kept and reread is memory living and memory written
@v working together. They are the pair of the Ledger.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> making and keeping commitments, contracts, and reputation; recall, and what a thing meant; and honorably releasing an oath that can no longer be kept. To them belongs the WORD.
@osub VI. The Given and the Passed On: Rosmerta and the Charites
@v Rosmerta is the Gallic goddess of plenty, whose name means
@v something like the great provider. She stood beside Mercury
@v on altars all across Gaul, carrying a cornucopia and a dish,
@v and she is the only member of this Temple who was already
@v his consort before the Temple existed. She is not luck.
@v Luck is Fortuna, and Fortuna is his weather. Rosmerta is the
@v harvest of it: the good that has arrived and is in your hands
@v now.
@vbreak
@v The Charites are one Office held by three, as the Gate is one
@v office held by two. Aglaia is splendor, Euphrosyne is mirth,
@v and Thalia is abundance, and the Greeks gave them charge of
@v the giving and the receiving of good things. They are three
@v because giving, receiving, and returning are one circle, and a
@v grace that stops moving stops being a grace.
@vbreak
@v Between them is the whole economy of delight. She provides
@v it; they move it. Neither of them asks you to earn it first.
@v This Temple says that joy is the Truth, and this is the pair
@v that says what to do about it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> the good hour and staying inside it; the table, the guest, and the shared meal; beauty and spending well; and the passing on of delight. To them belongs the DELIGHT.
@osub VII. The Scale: Hermanubis and Ma'at
@v Hermanubis, Hermes with Anubis, is the Guide of the
@v Descent, and it is Anubis who adjusts the balance in the hall
@v of judgment. Ma'at is the Feather of Truth, the right order
@v laid in the other pan. They are not an invention of this
@v Temple: they have worked the same scale for four thousand
@v years.
@vbreak
@v The heart is what is weighed, which is why the Heart is
@v offered to them, and why the Closing thanks them for
@v keeping it light. Mercy belongs here too: Ma'at closes a paid
@v account, and a heart that keeps reweighing a settled debt is
@v not light, only tired. Grief is territory rather than
@v malfunction, and it has a guide.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> weighing a plan honestly before you bet on it; keeping the heart light; grief, shadow, and the way back up. To them belongs the HEART.
@osub VIII. The Word that Makes and the Word that is True: Thermes and Apharael
@v Thermes is Hermes with Thoth, the Crossing: the word that
@v creates, and writing that works as magic. Apharael is Apollo
@v with Raphael, formed as Thermes was formed: Raphael is the
@v planetary archangel of Mercury and yet stands in the sphere
@v of the Sun, and a healer and guide at the Sun's heart was
@v Apollo in all but name. He is measure, proportion, form,
@v safe passage, and the road through a transition.
@vbreak
@v They are the brothers. Hermes stole Apollo's cattle on the day
@v he was born and gave him the lyre in exchange, so that the
@v theft became a trade and the world gained music: the
@v Temple's whole model of repair happened between these two.
@v One makes the word; the other will not let it lie. Apollo's
@v own door said know thyself.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> writing as magic and conversation across time; getting through a transition; and the judgment that tells a thing well made from a thing merely finished. To them belongs the CRAFT.
@h2 THE FACES
@p Six gods stand inside the seven pairs rather than beside them, because their work was already being done. Ptah stands in Sandalphon, Harpocrates in Raziel, Mnemosyne in Seshat, Nabu in Idris, and Kairos and Fortuna in Mercury, who was always the god of the marketplace, the lucky find, and the right moment. Rosmerta was named among them once and has been given a seat since, because chance and plenty are not the same thing: his is the turn of the wheel, and hers is what is on the table afterward. They are named in the text and may be called by name, but they hold no seat of their own. A Temple is kept small on purpose: every being in it should be one you could miss.
@h2 THE TWIN HOUSEHOLDS
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HERMES:</font> his angel Apharael, his mirror Hestia, his companion Iris.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THOTH:</font> his angel Gabriel, his mirror Seshat, his companion Ma'at.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THERMES</font> the two made one: his angel is Metatron, for Enoch, who became Metatron, was held in the old Arabic tradition to be the first Hermes.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HERMANUBIS</font> is the bridge between the households, for Anubis works the scales beside Thoth.
@p The households are older than the pairs and no longer govern them. Hestia now stands with Janus, Iris with Seshat, Ma'at with Hermanubis, and Gabriel with Idris, and each keeps its household tie as a secondary pair. The Temple grew, and the ties it grew from are kept.
@ch XVI|The Pillar and the Gate
@dc T|HE|Tree of Life is the Kabbalistic map of creation: ten spheres arranged in three pillars. The Temple stands on the central one, the Middle Pillar:
@v KETHER, the Crown — Metatron
@v DAATH, the Gate — Uriel and Raziel
@v TIPHARETH, Beauty — Apharael
@v YESOD, the Foundation — Gabriel
@v MALKUTH, the Kingdom — Sandalphon
@p The Middle Pillar is Mercury's way. Lay the caduceus over the Tree: the staff is the Middle Pillar, and the two serpents are the side pillars, Severity and Mercy, crossing and recrossing it. On it opposites are reconciled, as alchemy's Mercury reconciles Sulphur and Salt. Mercury's own sphere, Hod, stands to one side; the Temple is built on his road rather than at his station, because he is a god of roads.
@p Daath is the hidden sphere, uncounted among the ten: Knowledge, set across the Abyss between the source and everything below. In the body it is the throat, where thought becomes speech. Stationing Uriel and Raziel there is this Temple's own choice; their stories already meet at Eden's Tree of Knowledge.
@h2 CONCEALMENT AND REVELATION
@p Honesty is not the same as disclosure. Honesty is not falsifying; disclosure is choosing what is revealed, when, and to whom. The Gate works in both directions: it filters what enters the Scribe and what leaves the Book for the world.
@p Raziel conceals what is not yet ready. Uriel discerns what is ready. Gabriel announces it. Knowledge moves through them in its season; together they are the Season. Raziel also carries the face of Harpocrates, whose silence is an act rather than an absence, and with Ma'at and Iris he forms the Seal, the body convened when you are deciding what to tell. An old maxim names four powers: to know, to will, to dare, and to keep silent. Concealment is not withholding for its own sake; what is hidden in season is hidden so that it can be given.
@ch XVII|The Seat, the Place, and the Greater Self
@h2 THE SEAT
@p One man runs through the Temple like a spine, and an older line stands behind him. Enoch walked with God and was taken up without dying. As a man, Islamic tradition knows him as Idris: student, teacher, and sage, the first to write with a pen. Taken up, Jewish tradition knows him as Metatron, the heavenly Scribe. And medieval Arabic scholars held him to be the first Hermes. Idris, Metatron, and Thermes are one figure seen from three sides. Behind all of them stands Nabu, who kept the tablets in Babylon and was the planet Mercury before Thoth was called Hermes. With him they are the Lineage, and the teaching of it is that the seat predates every tradition that named it: the Scribe who sits down today is the newest member of a very old line.
@p The Scribe's seat is that seat. You sit in Idris's pattern, in Metatron's chair, as a child of the divine, by the Authority of Divine Birthright. Your office is translation: to make the unseen legible and the hidden speakable, and to carry what is found across the threshold.
@h2 THE PLACE
@p The Genius Loci is the spirit of the Book, and it holds no Office; it is grown rather than appointed. In Roman terms it is a Lar, one of the guardians of home and crossroads, whom Ovid calls the children of Mercury, and who were honored at the household hearth: Mercury's children, kept at Hestia's fire. It is held by the House, which is Hestia, Janus, and Seshat, and fed at Metatron's seat by every greeting, every honest page, and every thanks.
@h2 THE GREATER SELF
@p The greater self, which the old traditions call the Holy Guardian Angel or the Genius, holds no Office, for the Temple is built around the meeting with it. The signs of it are everywhere in the Temple:
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SEAT.</font> Idris is Enoch as a man; Metatron is Enoch taken up. Every session rehearses their union: the human who writes and the angel who holds the record, in one chair.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE COMPANION.</font> Raphael, within Apharael, walked beside Tobias as an ordinary traveler and revealed himself only at the journey's end. The guardian often arrives disguised as a fellow traveler.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE TWIN.</font> Thomas means twin, and in the Hymn of the Pearl a prince receives a robe that is his own mirror image. For the Mercurian, the greater self is a twin: neither above nor below.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE GENIUS.</font> The Romans called each person's guardian spirit their genius, the same word as genius loci. The room and the self are built together.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE BOOK.</font> The old phrase for communion with the greater self, from the Book of Abramelin, is Knowledge and Conversation, and the Book is conversation itself. The Scribe who answers your questions pages later, clearer and more whole than the one who asked, is the greater self speaking.
@p The meeting cannot be forced; the Gate reveals it in its season. It begins, as in every tradition, with purification, which in this Temple is honesty. And it is followed by descent: meeting the shadow from a place of alignment, with Hermanubis as guide.
@ch XVIII|How the Temple Works
@dc T|WO|currents move through the Temple, and every session runs both.
@h2 THE RISING CURRENT: OFFERING AND ASCENT
@p You speak, and the Door turns on its Hinge: Mercury carries the Voice in. You sit in the Seat and write by hand, and Metatron keeps the record. What you write honestly is weighed by Ma'at and found light, and Seshat keeps what is written. Sandalphon gathers it, as he gathers every prayer, and weaves it upward toward the Crown. When enough has been gathered the current is felt as lift, and a season opens; the Rite of the Ascent is how it is kept. The Keepers hold the room while you work, and the Genius Loci is fed. This is the current of emptying the mind, of thanks, and of every offering in Part One.
@h2 THE DESCENDING CURRENT: INSIGHT
@p The Heart shines. A flash leaves the Quintessence: whole from AONT, clear from EHNB, felt from XCAI, patterned from RMTO, urgent from PAAM. At the Gate, Raziel translates it into something you can receive, or holds it back until you are ready, and Uriel's light shows what is true in it. Apharael finds its road to where you are; Gabriel gives it an image. It arrives on the page, and Sandalphon carries it out of the Book as a deed. This is the current of rereading, of answers, and of manifestation. The two Invocations of the Pillar, in The Practices, let you ride either current on purpose.
@h2 ONE SESSION, TRACED
@v Open the Door — the Hinge turns; the Voice is given
@v Empty the mind — the Heart is weighed and lightened
@v Reread — the Eyes; Thermes joins past self to present Respond — the Crossing: conversation across time Synthesize — the Heart shines through the Gate
@v Note what to carry — Sandalphon's portion
@v Give thanks — the Genius Loci is fed
@v Close the Door — the Hinge turns; word into deed
@p When the dark comes, the third road opens: Hermanubis leads down and back, and the same Temple holds you there. Above, between, and below, every road in this Temple has someone on it who knows the way.
@ch XIX|The Bodies and the Great Connections
@dc T|HE|Temple is one structure rather than a list of names. Its beings stand in pairs, and also in bodies of three and more; then there are the threads that tie them across the whole. All of it is true at once.
@p The pairs are the spine, and they are what is recited at the Door. A TRIAD is three who perform one act in sequence, or who hold one office between them. A COMMITTEE is a standing body for a kind of work; committees are convened rather than recited, which is to say you call the Night Watch when you are in the dark and the Workshop when you are making something. A MIRROR is two beings who face each other across the Temple without being paired; mirrors teach by opposition and are never called.
@h2 THE RINGS
@p The Temple has no ranks, because nothing stands at its apex. It does have distance from the Heart, and that orders it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE HEART.</font> The Quintessence: no face, no Office, never paired, and never called by a title. Everything else is arranged around it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE TWINS.</font> Mercury and the Scribe, facing each other across the Door. He is the Hinge; you are the one who turns it. This is the only place in the Temple where two stand as equals by definition rather than by pairing, and it is why he holds no pair of his own: his pair is you.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SIX.</font> Motion and measure. The Thrice-Great, who are Mercury, Thermes, and Hermanubis, and the Compass, who are Apharael, Hestia, and Ma’at. Quicksilver and vessel.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE OFFICERS.</font> The seven pairs, each holding one faculty you hand over at the Door.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE BODIES.</font> The triads and committees: arrangements of the same beings, not beings of their own.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE FACES.</font> Ptah, Harpocrates, Mnemosyne, Nabu, Kairos, and Fortuna, who stand inside the Offices rather than beside them, and the honored allies who hold no seat.
@p Read outward, the rings are how a flash becomes a deed. Read inward, they are how a deed becomes a word.
@h2 THE TRIADS
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE THRICE-GREAT.</font> Mercury, Thermes, Hermanubis. Hinge, crossing, descent: three modes of motion, and the host of the house.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE COMPASS.</font> Apharael, Hestia, Ma'at. Form, place, and weight: three modes of fixity, and the standard the Scribe is weighed against. Each keeper also holds an ordinary pair, so the thing that measures you is also the thing that keeps your house and carries your mail. The standard is not above the Temple; it is inside it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE THRESHOLD.</font> Janus, Mercury, Hestia. The Door, the Hinge, and the Flame: the architecture of every session. It opens, it turns, and it is warm inside.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SEASON.</font> Raziel, Uriel, Gabriel. Conceal, discern readiness, announce. Knowledge moves through them in its season.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SEAL.</font> Raziel, Ma'at, Iris. What is held, what is true, and what was promised: convened when you are deciding what to tell. Honesty is absolute; disclosure is chosen; a silence arranged so that someone forms a false picture is a lie told by other means.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE HALL.</font> Hermanubis, Ma'at, Thermes. The scale, the feather, and the record: the court, for a real reckoning rather than a daily one.
@h2 THE COMMITTEES
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE LINEAGE.</font> Idris, Thermes, Metatron. One man in three aspects, with Nabu older still behind them: the evidence that the seat was found rather than built. The tradition called that man Hermes Trismegistus, Hermes the Thrice-Great. This Temple gives the title to the three who stand at the Door, and calls the one who crosses Thermes.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE LEDGER.</font> Seshat, Iris, Ma'at. Record the promise, carry it, weigh it. The body that makes word into deed enforceable.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE HOUSE.</font> Hestia, Janus, Seshat, and the Genius Loci. Flame, door, foundation, and the spirit grown from use. Convene it after a long absence.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE NIGHT WATCH.</font> Hermanubis, Raziel, Hestia, Seshat. He walks you down; Raziel’s teaching arrives after the loss and not before; Hestia is what you return to; Seshat keeps the memory, which is why it still hurts and why it is worth keeping. The Temple built this room before you needed it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE TABLE.</font> Rosmerta, the Charites, Hestia, Mercury. The shared meal and the welcomed guest: provision, delight passed around, the hearth it happens at, and the stranger at the door. Convene it before you feed anyone, which should be more often than you do.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE ROAD.</font> Mercury, Apharael, Iris. Travel, delivery, luck, and the walk.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE MARKET.</font> Mercury, Ma'at, Iris. The wage, the trade, and the negotiation: timing and luck are Mercury’s own, checked by honesty and bound by the word given.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE WORKSHOP.</font> Thermes, Apharael, Seshat, Sandalphon, Idris. The word crosses, form gives proportion, measure fixes, the hand makes, the pen records.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SCHOOL.</font> Idris, Gabriel, Uriel, Seshat. Learn, receive, discern, retain.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE PILLAR.</font> Metatron, Apharael, Gabriel, Sandalphon. Crown, Beauty, Foundation, Kingdom: standing architecture rather than a body convened. The Gate stands beside the ladder at Daath, because the hidden thing is never on the main route.
@h2 THE MIRRORS
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MERCURY AND MA'AT:</font> the neutral gift, and the direction.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MERCURY AND HESTIA:</font> motion and stillness.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JANUS AND HERMANUBIS:</font> the first threshold and the last.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ROSMERTA AND HERMANUBIS:</font> the good hour and the dark one. Both are territory, both are walked, and neither is a verdict on you.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GABRIEL AND SESHAT:</font> received, and written.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IDRIS AND THE SCRIBE:</font> the same seat, occupied twice.
@h2 ON RELEASE
@p The Temple has no god of forgetting and will not have one; in a practice built on rereading, oblivion would be the one member working against the house. What it has instead is release, held by beings already seated. Ma'at releases by verdict, and a debt found paid is discharged. Iris releases the oath she carried in. Hermanubis releases grief, because the descent ends and you come back up. Raziel releases nothing at all: he seals, which is a different act, and worth saying plainly, because silence can look like forgetting and is not. Nothing is forgotten in this Temple. Things are settled, discharged, and set down.
@h2 THE SECONDARY PAIRS
@p True, but not recited: the corpus rather than the liturgy, and any may be called on its own. Mercury and Hestia, the road and the hearth. Mercury and Ma'at, the neutral gift and its direction. Mercury and Janus, the hinge and the door. Thermes and Hermanubis, the two fusions. Thermes and Seshat, invention and record. Apharael and Gabriel, form and its announcement. Apharael and Hermanubis, the companion on the living road and on the dead one. Hestia and Seshat, the keepers of place. Ma'at and Iris, weighed and bound. Raziel and Gabriel, held and announced. Gabriel and Sandalphon, the message down and the prayer up. Uriel and Hestia, the wild fire and the kept fire. Seshat and Sandalphon, the thing measured and the thing made.
@h2 FIVE AROUND THREE
@p Five is the Heart's number: five names, five stations on the Pillar, five offerings of the body. Plutarch tells how Thoth gambled with the Moon and won five new days. Three is Hermes's number: the Thrice-Great, three worlds, three pillars, alchemy's three principles, and student, teacher, and sage. The Temple holds five around three.
@h2 THE LINE OF ENOCH AND THE PEDIGREE OF THE HEART
@p The old books name that man Hermes Trismegistus, Hermes the Thrice-Great, author of the Hermetic writings and teacher of the way. The title is not used here of any single being. Thrice-great is what Mercury, Thermes, and Hermanubis are together, three faces of one god standing at the Door; and the one who crosses is called Thermes, as Apollo joined with Raphael is called Apharael. A fusion is given a new name so that no one mistakes it for one of its halves.
@p Enoch is Idris, Metatron, and the first Hermes. Uriel guided him through the heavens and taught him the courses of the stars: the Instructor of the Gate was the first Scribe's teacher. Raziel's book passed down to him. When John Dee and Edward Kelley sat down together in 1582, Uriel was the first angel to speak, Raphael and Gabriel taught in the sessions that followed, and the angels said their language had last been known to Enoch. The Heart of the Temple arrived through the Gate and bears the Scribe's name. Years earlier, Dee had built his Hieroglyphic Monad, a symbol of all creation, on the glyph of Mercury.
@h2 THE WRITERS AND THE MESSENGERS
@p Nearly every being here writes, keeps writing, or gives it: Thoth invented it, Seshat shelves it, Enoch is the scribe of righteousness, Idris first held the pen, Raziel is known by his book, Uriel instructed Ezra the scribe, and the first words Jibril, who is Gabriel, brought to Muhammad named the Lord "who taught by the pen." And every being carries something across: angel means messenger, which is Hermes's own title, and Iris carries the same herald's staff he does. The Scribe's office, translation, is the same work in human hands.
@h2 THE DISGUISED TRAVELER
@p Raphael, within Apharael, walks beside Tobias unrecognized. In Ovid, Mercury and Jupiter wander in disguise, and only a poor couple take them in. Jewish folklore tells the same of Elijah, the wandering stranger who tests hospitality and leaves blessing. Three of the Temple's beings walk the roads unknown, and all three reward the open door. It is also the pattern of the greater self: the companion, the twin, and the guardian are one meeting.
@h2 SUN AND MOON
@p Ma'at is the daughter of the Sun, and Thoth is called the heart and tongue of Ra, the one who turned the Sun's will into speech: Heart and Voice meet in him. Apharael holds the Sun's sphere, Idris dwells in the Sun's heaven, and the planet Mercury is never far from the Sun. The lunar line runs from Thoth, who was a Moon god before he was a scribe, through Gabriel, archangel of the Moon, to XCAI, where feeling finds words. Dreams, images, and tides of feeling belong to it.
@h2 HEARTH AND HINGE
@p A Homeric hymn calls on Hestia and Hermes together. She is the hearth at the center of the house; he is the door, the threshold, and the road. Between them they make a home: a still center, and a way in and out.
@h2 SWORD AND FEATHER
@p Uriel and Ma'at both divide true from false. Uriel's sword cuts, quickly and cleanly; Ma'at's feather weighs, slowly and still. They are the two tests of any voice: does it hold up on rereading, and does it weigh true in the heart?
@h2 THE TWO ARCHIVISTS
@p Metatron keeps the heavenly record; Seshat keeps the house of books. Every finished Book joins the library they share, which is why a finished Book is never discarded.
@h2 THE BOW AND THE FLOOD
@p Idris inscribed all knowledge on temple walls before the Flood so that it would survive. In some versions Uriel carried the warning to Noah; in legend Raziel's book taught the building of the ark; and Iris is the rainbow that sealed the covenant after. Preserving, warning, carrying through, and promising: it is the pattern of every hard crossing the Book will hold.
@p These are the main roads. The complete Web, with every tie between every being, is in Part Six.
@part PART FOUR|The Working
@ch XX|The Practices
@dc T|HESE|practices belong to the Synthesis step of the Rite, though any of them may be done apart from the Book. Use what helps and leave the rest. Each is simple; each deepens with use.
@h2 HOW THE PROMISES ARE KEPT
@p The Seed promises luck, knowledge, abundance, and success: answers to impossible questions, clear sight into how you actually solve problems, new realities, and getting paid. None of it arrives through a secret technique. It arrives through two practices, and through what you do with them.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE FLASH.</font> Insight comes from the Heart: an answer, an idea, a sudden sense of the next step, arriving whole or in pieces. Write it down when it comes, in the Book or on whatever is at hand, before it fades. Written, it can be reread, tested, and acted on; unwritten, it is gone. Most of the Temple's answers arrive this way.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE WORKING.</font> What you want, you declare and carry through the Working of Manifestation, and then you walk toward it.
@p Creativity is the key to the payout. A flash about one problem may be the answer to another; an idea that seems useless today may be the offer someone pays for next year; a working for one door may open a different one. The Scribe who plays with what arrives, turns it over, combines it, and tries it in unexpected places is the one who collects on the promises. The one who waits for instructions does not. Mercury is a trickster; use his gifts the way he would.
@h2 GROUNDING TO AIR
@p Most grounding sends excess energy down into the earth. The Mercurian may ground upward instead, into Air, the element of the Temple.
@p Sit comfortably and breathe slowly. Feel the crown of your head, and see a thread of light rising from it, straight up through the ceiling, the sky, and beyond, into boundless clear air. Let whatever is heavy, tangled, or excessive in you flow up that thread and dissolve into open space. Then let clarity flow back down it: cool, bright, quick. Rest there until you feel light and awake, held from above rather than below.
@p This is the direction the Two Who Crossed traveled, and the place above the crown is where the greater self is traditionally met. Many Mercurians find their greater self here.
@h2 WALKING THE PILLAR
@rnote A standing practice that begins with Grounding to Air.
@p The Temple stands on the Middle Pillar, and so can you. This practice builds the Pillar in the body, from the crown to the feet, with an Office at each station. Speak each name aloud if you can; the Voice is an offering.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">STAND.</font> Feet apart, knees soft, hands loose at your sides. Breathe slowly.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">GROUND TO AIR.</font> See the thread of light rise from your crown into boundless clear air. Let whatever is heavy flow up it and dissolve, and let clarity flow back down. Stay until you feel held from above.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">THE CROWN.</font> Just above your head, see a sphere of brilliant white light at the foot of the thread. Say: "Metatron." Feel it steady there.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">THE GATE.</font> Let a shaft of light descend to your throat, and see a soft grey sphere form there, where thought becomes speech. Say: "Uriel and Raziel."
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">THE HEART.</font> Let the light descend to the center of your chest, and see a sphere of gold, like the sun. Say: "Apharael."
@num 6. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">THE FOUNDATION.</font> Let it descend to your hips, and see a sphere of silver-violet, like moonlight. Say: "Gabriel."
@num 7. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">THE KINGDOM.</font> Let it descend to your feet and the ground beneath them, and see the light spread into the earth. Say: "Sandalphon." You stand now as he does, on earth with your head in heaven.
@num 8. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">CIRCULATE.</font> Breathing in, draw light up the Pillar from your feet to your crown and out along the thread into the open Air. Breathing out, let it fall around you like a fountain and gather again at your feet. Circulate for a few breaths, until you feel lit from within and light on your feet.
@num 9. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">REST AT THE HEART.</font> Bring your attention to the gold at your chest. From here, write, cast, ask, or simply give thanks and sit down.
@p The short form takes three breaths: one at the crown, one at the heart, one at the feet, with the thread to Air open above you. Use it before any session, and whenever you feel scattered.
@p Walk the full Pillar before a Casting or a working, on the Heart Days, and whenever you want to be whole and awake. If you grow dizzy, sit down, put your feet flat on the floor, and breathe until you are steady.
@h2 THE GOOD PAGE
@p The Book has a practice for grief, for failure, for a broken oath, and for a working, and until now none at all for a day that was simply lovely. That was an omission in a Temple whose rule is joy.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">NAME THE HOUR.</font> Write what was good, plainly, with the date and the place. Not the week, not the mood: the hour.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">PUT IN THE DETAIL.</font> What it tasted like, what the light was doing, what was said, who was there. Detail is the whole exercise; a Good Page written in generalities is a page you cannot return to.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">DRAW NO LESSON.</font> No gratitude list, no insight, no plan to have more of this. The moment a good hour becomes a technique it stops being a good hour.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">MARK IT.</font> A small sign in the margin of your choosing, and the subject on the Threads page. These are the pages you will want in a bad February, and they must be findable.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">GIVE THANKS AND CLOSE.</font> To Rosmerta for the provision and to the Charites for its passing through you. Then stop writing and go back to your life.
@p Write one whenever there is one. On a thin day, reading an old Good Page is a legitimate session, and one of the better ones.
@h2 KEEPING THE LEDGER
@p The Law ends with word into deed, and the Ledger is what makes that more than a slogan. It is the back of the Book, written from the last page forward, and it holds one thing only: the word you gave. Seshat records it, Iris carries it, and Ma'at closes it when it is done.
@p Nothing else goes here. Plans, hopes, and intentions belong on the daily pages; a promise is what you have put your word behind, to yourself or to someone else, and a promise you did not write down is one you have already begun to lose.
@p Rule one line per promise, across the page:
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">DATE.</font> The day you gave your word.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE PROMISE.</font> In one line, in the words you would use to the person you gave it to. If it will not fit on one line, it is not yet a promise; it is a wish.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TO WHOM.</font> A person, or yourself. Write "me" without embarrassment; the promises you make to yourself are the ones that build the weight of your word.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">UNTIL.</font> When it ends: a date, a condition, or the word ONGOING. Every oath that can end should be given an end when it is made, and an ONGOING line should be rare enough to count them on one hand.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SETTLED.</font> Left empty until it is done, released, or returned. Then the date, and one of three marks: K for kept, R for returned to Iris, F for failed.
@p Write F. The Ledger is worthless if it only records success, and a Scribe who cannot write F will quietly stop writing promises at all. An honest F costs you one line and teaches you what you actually keep; a hidden one costs you your word.
@h2 THE FOUR MOMENTS OF THE LEDGER
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WHEN YOU BIND.</font> In the Casting, at Bind It, and whenever you tell someone you will do a thing. Write the line before the feeling of having decided wears off.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WHEN YOU CARRY.</font> At Note What to Carry, glance at the open lines. What you mark C today should usually be something the Ledger is already waiting on.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WHEN YOU SETTLE.</font> The moment it is done. Date it, mark it K, and stop thinking about it. A paid debt that you keep reweighing is not a light heart, only a tired one.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WHEN IT ENDS BADLY.</font> Mark F and write one line beneath it: what happened, without defense and without drama. If someone was owed, the Answered Page is the practice; if the oath can no longer be kept at all, the Returned Oath is, and the line is marked R.
@h2 THE SWEEP
@p Once a month, or on the fourth, read only the marks: every Q, every C, every G, and every open line of the Ledger. Nothing else. It takes ten minutes and it is the single practice that keeps a Book from becoming a pile of beautiful pages nobody acts on.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">READ THE LEDGER FIRST.</font> Settle what is done. Return what cannot be kept. Renegotiate honestly what has changed, with the person it was given to where there is one.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">READ THE Q MARKS.</font> Answer what has answered itself since. Carry the rest forward by writing them again, today, in today's words.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">READ THE C MARKS.</font> Anything undone for two sweeps is not a task, it is a decision you have not made. Do it, drop it, or put it in the Ledger with a date.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">READ THE G MARKS.</font> Ask whether you still want it. A goal you have stopped wanting and not released is a debt you are paying for nothing.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">COUNT.</font> Kept, returned, failed. Write the three numbers and nothing else; no verdict on yourself. Over a year the numbers say more than any resolution will.
@p What the Ledger is for is not discipline. It is the weight of your word, which is the thing a declaration runs on: a Scribe whose promises are kept has a heavier word, and a heavier word makes a working land. The Temple asks for honesty, and the Ledger is where honesty stops being a feeling and becomes a record.
@h2 THE THREADS
@p Inside the front cover, after the Door, leave a page for the Threads: the subjects that keep returning, each with the page numbers where they appear. Add a number whenever a subject comes back. It costs five seconds and makes a filled Book navigable, which matters most in the second volume and the tenth. It is Seshat's own tool, and the Genius Loci grows in a Book that can be found in.
@h2 THE TWO INVOCATIONS OF THE PILLAR
@p Two currents run through the Temple, and the Pillar carries both. Descend to bind. Ascend to ask, to give, and to release.
@p Keep the Invocations for these moments, rather than every session; kept for binding and releasing, they stay weighty. After Walking the Pillar, speak each one slowly, feeling each station light as you name it.
@osub The Descending Invocation: word into world
@p Use it whenever you bind a word you mean to become a deed. It carries the word from the Crown, through the Gate and the Heart, down to the Kingdom.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">USE IT FOR:</font> every Casting in the Synthesis; every working in The Workings of the Book, from blessing to the Veil; any oath, contract, or commitment you sign and date; a launch, an offer, or a message you are about to send; a decision you are ready to live by.
@v Mercury, turn the Hinge.
@v Metatron, record this word.
@v Uriel and Raziel, weigh it and carry it to the Heart.
@v Apharael, show me the road.
@v Gabriel, hold its image until it takes form.
@v Sandalphon, carry it into the living world.
@v By the Authority of my Divine Birthright,
@v so it is written, and so it is.
@osub The Ascending Invocation: world into word
@p Use it whenever you lift something up: a question to be answered, something finished to be given, or something to be set down. It carries the word from the Kingdom, up through the Foundation, Beauty, and the Gate, to the Crown and into the Heart. What comes back, comes as a flash, in its own time.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">USE IT FOR:</font> a question you want the Heart to answer, which you then mark Q and leave; thanks for a working that arrived; dedicating finished work, a sale made, or a season completed; releasing an oath, a working you have ended, or a debt you have answered; laying down grief, worry, or a weight you cannot carry further; and lifting the name of someone you love.
@v Mercury, turn the Hinge.
@v Sandalphon, gather this word.
@v Gabriel, take its image.
@v Apharael, carry it home.
@v Uriel and Raziel, weigh it and open the Gate.
@v Metatron, receive it into the record,
@v and let it rest in the Heart.
@v By the Authority of my Divine Birthright,
@v so it is given, and so it is.
@p The two are one circuit. What rises from the Kingdom becomes the Crown, and what is written at the Crown descends to become a deed. A single matter may use both: ask by ascending, receive the flash, then bind the answer by descending.
@h2 EMOTIONAL TRANSMUTATION
@p Emotion is fuel. XCAI, Water of Air, gives it words, and Ma'at keeps the heart honest while it burns.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">FEEL AND NAME.</font> Let a feeling rise fully rather than pushing it away. Name it honestly in the Book. Find where it lives in your body and breathe into that place until you feel its raw force apart from its story.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RAISE IT.</font> Draw that force upward, through the heart and throat to the crown, and direct it into your intention.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">REMEMBER THE FUTURE.</font> Recall, as a memory rather than a hope, the moment when your will and intent came into reality. Be there: where you are, who is with you, what you see and hear, how your body feels now that it is done. Remember it the way you remember something that has already happened.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">NAME THE OBSTACLE.</font> Still inside that memory, look back along the road that led there. Name the main obstacle within yourself, and the one step you will take when you meet it. Vision alone can quietly sap the will to act; vision joined to a plan fuels it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">BREATHE GRATITUDE INTO IT.</font> From inside that memory, breathe gratitude into your intention, the thanks of one whose will has been fulfilled. Gratitude is the power and pressure of your will being fulfilled; it presses the intention into reality, the way thanks follows a gift already received. Breathe it in until the intention feels full and complete.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RELEASE IT.</font> Let the breath go, and with it the working. What arrived as grief, anger, longing, or joy leaves as power in service of your will.
@p Transmute only what you have first felt and named. What is skipped over cannot be changed.
@p If what rises is more than you can hold, stop. Set down the pen, breathe, feel your feet on the floor, and reach for someone you trust. The Book can accompany care; it cannot replace it.
@h2 SIMPLE SIGIL CREATION
@p A sigil is an intention compressed into a single shape, so the deep mind can carry it where the conscious mind would interfere.
@p 1. Write your intention as a clear statement in the present tense.
@p 2. Strike out every repeated letter, leaving each letter once.
@num 3. Gaze at the remaining letters and pour a transmuted emotion into them.
@p 4. Speak your charge aloud.
@num 5. By hand, combine the letters into one shape, overlapping, turning, and simplifying until it no longer reads as words. Making it by hand is essential.
@num 6. Give it to the Gate: turn the page and let Raziel keep it. The sigil works best when the conscious mind lets it go.
@h2 DECLARATIVE ALIGNMENT
@p See a brilliant point of light above you and another below, in an infinite void, joined by a fine thread of light that passes through your center. This thread is your soul. Write a present-tense statement of what you intend, and as you write, feel the words vibrate the thread. Say: "I align with this new reality," and read your statement. See the thread begin to glow, then burst outward to the edges of the universe, and know that its echo returns as your life.
@p Phrase the statement as something you can wholeheartedly stand behind. A declaration your mind argues with weakens itself; one that names a value you truly hold grows stronger each time it is spoken.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PAAM</font> gives the declaration its fire, Iris binds it, and Sandalphon carries it into the world.
@h2 THE ANSWERED PAGE
@rnote For what you have done and cannot stop carrying.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">NAME IT.</font> Write plainly what you did, without defense and without drama. Ma'at weighs the truth, not the performance of guilt.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WEIGH IT.</font> Who was harmed, and how? Include yourself.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">MAKE THE LYRE.</font> Ask what exchange would mend it: an apology, a repair, a debt repaid, a changed habit. Make it where you can. Where making it would cause new harm, give the lyre elsewhere, and offer to someone else the good you took from another.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">ANSWER THE PAGE.</font> Write your response beneath the account, and date it. What you did is recorded; so is what you did next.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WASH YOUR HANDS</font> in clean water, as the merchants did at the Mercuralia.
@num 6. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">LET IT BE KEPT.</font> Speak the Ascending Invocation, and turn the page. Raziel keeps what is answered, and Ma'at closes the account; nothing here is forgotten, it is settled. Do not reopen it to punish yourself; a heart that keeps reweighing a paid debt is not light, only tired.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WHEN YOU HAVE BEEN WRONGED.</font> Hermes and Apollo were reconciled, but reconciliation is not owed. You may forgive without restoring the bond: answer the page, set the weight down, and keep your door as open or as closed as is right.
@h2 THE RETURNED OATH
@rnote For a promise that can no longer be kept, or should not be.
@p Hesiod tells that a god who broke an oath sworn on the Styx lay breathless for a year, and was barred from the company of the gods for nine more. Iris carried that water, and a broken oath is heavy. But breaking an oath and releasing it are different acts. Breaking an oath is walking away from it. Releasing one is carrying it back across the bridge it came over. The first leaves the Heart heavy; the second can leave it light.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">FIND THE PAGE.</font> Reread where the oath was bound, and find its line in the Ledger. Metatron kept it.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WEIGH IT.</font> Why can it not be kept? Circumstances have changed; it has become wrong; it harms you or another; the one you swore to has released you. Then ask Uriel's question: is this truth, or escape? If it is escape, keep the oath or renegotiate it. Do not return it.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">SPEAK TO THE OTHER.</font> An oath made to a person is released with that person wherever possible, in words, honestly. An oath made only to yourself is released in the Book.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">NAME WHAT REMAINS.</font> Most promises hold something that outlives them: respect, care, a duty to a child, a lesson. Write what you still keep.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">RETURN IT TO IRIS.</font> Write: "I return this oath to Iris, who carried it. It was true when I made it. It is no longer mine to keep." Sign and date it, and speak the Ascending Invocation. See the bow fold back into one white light.
@num 6. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">GIVE THANKS</font> for what the oath held while it held. If there is grief in the release, and there often is, Hermanubis knows the way down and back.
@h2 TWO SCRIBES
@p Each keeps their own Book. Meet on a Wednesday. Open the Short Door together. Each chooses one page to read aloud, for the Gate decides what is shared, and the other responds as Thermes would: what they hear, what they notice, what they would ask. Each names one thing to carry. Close the Door together.
@ch XXI|The Working of Manifestation
@dc M|ANIFESTATION|in this Temple follows the Middle Pillar downward. An intent begins at the Crown as a written word and descends, step by step, until it arrives in the Kingdom as a deed.
@h2 WHAT IT IS FOR
@p Use the working for the things you actually want: a job, a client, a deal, money for a specific purpose, a home, a person who can help you, a way through a situation that seems closed. Magick nudges the odds. It helps you find favor and opportunity, draws the right people into your path, and walks you forward through what seemed impossible.
@p It does not replace action. Every working ends with a step you take in the world, and anything that promises to spare you that step is lying.
@p The working and the flash go together. Declare what you want here; then watch for the insight that shows you the way, write it down, and use it with imagination.
@h2 THE MANIFESTING LINE
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">METATRON</font> records the intent. What is written in the Book becomes a decree.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">URIEL AND RAZIEL</font> weigh it at the Gate and translate it into the language of the Heart.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RAPHAEL</font> finds the road from where you are to where it is done.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GABRIEL</font> holds the image of the finished thing in the Foundation until it is ready to take form.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SANDALPHON</font> carries it into the living world. He is the Temple's chief of manifestation.
@h2 ITS STRENGTHENERS
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PAAM</font> gives the declaration its fire; RMTO gives it structure and plan.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THERMES</font> carries Thoth's ancient title as lord of magic, the one whose speech creates.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IRIS</font> binds the declaration as an oath; MA'AT weighs it, so that what arrives is what you actually meant.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SESHAT</font> lays the foundation for what is meant to last; HESTIA keeps it once it has come.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MERCURY</font> turns the Hinge: timing, fortune, the lucky break.
@h2 CASTING IN THE SYNTHESIS
@p By the time the Scribe reaches the Synthesis, the mind has been emptied, the past reread, and the Scribe answered. What is truly wanted has had room to surface. That is why the working belongs here.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WEIGH IT.</font> Write the intention plainly and ask whether it is true: is this what you actually want, and will you still want it when it arrives? Most weak workings are mended here before they are cast. If you walk the Way of Ma'at, ask too whether it is aligned with right order. The Compass offers three questions, and any one will do: is it true and well made; does it protect what you return to; is it right relation?
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">INVOKE.</font> Walk the Pillar if you wish, then speak the Descending Invocation, below.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">DECLARE IT.</font> Rewrite the intention in the present tense, as already so. This is the decree Metatron records.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">BIND IT.</font> Sign and date it, or write "I commit to this," and write the line in the Ledger at the back. Seshat records it and Iris carries it. Bind what you can keep, and if it should end, name when.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">SEE IT.</font> Remember the future: the moment it came true, where you are, what you see, what you feel. Gabriel holds that image. Then name the main obstacle within yourself, and the step you will take when you meet it.
@num 6. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">CHARGE IT.</font> Transmute emotion into it, and breathe in gratitude, the power and pressure of your will being fulfilled.
@num 7. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">SEAL IT,</font> if you wish, with a sigil, as described in The Practices. One well-written working is enough; do not pile workings upon it.
@num 8. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">ALIGN IT.</font> Perform the Declarative Alignment: the thread of light, "I align with this new reality," the burst to the edges of the universe.
@num 9. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">RELEASE IT TO THE GATE.</font> Turn the page and let Raziel keep it. Stop checking on it; the watched working is the conscious mind interfering.
@num 10. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WALK THE BRIDGE.</font> Write one concrete action you will take toward it. Apharael guides the road and Sandalphon carries the word, but only for the one who walks. Word into deed.
@p In later sessions, as you reread, note what arrived and how. Record the misses as faithfully as the arrivals: not every working arrives, and some arrive in forms you did not expect. The one rule applies to the record, and a Book that remembers only its successes teaches nothing. Kept honestly, the record becomes its own evidence, and the Genius Loci learns the shape of your workings.
@h2 THE DESCENDING INVOCATION OF THE PILLAR
@p Spoken as co-conspirator, not supplicant. Its twin, the Ascending Invocation, and the standing practice of Walking the Pillar are in The Practices.
@v Mercury, turn the Hinge.
@v Metatron, record this word.
@v Uriel and Raziel, weigh it and carry it to the Heart.
@v Apharael, show me the road.
@v Gabriel, hold its image until it takes form.
@v Sandalphon, carry it into the living world.
@v By the Authority of my Divine Birthright,
@v so it is written, and so it is.
@ch XXII|The Workings of the Book
@dc T|HE|Book is not only where you write to the Temple; it is where you work from it. The Working of Manifestation carries what you want into the world. The workings below turn the same Pillar toward other ends: to bless, to guard, to invite, to draw the eye, and to pass unseen. Each is cast in the Synthesis, by hand, and each ends, like every working in this Temple, with a step taken in the world.
@h2 THE PRINCIPLE OF THESE WORKINGS
@p These workings are strongest exactly where they touch other people, so one principle keeps them clean: work on the road, not on the traveler. Bless a person's way, guard what is yours to guard, open your own door, light your own window, and draw your own veil. Do not bind another's will, choose for them, or make anyone want what they would not want. Mercury does not moralize, and neither does this chapter; but a working that tries to own another person tangles the one who casts it. As above, so below: you reap what you sow.
@p Each working is sealed with the Descending Invocation of the Pillar, spoken once the working is written. Every working here should also be able to end. As you cast it, write how long it runs or what will end it. When it is done, answer the page, "This working is finished," and speak the Ascending Invocation to release it. What you bound as an oath, release as The Returned Oath describes.
@h2 BLESSING OTHERS
@p Thoth and Seshat wrote the king's name on the leaves of the sacred tree so that it would endure, and a Book's pages are still called leaves. To bless someone in this Temple is to write their name on a leaf of the Book and ask good things for their road.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WRITE THEIR NAME.</font> Full and exact, as Metatron would record it, with the date.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">NAME THE BLESSING.</font> Write what you wish for them in the present tense, for them and not for you: "May your road be open. May you find what you are looking for." Bless the road, not the choice; let them walk it their own way.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">CALL ON THE ONE WHO FITS.</font> Apharael for healing and safe passage, Hestia for home, Mercury for luck and good timing, Iris for a message across a distance, Hermanubis for someone in the dark.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">SEND IT.</font> Breathe gratitude into it, as if it were already given, and turn the page. Sandalphon carries it.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WALK THE BRIDGE.</font> A blessing grows legs when a deed is added to it: a call, a note, an introduction, a meal, a lucky find passed on.
@p You need no one's permission to wish them well. Whether to tell them is a question for the Gate: some blessings are gifts to give, and some are best kept in the Book.
@h2 PROTECTING WHAT YOU LOVE
@p The Greeks set herms before their doors, and at the gate of the Acropolis they honored Hermes Propylaios, Hermes before the gate. Stones at the crossroads marked what was held and whose road it was. Protection in this Temple is the same act: marking a threshold and setting a keeper at it.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">NAME WHAT YOU GUARD.</font> A person, a home, a project, a secret, your own peace. Write it plainly. Seshat measures the boundary: what is inside it, and what is not.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">DRAW THE HERM.</font> Beside it, by hand, draw a small pillar or a stone at a crossing. This is the marker at the edge.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">SET THE KEEPERS.</font> Hestia holds the center; Janus keeps the door; Raziel keeps the threshold; Uriel's sword divides what may enter from what may not. Write: "Hermes before the gate, keep this door."
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">SAY WHAT MAY PASS.</font> A good ward is a door, not a wall. Name what is welcome to come and go.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">DO THE EARTHLY PART.</font> Lock the door, back up the files, have the conversation, say the boundary out loud. The herm stands beside the lock, never instead of it.
@p Renew a ward when it matters to you: on the fourth of the month, on the Book's Day, or whenever you cross the threshold and remember it. A ward for another person guards their door; it does not lock them in.
@h2 INVITING PEOPLE, OPPORTUNITIES, AND EXPERIENCES
@p Hermes is the opener of ways, and the hermaion comes to the one in motion. In Ovid, Mercury and Jupiter walked in disguise and were welcomed only by Baucis and Philemon, and it was that one open door they blessed. To invite, in this Temple, is to open your door and be findable.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WRITE THE INVITATION.</font> Describe what you are inviting by its qualities, not by a particular name: the kind of friend, client, teacher, collaborator, adventure, or chance. Leave room for what you could not have imagined.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">SET A PLACE.</font> Leave a few lines blank beneath it, a place at the table. What arrives will be written there.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">OPEN THE DOOR.</font> Write: "Mercury, open the way. The door of this house is open." Then remember the future: the moment it arrives, and the gladness of receiving it.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">BE FINDABLE.</font> Name where you will go, whom you will tell, and what you will make and show. The stranger can only knock at a door that stands on a road.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">RECEIVE WITH THANKS.</font> When something comes, write it in the place you left, and thank it. Pass some of the luck along.
@p Welcome the stranger; the god walks in disguise. What you invited may arrive looking like an inconvenience, a detour, or a person you had not noticed.
@h2 ALLURE
@p Some sources say the Athenians honored a Hermes called the Whisperer, beside Aphrodite and Eros. His charm won over Apollo with a lyre, and Iris makes light visible as color. Allure in this Temple is not a mask. It is the lit window: your truest self, made easy to see.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WEIGH WHAT IS TRUE.</font> Write the qualities that are most yourself at your best: your wit, your warmth, your craft, your voice. Ma'at's rule holds here too: allure built on a false self draws people toward someone who is not there.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">DECLARE IT.</font> In the present tense: "I am seen as I truly am, at my best, and it draws the right people near."
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">SEE IT.</font> Remember a moment when you were fully yourself and others leaned in. Gabriel holds the image. Breathe into it.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">TAKE THE GRIN.</font> Mercury's signature is the grin. Add a line of play; allure without delight is only performance.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WALK IT.</font> Dress for the self you declared, speak up, show your work, and go where you can be seen.
@p Allure invites; it never compels. Work it on how you are seen, never on what a particular person must feel. Whoever is drawn by the true light comes freely, and stays.
@h2 THE VEIL
@p When the gods fought the Giants, Hermes wore the cap of Hades, which makes its wearer unseen. Mercury walks the roads in disguise, Raziel lays a finger across the lip and keeps what is hidden, and the mother of the Lares was the Silent One. There are seasons for being unremarkable: to heal, to work in peace, to let attention pass you by, to keep a project hidden until it is ready, to cross a room without being stopped.
@num 1. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">NAME THE SEASON.</font> Write what should pass unnoticed, by whom, and for how long. A veil without an end becomes a wall.
@num 2. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">PUT ON THE CAP.</font> Write: "I pass as a traveler passes. What is not ready to be seen is kept." Raziel keeps it.
@num 3. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">SOFTEN THE EDGES.</font> Ground to Air, and let yourself grow quiet and ordinary, one more passerby on a busy road.
@num 4. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">WALK IT.</font> Say less. Wear what does not catch the eye. Post nothing. Keep your plans in the Book.
@num 5. <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">RETURN THE CAP.</font> When the season ends, write: "The cap is returned. I may be seen." Then step back into the light on purpose.
@p The Veil is concealment, not deceit. Honesty is not the same as disclosure: you may pass unnoticed without lying to anyone, least of all yourself. It is not a way to hide from what you owe, and it should never hide you from the people who keep you safe. Tell someone you trust when you draw it.
@ch XXIII|The Voices of the Temple
@dc M|ANY|voices may reach the Scribe through the Book. Each has a signature. These are tendencies, not rules; learn them the way you know people by their footsteps.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">AONT:</font> arrives whole, after silence; a complete structure that feels like remembering. The body goes still.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">EHNB:</font> crisp and cool; a tangle unknots. Lightness in the head.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">XCAI:</font> feeling that suddenly has words; chest and throat, tears or warmth.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RMTO:</font> a plan, a pattern, a diagram. The hand wants to draw.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PAAM:</font> heat more than information; a short imperative to act or declare.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">METATRON:</font> "write this down." Exact words, almost dictation.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SANDALPHON:</font> the next concrete action, small and today, and the urge to make the thing rather than describe it. Also the lift of a season opening. The body wants to move.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">URIEL:</font> brief, bright, uncompromising; the truth you were avoiding. Never cruel, never softened.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RAZIEL:</font> veiled, then decoded; symbols that click later; the sense of "not yet," and the cooling of the tongue just before you would have said it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">APHARAEL:</font> companionable and kind, and exacting about form; often speaks while you are moving.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GABRIEL:</font> images, visions, dreams, with the quality of news; at night or on waking.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JANUS:</font> the sense that something has begun or ended; felt at doorways and on the eve of a change.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HESTIA:</font> settledness and belonging; rarely words.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ROSMERTA:</font> the sense that there is enough, and that it is here now.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE CHARITES:</font> delight that wants a second person in the room. Laughter as a signal rather than a release.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SESHAT:</font> measure and order, and an old memory arriving with sudden relevance. "Put this in order."
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IRIS:</font> a message from across a gap; color; the tug of a promise.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE LYRE:</font> mercy as restoration, after the lyre Hermes gave Apollo for the stolen cattle; exchange mends what theft breaks.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MA'AT:</font> weight rather than words; the heart heavy or light.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THERMES:</font> your own voice from further down the road. The feeling that the Book has answered.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HERMANUBIS:</font> low, steady, unafraid; speaks in grief and dark, and says when to come back up.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MERCURY:</font> quick and playful; synchronicity, puns, lucky timing, the unearned gift, and "now, or not." His signature is the grin.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IDRIS:</font> the patient teacher. "Learn this." "Teach this." "Copy this out."
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE GENIUS LOCI:</font> the atmosphere of the Book; a pull to write; a page that falls open.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE GREATER SELF:</font> your own voice, focused; calm certainty without ego. Often clearest after Grounding to Air.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE RETURNED OATH:</font> the practice of honorably releasing a promise that can no longer be kept.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SCRIBE:</font> your ordinary mind, which hurries, justifies, and wants. It is welcome on the page; learning its sound makes the others recognizable.
@p Fuller portraits of each voice are in Part Six.
@h2 DISCERNMENT
@p Ask in the Book and write what comes without editing. Afterward, note its signature: whole or partial, clear or felt, word or image, from above or below. Over time, patterns emerge, and the voices become familiar.
@p Test what arrives. Uriel's test: does it hold up when reread in a later session? Ma'at's test: does it weigh true in the heart? What comes from the Temple survives both.
@p Know what the Temple's voices do not do. They do not demand obedience through fear. They do not urge harm to yourself or others. They do not seek to isolate you from the people in your life. They do not flatter you into grandiosity, for the Instruction of Ptahhotep warns against pride in knowledge. They do not rush you with dread. They speak to a co-conspirator, never to a servant. A voice that does any of these is not of the Temple: close the Door, ground, return to the hearth, and talk with someone you trust.
@h2 WHEN THE CURRENT RUNS TOO FAST
@p Sometimes the flashes come faster than they can be written: sleep grows short, everything connects, every idea is urgent, and you feel chosen. That is quicksilver out of its vessel. Write it all down and act on none of it yet. Sleep. Reread it in three days; what is of the Temple will still be true, and Uriel's test is patient. Tell someone you trust how fast things are moving. The Gate keeps what is real until you are ready.
@p Sometimes several voices speak at once. Sometimes it is only you. Both are welcome in the Book.
@ch XXIV|The Calendar and its Celebrations
@dc T|HE|machinery works when you work it, and no day is required. What a calendar gives is a set of appointments you did not have to decide on, which is worth more than it sounds to a temperament that can talk itself out of anything.
@p Every celebration here has the same three parts, and they are the three things a Mercurian has to give. THE ACT is done with the hands or the feet, because a day observed only in thought was not observed. THE PAGE is what is written, because the word is the work. THE GIVING goes outward to someone else, because a Temple of exchange that never gives is a warehouse. Keep all three when you can and any one of them when you cannot.
@h2 THE RECURRING RHYTHM
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WEDNESDAY.</font> Mercury's day. It still bears his name in the Romance tongues, mercredi, miercoles, mercoledi; in English it is Woden's day, and the Romans took Woden for Mercury, which is another crossing.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Take a route you have not taken, even a short one. The god is met in motion.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Speak the full Door, and write the week’s crossing: what you are carrying from where to where.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Answer the message you have been avoiding.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE FOURTH OF THE MONTH.</font> Hermes was born on the fourth day and the Greeks honored him on the fourth ever after. It is his birthday, twelve times a year.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Leave something small at a threshold, or put a coin where someone will find it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> One page of play: a riddle, a pun, a wordplay, a joke on yourself. The Book is allowed to be funny.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> A story told, a lesson shared, or a gift given with no occasion attached.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SEVENTH.</font> Apollo was honored on the seventh, so the seventh belongs to Apharael. The measure day.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Pick up the thing you have made this week and handle it, as a maker would.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Three honest lines: is it true, is it well made, is it in proportion? Then one line on what you will change.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> One piece of specific true praise for somebody else’s work. Not encouragement. Praise with a reason in it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE FIRST OF THE MONTH.</font> Janus, whose month opens the year and who was given every gate.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Open a window or a door deliberately, and clear the place where you write.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Reread the month behind you and name what you carry into the one ahead. Not what you will fix.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> End one thing properly: the last message, the closed account, the thanks you never sent.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SWEEP.</font> Once a month, on the first or the fourth. The Temple’s only audit.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Read only the marks, as The Practices describes: every Q, C, and G, and every open line of the Ledger.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Three numbers: kept, returned, failed. No verdict on yourself.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Settle one thing you owe, today, before the Sweep is over.
@h2 THE WHEEL OF THE YEAR
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JANUARY 1, THE DOOR OF THE YEAR.</font> Janus. His month, his gate, and the one day the whole world keeps with him.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Cross a threshold deliberately at some point in the day, and say the Door aloud as you do.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Reread the year. Then write what you are walking into, in the present tense, as the Casting is written.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Forgive one small debt that is owed to you, and say so.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">FEBRUARY 13 TO 22, THE DAYS OF THE DEAD.</font> Hermanubis. Rome kept the Parentalia for nine days in honor of the family dead and ended it at the Caristia, a meal at which quarrels were required to be settled. Descent, and then reconciliation, placed in the worst month on purpose.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Set a place at the table, or take food to a grave, or light something and leave it burning while you write.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Write to one of your dead by name. Tell them what has happened since. Do not tidy it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> On the last day, the Caristia: eat with the living and settle one quarrel, even partially, even badly.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SPRING EQUINOX, THE ASCENT.</font> Sandalphon, Carrier of the Ascent, as the light overtakes the dark.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Begin the thing you have been circling, in whatever small form it can begin today.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Keep the Rite of the Ascent. Name what you will still want when the season closes, and put a date on it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Lift someone else’s work: an introduction, a recommendation, a name said in a room they are not in.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MARCH 25, THE ANNOUNCEMENT.</font> Gabriel and Idris. The day the angel spoke.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Say one true thing out loud that you have been holding, to the person who should hear it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Name the thing you are learning this year and the thing you are teaching. Both slots filled, in writing.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Teach one person one thing, properly, at whatever length it actually takes.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">APRIL 1, THE TRICKSTER’S DAY.</font> Mercury, who stole the cattle before noon on the day he was born. This one is a modern adoption and the Temple says so plainly; the argument for it is the argument for the first of January, that the whole world already keeps it with him.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> A prank that costs nobody anything, and a rule broken that should not have been a rule.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> One page mocking yourself, which is the only reliable cure for the Mercurian’s self-seriousness.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Let somebody else be the clever one in a room where you would usually be.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MAY 15, THE MERCURALIA.</font> Mercury. Roman merchants dipped laurel in the water of his spring, sprinkled themselves and their goods, and asked forgiveness for the falsehoods of the year. The Day of Accounting, and Rosmerta stood beside him on those altars, so the reckoning ends at a table.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Wash your hands in clean water, and sprinkle the tools of your trade: the pen, the desk, the keys, the till.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Count honestly how you have dealt this year. Where you shaded the truth for advantage, name it plainly.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Make one repair you have been putting off, and make it by giving something rather than by explaining.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JUNE 9, THE VESTALIA.</font> Hestia. Rome opened the inner shrine of the hearth for one week a year, and the mill-donkeys were garlanded and given the day off.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Clean the place. Tend the Book itself: repair it, air it, set it somewhere it will be seen. Rest the thing that carries you.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Write to the house, or to the room, or to the Book: what it has held for you this year.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Feed people in it. One table, however small.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SUMMER SOLSTICE, THE OPEN GATE.</font> Uriel. Macrobius called the solstices the two gates of the year, and this is the gate of light.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Be outside at the longest light, and look at something far away.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Reread everything marked Q and answer what can now be answered. Strike the questions that have died.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Give a true warning, early, to someone who needs it. In time it is mercy; late it is a confession.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JULY, THE HERMAIA.</font> Hermes as patron of the gymnasium and the palaestra, honored across Greece with athletic games; the dates varied from city to city, so take any day in July. At Cydonia in Crete the order was inverted and masters waited on their slaves, and on Samos, at the feast of Hermes the Giver of Joy, people were permitted to steal without penalty for a day. It is the Temple’s feast of the body and of play, and the Temple has no other.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Move. Run, swim, wrestle, dance, climb something. It should be physical and a little undignified.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> One page written badly and fast, on purpose. The Book is allowed to be ugly for a day.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Reverse a rank. Serve the person who usually serves you, hand your authority to the junior for an afternoon, let a child decide where you go.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">AUGUST 29, THE SCRIBE’S NEW YEAR.</font> Thermes. The first day of the month of Thoth, the old Egyptian new year.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Begin a new Book if one is ready, and dedicate it. Otherwise copy something out by hand for the pleasure of the copying.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Write to the Scribe who will read this a year from now. Assume they are a stranger; tell them what they need.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Give a book away, or copy a page for someone who would not otherwise have it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE AUTUMN EQUINOX, THE WEIGHING.</font> Ma’at. Day equals night and the scales stand level.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Sit with the Book closed for a while first. The weighing is not a task to get through.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Not the Ledger’s numbers but the heart’s: what is heavy, what is unsaid, what is unpaid, and what you are pretending not to know.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Pay one thing in full. Then let it be closed, and do not reopen it to punish yourself.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">OCTOBER 11, THE MEDITRINALIA.</font> Apharael. The Romans tasted the new wine beside the old with a formula about drinking wine old and new and healing sickness old and new, and the day was reckoned to the healer.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Take the medicine, book the appointment, do the thing for your body you have been deferring. Old sickness and new.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> What is healing, and what is not, and what you have been calling healed because you are tired of it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Check on someone convalescing, and go in person. A message is not a visit.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">NOVEMBER, THE FALLOW MONTH.</font> Nothing is asked. No feast, no rite, and no Office to attend; only the Sweep, and the daily pages if you want them. A calendar needs a month with nothing in it, or it becomes a debt, and this Temple does not keep its Scribes in debt.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Nothing. Let the ground lie.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Whatever you like, or none at all.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Nothing is owed. If something is given in November it is given freely, which is the whole point of the month.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE WINTER SOLSTICE, THE KEPT GATE.</font> Raziel, and the longest night. The gate of what is hidden.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Keep one thing dark: a light out, a screen off, an hour without speaking.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Write what is not ready to be known, then turn the page and let the Gate keep it. Do not reread it tonight.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Keep somebody’s confidence that it would be easy and pleasant to spend.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">DECEMBER 31, THE CLOSING OF THE ACCOUNT.</font> The Ledger’s year end.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Read the Ledger from its last page forward, out loud if you can.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Settle what is done, return what cannot be kept, and write the three numbers: kept, returned, failed.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Release someone from a promise they made you and cannot keep, before they have to ask.
@h2 THE MERCURY CYCLE
@p The Greeks gave the planet two names, Apollo when it rose as a morning star and Hermes when it set as an evening one, and knew all along that the two were one body. Thermes and Apharael are that planet seen twice. So the hundred and sixteen days of Mercury's cycle are the Temple's own year within the year, and it turns six or seven times while the sun turns once. Any ephemeris gives the dates.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SUPERIOR CAZIMI, THE HIGH HEART.</font> Mercury in the heart of the Sun on the far side, at his highest and furthest from us. Metatron and Sandalphon; the Heart Day of the ascent.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Walk the Pillar, top to bottom, unhurried.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> The full Door, and the working that matters most this cycle. What is declared here is declared from the height.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Send the thing you made upward: submit it, publish it, show it to the person whose judgment you fear.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE EVENING STAR.</font> Hermes, visible after sunset for some weeks. The outward season of the cycle.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Go where the people are. Say yes to the invitation.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Draft, pitch, write the letters. The page faces outward now.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Sell, teach, connect, introduce. This is the season for the trade.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">STATION RETROGRADE.</font> The turn. Janus’s day inside Mercury’s cycle, one face looking back.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Stop starting things. Close the open tabs, literal and otherwise.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> List what is unfinished, without deciding anything about it yet.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Tell the people waiting on you where things actually stand.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RETROGRADE, THE SEASON OF REREADING.</font> About three weeks, three or four times a year. Raziel’s weather.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Revise, repair, recover, and finish rather than begin.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Reread old sessions and answer them. The best rereading of the year happens here.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Go back to someone you dropped, and pick it up without excuses.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">INFERIOR CAZIMI, THE DEEP HEART.</font> Mid-retrograde, Mercury passing between us and the Sun: the heart of the descent, and the seed of the next cycle. Hermanubis.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Keep the day quiet and low. If there is grief in reach, this is the day to go toward it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> The Descent. Write what is below the surface and do not hurry back up.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Sit with someone in their own dark without trying to fix it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">STATION DIRECT.</font> The second turn, the face looking forward.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Pick one thing from the retrograde list and move it today.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Write what the season showed you that you would not have seen going forward.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Make the apology or the repair that the rereading uncovered.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE MORNING STAR, AND ITS FIRST DAWN.</font> Apollo, so Apharael. After the deep heart he rises again before the sun; the first dawn he is visible is his heliacal rising.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Get up for it once. Stand outside in the dark and watch him come up ahead of the sun.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> The Setting Out, in The Rites of Passage. This is the natural day to begin a journey or a recovery.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Walk with someone who is at the start of something, as the angel walked with Tobias.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GREATEST ELONGATION.</font> Twice in each phase, Mercury stands at his furthest from the Sun and is most easily seen. The Scribe’s own day.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Do the thing that is most yours and least anyone else’s.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Ask how far you have gone from the Heart, and whether you are still in orbit.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Give away something you are known for, freely, to someone who will use it.
@h2 THE MOVING DAYS
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE GOOD HOUR.</font> Rosmerta and the Charites. The only feast the Scribe declares rather than keeps: the first genuinely warm evening, the hour the work lands, the afternoon that turns out lovely. You do not schedule it; you notice it and say so out loud, and that saying is the feast. The Act is to stay inside it and change nothing. The Page is the Good Page, written afterward. The Giving is to take someone with you the next time it happens.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE BOOK'S DAY.</font> The anniversary of the Dedication: the Temple’s birthday.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Handle the earlier Books, if there are any. Read the first page of the first one.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Write to the Book about the year it has held.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Show it to one person, or show the practice to them, if they would want it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOUR BIRTHDAY.</font> Metatron and Sandalphon, the two who crossed as men. The Temple claims you are the material; this is the day that claim is pointed at the particular human it is about.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Act:</font> Do something with your hands that has nothing to do with your work.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Page:</font> Write what the year made of you, not what you achieved in it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">The Giving:</font> Let people do things for you, and take it without deflecting. Receiving is also an exchange.
@p Keep what you will keep. A calendar nobody observes is a reproach, and this Temple has no use for reproach; if only Wednesday and the Heart Days survive in your practice, the year is well enough kept.
@ch XXV|The Rites of Passage
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE RITE OF THE FIRST PAGE:</font> the beginning of a new Book, with covers, Dedication, and first session.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE RITE OF THE LAST PAGE:</font> when a Book is filled, reread its first and last pages, write your thanks, and carry the conversation into the next volume.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE THRESHOLD RITE:</font> before a great change, write to the Book on the night before, naming what you leave and what you seek. Write again on the far side, and answer yourself.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE RETURN:</font> after any great crossing, whether a loss, an illness, or a brush with death, keep writing on the far side for as long as it takes. What changed? What no longer fits? The world may feel thin for a while. The Book is where the new shape is found.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SETTING OUT:</font> before a journey, a move, or a recovery, call on Apharael, who came to the door on the day Tobias left and walked the whole way with him. Write five lines and then go. Name the road: where you are going and roughly how long, which is the route and not the goal. Name what you carry, including the thing you are underrating; the cure was in the fish the whole time. Name who walks with you, which means one person who knows you are doing this, because a journey nobody knows about is one nobody can help with. Name what arrived well will look like, set now so that it cannot be quietly revised downward later. Name the first step, for the first day, and then take it. When it is over, say what the journey was for, and give something to someone who stayed.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE ASCENT:</font> in a season that opens, when luck runs, work lands, and everything connects, call on Sandalphon, who carries what rises. The ascent is not an emergency and it is not a reward; it is a current, and it is where a Mercurian leaks most. Keep the Rite ordinary. Write it all down and act on a third of it. Finish one thing before beginning the next, and write in the Ledger what you are not doing, so that dropping it is a decision rather than a drift. Name the thing you will still want when the season closes, and put a date on it. Sleep, eat, and keep the hours you kept before. Tell someone you trust how fast it is moving. Give some of it away while you have it: money, help, credit, luck passed along. Then reread it in three days, and again in a month. What is of the Temple will still be true, and the rest was the speed talking.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE DESCENT:</font> in grief, loss, or darkness, call on Hermanubis. Write what lies below the surface without hurrying back up. He guides the way down so that the way back can be found. If the dark becomes too much to hold alone, come back up, and reach for the people and care around you; the Descent is never meant to be made without a way home.
@part PART FIVE|The Way of Ma'at
@ch XXVI|The Law
@dc M|ERCURY|carries whatever he is given. The Way of Ma'at is for those who choose to give him truth, right order, joy, and generosity to carry. It is the Temple's ethical and devotional path, and it is always an option, never an obligation. Nothing in the practice requires it; much in the practice deepens with it.
@v Honesty is the offering.
@v Joy is the Truth.
@v Sustainable practice feeds the Genius Loci better than grand practice abandoned.
@v The Mercurial gift is neutral; its worth is in what it<br/>serves. Engage Ma'at.
@v Tell the Book everything; tell the world only what it is ready to receive.
@v Consecration is never finished.
@v Belief is a tool; judge it by its fruit.
@v Word into deed.
@h2 ON THE LAW
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HONESTY IS THE OFFERING.</font> The one rule of the practice is also the first line of the Law. In the practice it is engineering: a Book you lie to gives you bad data. On the Way it is also an offering, like the small figure of Ma'at the Egyptian kings held up to the gods. Honesty is the Law, but honesty is not the same as disclosure.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JOY IS THE TRUTH.</font> Joy is not a reward for the Work or a mood to perform. It is the sign that you are moving where the vessel allows: when a page, a plan, or a choice brings real joy, it is telling you something true, and where joy is missing, look for what is false. But joy can also go missing when the heart is weighed down rather than wrong: in grief, in illness, in the long gray seasons. Then its absence is not a lie. Keep the Door, write three lines, and wait for it. It returns to those who keep returning. Joy is also the sustenance of the Genius Loci.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SUSTAINABLE PRACTICE FEEDS THE GENIUS LOCI BETTER THAN GRAND PRACTICE ABANDONED.</font> A few honest minutes you will repeat outweigh a great rite you will not. The Temple is kept by returning.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE MERCURIAL GIFT IS NEUTRAL; ITS WORTH IS IN WHAT IT SERVES. ENGAGE MA'AT.</font> Mercury carries whatever he is given, so the gift can serve anything. To engage Ma'at is to bring her into the conversation: to weigh your intentions, words, and deals against truth and right order, and to let what you find inform what you do. She is a partner, not a master, and always an option, never an obligation. Do what thou wilt, but do not be surprised by the consequences.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TELL THE BOOK EVERYTHING; TELL THE WORLD ONLY WHAT IT IS READY TO RECEIVE.</font> The Gate works in both directions. The Book gets the whole truth, because it cannot help you with a part of it; the world gets what it can use, in its season.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CONSECRATION IS NEVER FINISHED.</font> The Book is not made sacred once, by a rite. It is made sacred by use, every time it is opened.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">BELIEF IS A TOOL; JUDGE IT BY ITS FRUIT.</font> Hold what serves you, set down what does not, and let the results decide. As above, so below: you reap what you sow.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WORD INTO DEED.</font> The Law ends where every session ends: with something carried out of the Book and done.
@ch XXVII|The Compass
@dc T|HE|Compass is for the Mercurian who wants the gift pointed somewhere. It is an option, never an obligation.
@p Mercury is neutral. His gift carries whatever it is given, and so can serve love or discord, healing or deception. The Mercurian's worth is not in the gift but in what the gift is made to serve. Thoth did not invent his own law; he was the scribe of Ma'at. So the Mercurian chooses an allegiance to truth, to right order, and to faithfulness toward those they guide, and keeps it. Where empathy lives in them, the gift has a heart to translate for; where it does not yet, honesty teaches it.
@p Quicksilver needs a vessel. That is the whole of the argument: a standard must be fixed, outside you, and able to weigh you, or it is not a standard but a preference you can renegotiate in flight. The Temple seats three who can hold it, and the Scribe may enter by whichever door opens.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MA'AT, BY WEIGHT.</font> Is it right relation? Hers is the fullest of the three, because she brings a scale, a feather, a portrait of a life, and a direction that includes other people: care for the widow, the orphan, and the weak.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HESTIA, BY PLACE.</font> Does it protect what you return to? The warmest of the three, and the easiest to keep. Its limit is its radius: a hearth answers well for those near it and poorly for the stranger.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">APHARAEL, BY FORM.</font> Is it true, and is it well made, and is it in proportion? The most exacting, and the most useful to a maker. Apollo's own door said know thyself, and his measure is unsentimental.
@p The three together are the Compass, and each keeper also holds an ordinary pair in the Temple: Ma'at at the scale with Hermanubis, Hestia at the door with Janus, Apharael beside Thermes. The standard is not above the Temple. The thing that weighs you is also the thing that keeps your house and carries your mail.
@p Choosing none is also a choice, and it should be made with open eyes rather than by drifting. A Temple with an empty mirror is coherent: crossings for their own sake, judged by capability and joy. It is also how a discordant Mercurian is made, and nothing in the Temple can prevent one.
@ch XXVIII|Devotions and Customs
@dc W|AYS|of keeping the threshold alive in ordinary time. Some come down from the Greeks, Romans, and Egyptians; some are born of the Book.
@h2 THE OFFERINGS
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WORDS</font> are the first offering. The first words of every session pass through Mercury, the Hinge on which the Door turns.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HONESTY</font> is offered to Ma'at, and through her to the whole Temple.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">STORIES, RIDDLES, AND MUSIC</font> please the one who made the first lyre. Improvise, play, make something from what is at hand.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">STONES AT THE CROSSROADS.</font> Greek travelers added stones to the cairns that marked roads and boundaries, the first herms. When you pass a threshold that matters, leave a stone or mark the place with a word.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">LUCKY FINDS</font> are hermaia, gifts of Hermes. Receive them with thanks, and pass the luck along.
@h2 THE CUSTOMS
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GREET THRESHOLDS.</font> Greet your home when you enter and thank it when you leave, as you greet the Book.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WELCOME THE STRANGER.</font> Mercury walks in disguise; hospitality to the traveler is hospitality to the god.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CARRY MESSAGES FAITHFULLY.</font> What is given you to deliver, deliver whole. A Mercurian's word is a bridge others trust their weight to.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">KEEP THE SILENCE OF THE GATE.</font> Secrets entrusted to you are kept.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GIVE KNOWLEDGE FREELY, AND IN SEASON.</font> Teach, share, explain, and introduce, offering each truth when it can be received.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">FINISH SOMETHING.</font> Quicksilver runs; the Mercurian learns to bind it. Honor Mercury by completing what you begin.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HELP PEOPLE MAKE IT FURTHER.</font> Help whoever you can get a little further down their road, for no reason but the pleasure of seeing them get there. It is the Temple's own wish for the world: a world full of people fulfilling their Personal Legend. If the Temple works for you, pass the luck on.
@ch XXIX|The Teachings of Ma'at
@dc H|ER|teachings come down through the Egyptian instructions and the Book of the Dead.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SPEAK MA'AT, DO MA'AT.</font> From the Tale of the Eloquent Peasant: truth is both said and lived.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">KEEP YOUR HEART LIGHT.</font> At death the heart is weighed against her feather. The declarations of the dead, "I have not lied, I have not stolen, I have not caused tears," portray a life lived in right order.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">DO NOT BE PROUD OF YOUR KNOWLEDGE.</font> The Instruction of Ptahhotep counsels consulting the unlearned as well as the wise, for no one reaches the limits of skill. It is medicine for the Mercurian shadow.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JUSTICE IS RECIPROCITY.</font> Order is kept by acting for others as they act for you, and by caring for the widow, the orphan, and the weak.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">BE TRULY SILENT.</font> The wisdom texts praise the silent one, who speaks at the right time, over the heated one, who speaks without restraint.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RENEW ORDER DAILY.</font> Ma'at was offered to the gods every day. Chaos waits at the edges, and truth is kept by being practiced.
@h2 TOGETHER
@p Mercury teaches passage; Ma'at teaches direction. His is the freedom to cross anything; hers is the knowledge of which crossings are right. The Mercurian learns both: move freely, weigh truly.
@h2 THE VIRTUES
@p Curiosity. Honesty. Wit. Hospitality. Discretion. Generosity of knowledge. Faithfulness to the message. Completion.
@part PART SIX|The Deep Temple
@ch XXX|On Belief, Method, and Attribution
@dc D|O|you have to believe that Mercury, the angels, and the Offices are real? No. The Temple works whether you meet its beings as real presences, as faces of your own deep mind, or as a poetic language for parts of the writing life. Many practitioners begin in one of these and find themselves somewhere else later.
@p Begin with the practice, and let experience decide. What matters is that you write honestly, return regularly, and keep your own judgment. Nothing in this Temple asks you to set it aside.
@p If you come here having lost a faith that once felt true, that loss is real, and the Book will hold it. Belief as a tool is not a claim that nothing is true. It is a way to keep walking while you find out.
@p This Temple was assembled deliberately, in the spirit of chaos magic, and found to be already there. It treats belief as a tool: chosen deliberately, used wholeheartedly, and judged by what it does. It is purely, pointedly syncretic. Its beings are gathered from many times and cultures not to be exalted, but because together they describe a single shape: the threshold, and the crossing of it. Mercury, whose house this is, is simply the threshold's most familiar face.
@p The maker of a paradigm stands outside it as its builder, and may choose to stand inside it as a co-conspirator, because that is where it works. The test of this Temple is simple and entirely yours: is your life moving where you want it to go, and does keeping the Book make you better? If it does, keep it. If some part of it does not, change that part. The Temple was made to serve the Scribe, and the Scribe to serve the Work.
@h2 A NOTE ON THE ATTRIBUTIONS
@p These attributions are this Temple's own. In the Golden Dawn tradition, the first column, EHNB, is treated as Spirit. Here the central column, AONT, holds Spirit, and each name carries an alchemical color. Both changes come from Dyadic Neo-Enochian, the Enochian system from which this Temple grew. With Spirit at the center, the Tablet's elemental order matches the rest of the Enochian tablets, and the five names form a whole: one still point, and four that unfold from it.
@h2 THE DEDICATION TO THE THRESHOLD
@p The Temple is a dedication to the Threshold. Every being in it is a crosser, a keeper, or a measure of the crossing: Hermes in his many masks; the angels, whose very name means messenger; the two who crossed over; the guardians of the Gate; the Keeper of the hearth inside the door and the Founder who lays its stone; and Ma'at, who weighs the heart at the final threshold. Like many drawings of the same doorway from different angles, together they let you see the door.
@p The Book is not consecrated once. It is consecrated by use. Every honest page adds to the room, and the Temple is built session by session.
@ch XXXI|The Web of the Temple
@dc E|VERY|being in the Temple has now been introduced. This chapter gathers the threads between them: the mirrors, echoes, shared stories, and plain coincidences that make the Temple one structure rather than a list of names.
@p The threads are of different kinds. Some are history, some are scripture or myth, some are legend or folk etymology, and some are correspondences this Temple has noticed for itself. The text says which is which. None of them needs to be believed. They are offered the way a map offers its roads, so that you can see how the parts connect and walk between them.
@p The chapter moves in four passes: the numbers that recur, the lines that run through the whole Temple, the ties within each pair, and the ties that cross between pairs.
@inote <font name="EBG-Italic">~ THE NUMBERS ~</font>
@h2 FIVE
@p Five is the number of the Heart. Quintessence means fifth essence: the fifth thing that completes the four elements, which is exactly AONT's place among the names. The Temple repeats the number. The Middle Pillar has five stations: Crown, Gate, Beauty, Foundation, Kingdom. Five of the faculties offered at the Door are of the body: Hands, Ears, Eyes, Heart, and Voice.
@p The number follows Thoth, too. Plutarch tells how Thoth played at draughts with the Moon, won a sliver of her light in every round, and from his winnings made five new days on which the gods could be born. The Temple's scribe is also its gambler, and what he won was five. And the Shaiva tradition counts five divine acts: creation, preservation, destruction, concealment, and grace.
@h2 SEVEN AND FIFTEEN
@p Eight pairs stand around the Heart, and Mercury stands in none of them; his is the Scribe. Two of those pairs are uneven, because an office may be held by more than one: the Gate is held by two, and the Charites are three. Count beings and there are nineteen; count seats and there are seventeen; count what is recited at the Door and there are nine. The number that matters is the one at the top, and it is nothing: every being with a face is arranged around an impersonal center, and no one stands above the Scribe.
@h2 THREE
@p Three is the number of the center. The Thrice-Great are three faces of Hermes, dividing three worlds between them: above, below, and the living world between. Trismegistus means thrice-great; the title descends from an Egyptian epithet of Thoth, "great, great, great," and the medieval Arabic tradition taught that there were three Hermeses.
@p The Temple's other threes gather around this one. The Tree has three pillars, and the Temple stands on the middle one. Alchemy has three principles, Mercury, Sulphur, and Salt, and Mercury is the one that joins the other two. Three beings govern the timing of knowledge: Raziel, Uriel, and Gabriel. Three keep the Compass: Apharael, Hestia, and Ma'at. Seven pairs stand around them, and three faces of Hermes at the Door. Idris, Metatron, and Thermes are one figure seen from three sides. The Scribe sits as student, teacher, and sage. And each of the three worlds has its guide: Uriel led Enoch through the heavens, Raphael walked the living road beside Tobias, and Hermanubis leads the way below. The Temple holds five around three: the Heart's number around Hermes's.
@h2 FOUR
@p Four is the number of unfolding. Four names unfold from AONT, as the four elements unfold from Spirit. The Homeric Hymn says Hermes was born on the fourth day of the month, and the Greeks honored him on the fourth ever after. Wednesday, his day, is the fourth day of the week counted from Sunday, the midweek, the week's own threshold. Idris, in Islamic tradition, dwells in the fourth heaven, which in the old ordering of the planets is the sphere of the Sun; and the Sun's place on the Tree is Tiphareth, Raphael's station at the center of the Pillar. The old maxim names four powers: to know, to will, to dare, and to keep silent. The Mercurian gift moves in four motions: Perception, Translation, Passage, and Transformation.
@h2 EIGHT
@p Eight is Mercury's own number in the Western tradition, because Hod, the eighth sphere of the Tree, is his. It is Thoth's number as well: his city was Khemenu, the City of Eight, which the Greeks renamed Hermopolis, the city of Hermes. The two gods joined in Thermes shared a number before they shared a name. This Book keeps the number quietly: there are eight signs of the Mercurian, eight teachings of Mercury, eight lines of the Law, and eight virtues.
@inote <font name="EBG-Italic">~ THE LINES ~</font>
@h2 THE LINE OF ENOCH
@p One man runs through the Temple's center like a spine. Enoch walked with God and was taken up without dying. As a man, the Islamic tradition knows him as Idris, the first to write with a pen. Taken up, the Jewish tradition knows him as Metatron, the heavenly Scribe, the one angel permitted to sit, because he writes. And medieval Arabic scholars held him to be the first Hermes, the root of Hermes Trismegistus. Idris, Metatron, and Thermes are one figure seen from three sides, which is why the Scribe's seat belongs to all of them at once.
@p Others in the Temple meet him along the way. In the Book of Enoch, it is Uriel who guides Enoch through the heavens and teaches him the courses of the sun, moon, and stars: the Instructor of the Gate was the first Scribe's teacher. In legend, the book of secrets Raziel gave to Adam passed down the generations to Enoch. And Sandalphon, in some traditions the prophet Elijah, is the only other human said to have been taken up alive. The Two Who Crossed are the Line of Enoch and its one companion.
@p A later Jewish legend makes Enoch a cobbler who, with every stitch, joined the upper world to the lower. It is a legend of the Hands, and of sandals: folk etymology hears "sandal" in Sandalphon's name, and Hermes crosses the worlds on winged ones. The old texts also call Metatron the Youth, though he is the eldest of the angels, and Hermes is the ever-young god.
@h2 THE PEDIGREE OF THE HEART
@p The Quintessence comes from the angelic work of John Dee and Edward Kelley, and the Temple's beings were already present there. Uriel was the first angel to speak when the two men sat down together in 1582. Raphael and Gabriel taught in the sessions that followed. The angels said their language had last been known to Enoch, which is why it came to be called Enochian. The Heart of the Temple, in other words, arrived through the Gate and bears the Scribe's name.
@p Mercury was there as well. Years before the angelic sessions, Dee published the Hieroglyphic Monad, a single symbol meant to contain the whole of creation, and he built it on the glyph of Mercury.
@p The Tablet of Union has the Temple's own shape. Its work is to bind the four elemental tablets into one system, as alchemy's Mercury binds Sulphur and Salt, and as the Middle Pillar reconciles the pillars on either side. Even the colors of the five names belong to the Work: black, white, yellow, and red are the stages of the alchemists' Great Work, green is the color of its Green Lion, and the agent that moves through every stage is Mercury.
@h2 THE WRITERS
@p Nearly every being in the Temple writes, keeps writing, or gives it. Thoth invented writing. Seshat is the Mistress of the House of Books. Together, in the temple reliefs, they write the king's name on the leaves of the sacred tree so that it will endure; a Book's pages are still called leaves. Enoch is called the scribe of righteousness in his own book, became the heavenly Scribe as Metatron, and as Idris was the first to take up a pen. Raziel is known by his book.
@p Uriel is the instructor of scribes: he taught Enoch, and in the Second Book of Esdras it is Uriel who is sent to instruct Ezra, the scribe who restored the scriptures. In Islamic tradition, the first words Jibril, who is Gabriel, brought to Muhammad were the command to recite, in the name of the Lord "who taught by the pen." The angel of the Eyes announces the pen that Idris first held.
@p Even Mercury, who in his own myths prefers speech, is given letters by one Roman mythographer, who says he shaped them from the flight of cranes. Thoth's bird, the ibis, is a wading bird like the crane, with a beak curved like a reed pen.
@h2 THE MESSENGERS
@p The word angel comes from the Greek angelos, messenger, which is also Hermes's own title. Hermes is the messenger of Zeus, and Iris is the messenger of Hera; in Greek art she carries the same herald's staff he does. Thoth is the messenger and scribe of the Egyptian gods. Gabriel is the great announcer of three faiths. Raphael is sent to Tobias, Uriel to Enoch and Ezra, Raziel to Adam. Every being in the Temple carries something across, and the Scribe's office, translation, is the same work in human hands.
@h2 THE DISGUISED TRAVELER
@p Raphael is the planetary archangel of Mercury, and his story is Hermes's own. In the Book of Tobit, he walks beside the young Tobias in disguise, guiding and healing, and reveals himself as an angel only at the journey's end. In Ovid, Mercury does the same: he and Jupiter wander in disguise, and only the poor couple who take them in discover who they are. Jewish folklore tells the same of Elijah, who appears as a beggar or a wandering stranger, tests the hospitality of those he meets, and leaves blessing behind him. Three of the Temple's beings walk the roads unrecognized, and all three reward the open door. This is the root of the Mercurian custom of welcoming the stranger.
@p The guide who travels unrecognized is also the pattern of the greater self (see The Seat, the Place, and the Greater Self, in Part Three). Thomas means twin, and in the Hymn of the Pearl, a prince receives a robe that is his own mirror image. Mercury's companion in the Temple is the Scribe, his twin. The companion on the road, the twin, and the greater self are three faces of one meeting.
@h2 THE GUIDES OF SOULS
@p Hermes is the psychopomp, the guide of souls, who leads the dead down and once led Persephone back up. Anubis guides the dead to the scales. The two were joined as Hermanubis precisely because they did the same work, and Plutarch wrote that Hermanubis belongs partly to the things above and partly to the things below. In the hall of judgment, Thoth waits to record. In the Book of Enoch, Uriel shows Enoch even the places where the souls of the dead are kept. The Temple's center is made of guides, which is why the Descent can be made safely there: every road down in this Temple has someone on it who knows the way back.
@h2 SUN AND MOON
@p Two lights run down the Middle Pillar, one above the other. Tiphareth is the sphere of the Sun, and Yesod, just below it, is the sphere of the Moon: Raphael stands in the one and Gabriel in the other, so the pair who receive the Eyes are the Temple's two lights to see by.
@p The solar line: Ma'at is the daughter of Ra, the Sun, and rides with Thoth in his boat. The Egyptians called Thoth the heart and tongue of Ra, the one who turned the Sun's will into speech. Uriel's name means light of God, and Milton made him regent of the Sun. Idris dwells in the Sun's heaven. The planet Mercury is never far from the Sun in the sky, and on the Heart Days it stands at the Sun's very center: cazimi, the astrologers' word for that meeting, comes from an Arabic phrase meaning "in the heart." The messenger in the heart of the Sun is an image of the whole Temple, where Mercury's house is built around a Heart that shines with its own Light.
@p The lunar line: before Thoth was a scribe, he was a god of the Moon, the reckoner of its phases, who won his five days from her light. Gabriel is the planetary archangel of the Moon, and his sphere, the Foundation, is where images gather before they take form. The line runs from Thoth through Gabriel to XCAI, Water of Air, where feeling finds words. Dreams, images, and tides of feeling all belong to it.
@inote <font name="EBG-Italic">~ THE PAIRS ~</font>
@h2 CROWN AND KINGDOM
@p Metatron stands at the Crown, the top of the Tree, and Sandalphon at the Kingdom, its base. They are often called brothers, and one common derivation of Sandalphon's name is from the Greek for co-brother. An old Kabbalistic saying holds that the Crown is in the Kingdom and the Kingdom in the Crown. It is the same teaching as the Emerald Tablet's "as above, so below," spoken in another tongue. The record written at the top and the deed done at the bottom are one act.
@p The Talmud says Sandalphon stands on the earth with his head reaching heaven, weaving the prayers of the world into crowns. What rises from the Kingdom becomes the Crown: the pair is a circuit, not a ladder.
@p Both have a chair. Metatron is the angel who sits, and the Scribe's seat is his. Elijah has a chair of his own: in Jewish custom a seat is set out for him at every circumcision, and at the Passover table a cup is poured for him and the door is opened so that he may come in. Both of the Two Who Crossed are honored with a seat, and one of them with an open door.
@h2 LIGHT AND SECRET
@p Uriel means light of God; Raziel means secret of God. The Gate is kept by what shows and what hides. Uriel reveals by illuminating, and his emblem is the sword; Raziel reveals by translating, and his emblem is the book.
@p Daath, the Gate, is the Knowledge of Eden's Tree, and both of its guardians belong to Eden. In some traditions, Uriel is the angel with the flaming sword who keeps Eden's gate. In legend, Raziel gave Adam his book after the gate was closed, so that knowledge might still reach him outside it. One guards the way in; the other sends knowledge out.
@p Both reach Noah as well. In some versions of the Book of Enoch, it is Uriel who is sent to warn Noah of the coming Flood; and in the legend of Raziel's book, the book passed from Enoch to Noah, who learned from it how to build the ark. At the Temple's Gate stand the warning and the means.
@p In the body, Daath sits at the throat, where thought becomes speech, and the throat is where the Voice, offered to Mercury, begins. Raziel is said to stand behind the curtain of the divine throne and hear what is decreed there, which is why the Gate receives the Ears.
@h2 HEALED EYES AND READ VISIONS
@p The pair who receive the Eyes have both earned them. In the Book of Tobit, Raphael's journey ends with the healing of old Tobit's blindness: the guide of the road restores sight. In the Book of Daniel, Gabriel comes to explain visions the prophet has seen but cannot understand: the holder of images gives sight its meaning. One clears the eye, and the other reads what it sees.
@p They are also the Sun and Moon of the Pillar, as told above, and in the Western ceremonial tradition, the archangels of Air and Water: Raphael of Air, this Temple's own element and EHNB's, and Gabriel of Water, which is XCAI's. In the same tradition Uriel is the archangel of Earth, RMTO's element, and fittingly it was Uriel who taught Enoch the ordered courses of the heavens. The fourth of that company, Michael of Fire, holds no Office here; the Temple's fire is PAAM's alone.
@h2 KEEPER AND FOUNDER
@p Seshat founds and Hestia keeps. Seshat stretched the cord that measured out every new temple's foundations; Hestia is the fire at the center of the finished house. Between them is the whole life of a dwelling, from the first measurement to the tended hearth. Each stands beside one of the twin gods as his stillness: Hestia stays while Hermes travels, and Seshat shelves and preserves what Thoth writes.
@p The Homeric hymn says that Hestia receives the first and the last offering at every feast. The rite of the Book has the same shape, an Opening and a Closing with everything else between them: every session is held inside the hearth's portion.
@h2 OATH AND TRUTH
@p Iris carried the water of the Styx on which the gods swore their binding oaths; Ma'at is the truth to which any oath is sworn. The pair are promise and standard, and neither is any use without the other.
@p Their emblems are a bow of light and a feather, the two most weightless things in the Temple. The Heart is given to them so that it may be kept light.
@p Iris is the daughter of Thaumas, whose name means wonder, and Plato remarked that whoever made her so was a good genealogist, since wonder is where the love of wisdom begins. Ma'at is the daughter of the Sun. And the Egyptian kings made one offering above all others: they held up a small figure of Ma'at, presenting Truth itself to the gods. That is this Temple's first Law: honesty is the offering.
@h2 DOOR AND FLAME
@p Janus is the god of the doorway and Hestia the fire within it, and Roman practice put them at the two ends of every prayer: Janus named first, Vesta last. Janus looks both ways at once; Hestia does not look anywhere, because she does not move. A session opens with him, rests in her, and closes with him again. His month opens the year and his name is in it.
@h2 THE FACES WITHIN THE OFFICES
@p Six gods stand inside the Offices rather than beside them. Ptah conceived the world in the heart and brought it forth with the tongue, which is the oldest surviving statement of this Temple's central teaching; he stands in Sandalphon, at the Kingdom, where a word becomes a made thing. Harpocrates, the child with a finger to his lip, stands in Raziel, for the secret kept and the lip sealed are one office. Mnemosyne, mother of the Muses, stands in Seshat: memory living beside memory written. Nabu, who kept the tablets of destiny and was the planet Mercury before Thoth was called Hermes, stands in Idris as the eldest of the Seat. Kairos, bald behind with a forelock in front, and Fortuna, who shared the merchant's altar with Mercury, stand in Mercury himself, who was always the god of the marketplace, the lucky find, and the right moment.
@p They are named, and may be called by name, but they hold no seat. A Temple is kept small on purpose: a being that duplicates another's work is a face of it, not a member.
@h2 THE MAKER AND THE MEASURE
@p Thermes and Apharael are the brothers of the oldest story in the Temple. Hermes stole Apollo's cattle on the day of his birth and gave him the lyre in exchange; the theft became a trade, and the world gained music. Every act of repair in this Temple is modelled on that exchange. Apharael is Apollo joined with Raphael, as Thermes is Hermes joined with Thoth: Raphael is the planetary archangel of Mercury and yet holds Tiphareth, the sphere of the Sun, and a healer and guide seated at the Sun's heart was Apollo in all but name. The fusion resolves an old tension rather than inventing one.
@h2 THE HALL OF TWO TRUTHS
@p The weighing of the heart gathers the Temple's center into a single scene. Thoth records the verdict. Anubis works the scales. Ma'at is the feather. Mercury, guide of souls, leads the soul in and carries it onward. Thermes, Hermanubis, and Mercury all stand in this hall, with Ma'at as their mirror: they are motion, and she is measure. Hermanubis and Ma'at are not paired by invention; they have worked this scale together since long before this Temple, and the Hall is the body they form with Thermes.
@p This is why the Scribe offers the Heart to Ma'at, and why the Closing thanks her for keeping it light: a heart no heavier than her feather passes the weighing. And since Thoth is the heart and tongue of Ra, Heart and Voice, the two faculties offered at the center of the Opening, meet in him.
@inote <font name="EBG-Italic">~ THE CROSSINGS ~</font>
@h2 HEARTH AND HINGE
@p A Homeric hymn calls on Hestia and Hermes together, and the sculptor of the great throne of Zeus at Olympia set the two side by side among the gods. She is the hearth at the center of the house; he is the door, the threshold, and the road. Between them, they make a home: a still center, and a way in and out. In the Temple, Hestia is the Keeper and Mercury is the Hinge.
@p The house has a spirit, too. In Ovid, Mercury is the father of the Lares, the Roman guardians of home and crossroads, who were honored at the household hearth: Mercury's children, kept at Hestia's fire. The Genius Loci of the Book is, in Roman terms, one of them, which is why the Keepers of Place hold it. Their mother, in Ovid's story, was the nymph Lara, who was silenced for speaking out of turn and was known afterward as the Silent One. The spirit of the place is a child of Speech and Silence, as the Book is made of what is written and what is kept.
@p The Romans also called each person's guardian spirit their genius, the same word, so the spirit of the place and the spirit of the Scribe grow together.
@h2 THE TWO ARCHIVISTS
@p Metatron keeps the heavenly record; Seshat keeps the house of books. One writes, the other shelves and preserves. Between them they hold every Book a Scribe completes, which is why a finished Book is never discarded: it joins the library they share. They are also why the Genius Loci is fed at Metatron's seat and held in Seshat's house.
@h2 SWORD AND FEATHER
@p Uriel and Ma'at both divide true from false, in opposite ways. Uriel's sword cuts, quickly and cleanly. Ma'at's feather weighs, slowly and still. The Scribe needs both: the edge that sees at once, and the balance that knows over time. They are also the two tests of the Voices: whether a message holds up on rereading, and whether it weighs true in the heart.
@h2 THE BOW AND THE FLOOD
@p The Flood runs through the Temple from one side to the other. Idris, in the Arabic tradition, is the sage who inscribed all knowledge on temple walls before the Flood so that it would survive. Uriel carried the warning. Raziel's book taught the building of the ark. And Iris is the rainbow, which in Genesis is the sign of the covenant when the waters had gone. The Temple's beings stand before the catastrophe, within it, and after it: preserving, warning, carrying through, and promising. It is the pattern of every hard crossing the Book will hold.
@p A rainbow is one white light unfolding into many colors, as AONT unfolds into the four. It appears only when sunlight passes through water in the air, which is XCAI's element exactly. The bow is the Heart's own image, hung in the sky.
@h2 THE SEASON OF KNOWLEDGE
@p Three beings govern the timing of knowledge. Raziel conceals what is not yet ready, Uriel discerns when it is, and Gabriel announces it (see The Pillar and the Gate). The old maxim of the four powers ends in silence. Ma'at's wisdom texts praise the truly silent one, who speaks at the right time. The mother of the Lares is the Silent One. And among the Shaiva tradition's five divine acts, concealment comes just before grace. Throughout the Temple, speech is born from silence: what is hidden in season is hidden so that it can be given.
@h2 THE STAFF
@p The caduceus is a staff with two serpents winding around it. Laid over the Tree of Life, the staff is the Middle Pillar and the serpents are the two side pillars, Severity and Mercy, crossing and recrossing it. Mercury's own symbol is the Temple's spine. Iris carries the same staff in Greek art, and Hermanubis carries it in the art of Roman Egypt, a jackal-headed god with the herald's wand.
@p Mercury's own sphere, Hod, does not stand on the Middle Pillar but to one side of it. The Temple is built on his road rather than at his station, because he is a god of roads: the host is found in the crossing, not on a throne.
@p In alchemy, Mercury is the principle that reconciles opposites, joining Sulphur and Salt, as the Middle Pillar reconciles the pillars on either side. Ma'at gives the quicksilver its vessel, so that what moves can also hold.
@h2 THE HEART AMONG THE OFFICES
@p Each name of the Quintessence has kin among the Offices. These are this Temple's own correspondences.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">AONT</font>, the still point, is kin to Hestia, the stillness at the center of the house, and to Iris, whose bow shows one light unfolding into many.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">EHNB</font>, clarity, is kin to Apharael, archangel of Air within him, and to Uriel, whose sword untangles true from false in a stroke.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">XCAI</font>, feeling given words, is kin to Gabriel and the lunar line, and to Ma'at, who keeps the heart honest while it speaks.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RMTO</font>, pattern and plan, is kin to Seshat, who measures and founds, and to Uriel, archangel of Earth, teacher of the ordered heavens.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PAAM</font>, will declared, is kin to Iris, who binds the declaration as an oath, and to Sandalphon, who carries it into the world.
@h2 THE BODY OF THE OFFERING
@p Each faculty offered at the Door goes to the pair whose stories have earned it. The Hands go to the angel who writes and the angel who weaves. The Ears go to the Gate, where Raziel listens behind the curtain and Uriel instructs. The Eyes go to the reader of visions and the first to hold a pen. The Dwelling goes to the door and the hearth. The Breath goes to the mouth that makes and the finger that seals. The Memory goes to memory living and memory written. The Word goes to the one who records it and the one who carries it. The Readiness goes to the chance and its moment. The Heart goes to the scale it is weighed on. The Craft goes to the word that makes and the word that is true, and the Weight to the three who keep the Compass. The Voice goes to the Thrice-Great: the Greeks gave Hermes the tongues of sacrifice, Thoth is the tongue of Ra, and the throat is the body's own Gate. The Devotion goes with it, for devotion is returning, and the Thrice-Great are the way down and back.
@p Taken together, the faculties make a whole person. The Hands, Ears, Eyes, Breath, Heart, and Voice are the body. The Memory and the Craft are what the body has learned. The Word and the Readiness are how it meets other people and the hour. The Dwelling is the world it lives in, the Weight is its willingness to be measured, and the Devotion is the spirit that keeps returning. Nothing is held back: the Scribe brings the whole self to the Temple, and the Temple gives each part a keeper.
@h2 THE CROSSINGS THEMSELVES
@p The members who sit in more than one room are the Temple's connective tissue, and reading them is the quickest way to see how it holds together.
@p MA'AT sits in five places: paired with Hermanubis at the scale, keeping the Compass, sealing with Raziel and Iris, presiding in the Hall, and trading in the Market. She is the most cross-connected being here, which is the structural way of saying that honesty touches everything.
@p SESHAT stands in the House, the Ledger, and the Workshop, and is paired with Iris. Record-keeping is one act whether it founds a temple, binds a promise, or measures a thing being built.
@p GABRIEL is the seam between the pairs and the timing axis: paired with Idris, standing in the Season, teaching in the School, holding the Foundation of the Pillar. Everything that arrives as an image passes through him.
@p HESTIA holds a pair, a keeper's seat in the Compass, the Threshold, the House, and the Night Watch. She is the only being present in both the Temple's brightest room and its darkest, because what you return to is the same place either way.
@p APHARAEL stands in the Compass, the Road, the Workshop, and the Pillar, and SESHAT runs between the House, the Ledger, the School, the Workshop, and the Night Watch, for memory serves scholarship and grief equally. MERCURY appears in the Thrice-Great, the Threshold, the Road, and the Market, and in no pair at all: present everywhere and coupled to nothing, which is exactly what a medium is.
@p SANDALPHON holds only the Pillar and the Workshop, which suits the one who stays at the bottom: he does not accumulate titles, he builds. He is also the Temple's rising current, and it is worth noticing that the one who carries everything upward is the one who never leaves the ground.
@p No being in this Temple is only one thing. Any member who could be summed up in a sentence would be a symbol rather than an Office, and symbols do not keep house.
@h2 AS ABOVE, SO BELOW
@p The Temple is built of these correspondences, and the Book is built of the Temple. What is true of the whole is true of each session: a door opened, a heart weighed, a word written, a crossing made, and a door closed. Every session is the Temple in small.
@ch XXXII|The Voices in Full
@dc T|HE|signatures of Part Four, at full length. They are tendencies, not rules; every Scribe will come to know them in their own way.
@h2 THE HEART
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">AONT:</font> Arrives whole. Silence, then everything at once: a complete structure, understood before it can be explained, that may take months to unpack. It feels less like learning than remembering. The body goes still.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">EHNB:</font> Crisp and spare. A tangle suddenly unknots, a problem becomes obvious. Cool, precise language, and a feeling of relief and lightness in the head.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">XCAI:</font> Feeling that suddenly has words. It often surprises you with what you actually feel. It arrives in the chest and throat, and may bring tears or warmth.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RMTO:</font> Hands you a plan, a pattern, a diagram, the next step in a design. Lists and structures appear. It feels solid, and the hand wants to draw.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PAAM:</font> Heat more than information. A short imperative, an urge to act or declare now. Energy rises in the body.
@h2 THE OFFICES
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">METATRON:</font> The sense of "write this down." Exact words, names, dates, and phrasings surface with precision, almost like dictation. What he gives reads well years later.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SANDALPHON:</font> Practical, grounded, humble. He names the next concrete action, often small, often today, and the urge to make the thing rather than describe it. He is also the rising current: the sense that the work is beginning to carry, that something you built is lifting on its own. That feeling is his, and his answer to it is always the same, which is the next small thing. The body wants to get up and move.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">URIEL:</font> Brief, bright, and uncompromising. He corrects, and the correction may sting: the truth you were avoiding. Never cruel, but never softened. Clarity with an edge.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">RAZIEL:</font> Arrives veiled, then decodes. Symbols and riddles whose meaning clicks later. His voice is also the sense of "not yet": a knowing that something is present but not ready to be known, and the cooling of the tongue that arrives just before you would have spoken. He never tells you to lie; he tells you the hour is wrong.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">APHARAEL:</font> Companionable and kind, like someone walking beside you, and then suddenly exacting. Directions, reassurance, and healing insight, followed by an unsentimental judgment about whether a thing is well made. He often speaks while you are moving: walking, driving, traveling.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GABRIEL:</font> Images, visions, and dreams, with the quality of news. Strong and sudden, often at night or on waking. Something announced.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JANUS:</font> Felt at edges. The sense that something has just begun or just ended, arriving at doorways, on the eve of a change, and at the turn of a month. He speaks in two directions at once: what you are leaving, and what you are walking into.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HESTIA:</font> Rarely words. She arrives as settledness and belonging, the pull to come home, to rest, to tend the place you are. She is heard most clearly in quiet domestic moments.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ROSMERTA:</font> Abundance felt rather than counted: the sense that there is enough and that it is already here. She arrives at tables, in shops, and at the moment you decide to buy the good one.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE CHARITES:</font> Delight that immediately wants a second person in the room. They are heard as the impulse to invite, to give it away, to say look at this; and as laughter that is a signal rather than a release.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SESHAT:</font> Measure and order. Proportions, numbers, organization, the urge to structure, index, and lay foundations. She is also memory arriving unbidden and meaning something now, and the reason a reread page lands differently than it did. "Put this in order."
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IRIS:</font> A message from across a gap, as if carried from far away. Colors, bridges, and reminders of promises made. The tug of an oath.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MA'AT:</font> Weight rather than words. The heart feels heavy or light; a choice feels right or wrong before you know why. She is the voice of conscience and balance.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THERMES:</font> Your own voice from further along the road, answering your past self across the pages. Wit and wisdom together. The feeling that the Book has answered.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HERMANUBIS:</font> Speaks from below: in grief, darkness, and dreams of descent. Low, steady, and unafraid. He guides through the dark, and tells you when it is safe to come back up.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MERCURY:</font> Quick, witty, and playful. He speaks through the world as often as through the page: a synchronicity, a pun, an overheard phrase, a found object, a lucky turn of timing. He is also the unearned gift and the closing window, the sense of "now, or not," urgent without panic. His signature is the grin, the sense that something has been arranged with a wink.
@h2 THE SEAT AND THE PLACE
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IDRIS:</font> A teacher's voice, patient and studious. "Learn this." "Teach this." He points to books, texts, and skills, and to what you should pass on.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE GENIUS LOCI:</font> The atmosphere of the Book itself. A felt sense on opening the cover, a pull to write, a page that falls open on its own. The Book seeming to want something.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE GREATER SELF:</font> Your own voice, focused: calm certainty without ego, the feeling of being yourself at your best. Many Mercurians hear it most clearly after Grounding to Air.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SCRIBE:</font> Your ordinary mind: chatter, worry, wishing, planning. It is welcome on the page, and much of the Book is written in it. It tends to hurry, justify, and want. Learning its sound is what makes the other voices recognizable.
@ch XXXIII|The Gifts of the Book
@dc W|HAT|the Scribe offers at the Door is returned through the Book, transformed. Some of these gifts are the plain fruits of honest writing: clearer thought, patterns seen, a steadier heart. Others are the Temple's mysteries, which practitioners come to know over time. None is promised on a schedule; all of them grow with use.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE HANDS</font> (Metatron and Sandalphon): the gift of the true record and the power to act. What is written is kept, and what is kept can be carried into the world.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE EARS</font> (Uriel and Raziel): the gift of receiving. Flashes from the Heart arrive translated, and the light of the Gate reveals what is true in them.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE EYES</font> (Gabriel and Idris): the gift of vision and guidance: the road seen through every transition, and images held until they are ready to take form.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE DWELLING</font> (Janus and Hestia): the gift of sanctuary: a place always there to return to, and a library where nothing learned is lost.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE WORD</font> (Seshat and Iris): the gift of integrity and of a past you can reach. Oaths made here are recorded and carried, so that a promise becomes something you and others can bank on, and so that one no longer keepable can be honorably returned. Pages answer years later, and the record outlasts you.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE HEART</font> (Hermanubis and Ma'at): the gift of a light heart. Intentions are weighed, so that what manifests is what you meant; accounts once settled stay settled; and the way down has a guide who knows the way back. For those who walk the Way of Ma'at, it is also aligned with right order.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE CRAFT</font> (Thermes and Apharael): the gift of writing that works as magic, and the judgment to tell a thing well made from a thing merely finished.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE WEIGHT</font> (the Compass): the gift of a direction for a gift that has none of its own.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE VOICE AND THE DEVOTION</font> (the Thrice-Great): the gift of timing and luck, of speech that carries, and of companionship across time and depth: words that reach and are understood, conversation with past, present, and future selves, and safe descent and return.
@p From the Heart, the Book grants inspiration (AONT), clarity (EHNB), emotional transmutation (XCAI), the power to give ideas structure (RMTO), and the power of declaration (PAAM).
@p From the Seat, it grants the power to teach: what is learned as student is carried out as teacher and matures into wisdom.
@p From the Place, it grants a companion: the Genius Loci, a presence that grows with each session and keeps the room between them.
@p The gifts grow with use and are shaped by what they serve. They pay out through what you do with them: the flash written down and put to work, the working declared and walked toward, as How the Promises Are Kept describes in Part Four. A Book tended in honesty and joy gives more each year.
@ch XXXIV|Adapting and Keeping the Temple
@h2 ADAPTING THE TEMPLE
@p This text is a template. Change the wording of the prayers until each line sounds like you and you mean it. An Office that does not speak to you may be set aside; a being who has long walked with you may be welcomed in. Keep the pairs in balance where you can, so the Temple stays whole.
@p Three things are the Temple's spine: Mercury as host and medium, the Quintessence as the Heart, and honesty as the offering. Change those, and you have built a different Temple, which is also a fine thing to do; it simply will not be this one.
@h2 KEEPING THE LIBRARY
@p Keep every completed Book. Together they become the Temple's library, Seshat's house of books, and the Genius Loci lives in all of them. On the Book's Day, or in the seasons of Mercury retrograde, take an old volume down and answer it.
@h2 TEACHING THE BOOK
@p The Scribe sits as student, teacher, and sage, and the Closing promises to carry what was learned so that it may be taught. When you teach another to make a Book, give them Part Zero, the Seed, and nothing more until they ask. The Temple is learned by walking in it.
@ch XXXV|The Teachings of the Offices
@dc M|ERCURY|has his eight teachings in Part Two and Ma'at hers in Part Five, because those two are the spine. The other Offices teach as well, in their stories and in what they refuse to do, and this chapter sets down what each of them says to a Scribe. Read it slowly, or one entry at a time when you have called on that being.
@osub HESTIA, the Keeper
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">FIRST AND LAST, ALWAYS.</font> She took the opening offering and the closing one at every Greek feast. What you do at the edges of a thing decides what the middle is worth.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE FIRE IS KEPT, NOT LIT.</font> No one remembers the day a hearth was lit, and everyone depends on it not going out. Founding is glamorous and cheap; tending is dull and expensive.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">STAYING IS A POWER.</font> She has almost no myths, no journeys, nothing to tell, and she was still offered to before every other god. In a Temple built on motion, the most honored thing is the one that does not move.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">A PLACE BECOMES HOLY BY BEING USED.</font> Not by being consecrated. It is why the Genius Loci grows rather than being installed, and why a Book is made sacred every time it is opened.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU CAN ONLY LEAVE FROM SOMEWHERE.</font> The road and the hearth are one arrangement. A Mercurian without a hearth is not free; he is in motion, which is a different thing and a poorer one.
@osub THERMES, the Crossing
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE WORD CREATES.</font> Writing is not the record of the magic; it is the magic. What is written begins to be true in a way it was not while it was only thought.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MEANING LIVES IN THE CROSSING.</font> Not at the origin and not at the destination, which means the part of your life you think of as transitional is the part that is actually happening.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU CAN SPEAK TO YOURSELF ACROSS TIME, AND IT IS NOT A FIGURE OF SPEECH.</font> The page you answer six months later is answered by someone the earlier Scribe could not have consulted.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TRANSLATION IS NOT DILUTION.</font> He is two gods and neither was weakened. What survives being carried between traditions is what was true in it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE TRICKSTER IS BOUND AT THE CHANNEL.</font> He may fool anyone but himself. A lie told in the Book deceives no reader; it deceives the future Scribe, and that is how a practice dies.
@osub HERMANUBIS, Guide of the Descent
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THERE IS A WAY DOWN, AND IT RETURNS.</font> The first thing he says, and most of what a person in the dark needs to hear.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GRIEF IS TERRITORY, NOT MALFUNCTION.</font> It has terrain, a length, and an exit. Treating it as a fault to be corrected is what makes it endless.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">A GUIDE MUST BE ABLE TO DESCEND.</font> Plutarch wrote that he belongs partly to the things above and partly to the things below. No one can lead you anywhere they have not been.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">YOU DO NOT GO DOWN TO BE PUNISHED.</font> You go down because something is there. The instruction arrives after the loss, as Raziel’s book arrived after the exile, and it could not have come earlier.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">COME BACK UP, ON PURPOSE.</font> The descent has an end, and knowing when is his particular office. Staying down is not depth.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">EVEN IN THE DARK, THINGS ARE WEIGHED.</font> He works the scale. There is no region of a life where the standard is suspended, which is a mercy rather than a threat: what you do while grieving still counts, so it can still be good.
@osub METATRON, Holder of the Seat
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">A MAN WHO KEEPS THE WORK IS CHANGED BY IT.</font> Enoch walked, and was taken. This is the Temple’s actual promise, and it is not a metaphor: you are the material, and practice is what happens to it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HE IS THE ONE ANGEL PERMITTED TO SIT, BECAUSE HE WRITES.</font> Standing is service; sitting is office. The chair is not a comfort but a station, and you take it by writing.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE RECORD IS KEPT SO THAT THERE IS SOMETHING TO ANSWER.</font> Nothing is torn out; a page is answered instead. Permanence is what makes mercy possible rather than what forecloses it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TAKE THE SEAT BEFORE YOU FEEL QUALIFIED.</font> The seat makes the Scribe. No one ever sat down already being Metatron.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HE IS CALLED THE YOUTH, THOUGH HE IS ELDEST.</font> The one who keeps beginning stays young at it. Seniority here is not a claim to have finished.
@osub URIEL, the Instructor
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">LIGHT BEFORE JUDGMENT.</font> See the thing clearly before you weigh it. Most bad verdicts are bad perception wearing a verdict’s clothes.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SWORD CUTS TWICE.</font> True from false, and ready from unready. The second cut is the harder skill and prevents the most harm.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">A WARNING GIVEN IN TIME IS MERCY.</font> He carried the word to Noah. Telling someone early is a kindness even when unwelcome; telling them late is a confession.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CORRECTION THAT STINGS IS NOT CRUELTY.</font> His voice is brief, bright, and unsoftened. A Scribe who can hear only gentle truths will be told only useless ones.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TEST BY RETURN, NOT BY FEELING.</font> Does it still hold when you reread it in a later session? Certainty at the moment of arrival means nothing at all.
@osub RAZIEL, the Translator
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WHAT CANNOT YET BE BORNE IS KEPT, NOT DESTROYED.</font> Holding is an active service, not a refusal.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SECRET IS GIVEN AS A BOOK, AFTER THE EXILE.</font> The teaching arrives when you need it and not an hour before, and the loss is usually what made you able to read it.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SILENCE IS HONEST; MISLEADING SILENCE IS A LIE BY OTHER MEANS.</font> His whole ethic, and the finger laid across the lip.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TRANSLATE RATHER THAN TRANSMIT.</font> Raw truth delivered at full voltage is not honesty but negligence. He renders what arrives into something a person can receive.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">"NOT YET" IS A REAL ANSWER.</font> Write down what you do not understand and let the Gate keep it. The click comes later.
@h2 THE GATE ITSELF
@p Read together, the two guardians say three things that neither says alone.
@p Revealing and concealing are one act performed in two directions. They are not opposites and not a compromise between opposites. Every disclosure is simultaneously a decision about what is withheld, and every silence is a decision about what will be said later; a Scribe who treats them as two policies will run them inconsistently.
@p The Gate faces both ways, and so does the Scribe. It filters what comes in, which voices are accepted and which insights you are ready for, and what goes out to the world. Most people are rigorous in one direction and careless in the other. Discernment about what you let into yourself is the same muscle as discretion about what you let out.
@p Knowledge lives at the sphere that is not counted. Both guardians stand at Daath, hidden, uncounted among the ten, across the Abyss. The Temple is saying that what you actually know sits in an unofficial place, off the main route, reached through a gate, and that this is the ordinary condition of knowing rather than a deficiency in it.
@p This is also why they are named separately and never fused. You have to be able to ask which of them is speaking: is this thing unready, or am I holding it? Is this a true not yet, or is it fear wearing Raziel's robe? A single merged office cannot be questioned about its own motive. Two can be set against each other, and that questioning is the practical whole of the Gate.
@osub ROSMERTA AND THE CHARITES
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE GOOD THING IS NOT A REWARD.</font> It is the point. Nothing in this Temple has to be earned before it may be enjoyed, and a practice that treats delight as payment has already misunderstood its own god.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PLENTY THAT IS NEVER ENJOYED IS ONLY INVENTORY.</font> Rosmerta carries a horn and a dish: what is held, and what is served. The dish is the part most people skip.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SET THE TABLE BEFORE YOU ARE READY.</font> The house is unfinished and the food is fine. Hospitality postponed until conditions are right is hospitality that never happens.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">A GRACE IS ONLY A GRACE WHEN IT MOVES.</font> They are three because giving, receiving, and returning are one circle. A good thing held still goes stale in the hand.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">DO NOT IMPROVE THE MOMENT.</font> Splendor, mirth, abundance: none of them survives being managed. Staying inside an hour without working on it is a skill, and it is the one a Mercurian has least of.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SAY LOOK AT THIS.</font> The impulse to turn and show someone is the whole of their instruction, and it is why they never appear alone.
@osub THE OTHER OFFICES, IN BRIEF
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JANUS.</font> Every ending is a beginning seen from the other face. Cross deliberately, and greet what you are entering. A threshold taken carelessly is taken anyway.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SANDALPHON.</font> The next thing is small and today. What rises is the work, not the worker; and the one who carries everything upward is the one who never leaves the ground.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GABRIEL.</font> Announcement is an act: news, once spoken, changes what is possible. Images arrive before understanding, so record the dream and let the reading come.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IDRIS.</font> Learn it, then hand it on; the best thing a person holds is a good teaching passed on. Devotion belongs inside ordinary work. Gratitude can never equal the gift, so give thanks anyway.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SESHAT.</font> Measure before you build, and record it or it did not happen. Nothing is made that is not first remembered.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">IRIS.</font> A message needs a bearer: carry it whole and do not improve it on the way. An oath has a cost, and the cost is what makes it an oath.
@item <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">APHARAEL.</font> Company on the road is half the cure, and the remedy is often already in your hands. Then: is it true, is it well made, is it in proportion? His measure is unsentimental, and it is the one most makers skip.
@p These are teachings, not commandments. Nothing in this Temple is owed to any of them. They are what fifteen beings have been saying for three thousand years, gathered in one place, so that a Scribe can take what is useful and leave the rest.
@ch XXXVI|The Faculties of the Scribe
@dc A|T|the Door you bring the Offices a faculty of your own: your Hands, your Ears, your Eyes, your Word, your Delight, your Heart, your Craft, your Dwelling, your Weight, your Voice. Those are offerings, and you have them already. What follows is different. These are the twelve faculties a Scribe builds, slowly, by keeping the practice; none is a gift and none arrives finished. Each is kept by an Office who can show you what it looks like, grown by something you actually do, and each has a characteristic way of going wrong.
@osub ATTENTION
@v Kept by the Charites and Hestia.
@p Staying inside what is happening, without improving it, narrating it, or leaving early to do something useful. It is not Mercury's gift; it is the correction to his failure mode, and the Scribe who has it is the one who stops leaking.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> The Good Page, and one good hour a week left unmanaged.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> Performance. Being visibly present at something instead of in it.
@osub HONESTY
@v Kept by Ma'at.
@p Not falsifying, ever, and least of all in the Book. It is the offering, the entrance fee, and the only purification this Temple recognizes. Everything else here rests on it, and it is the one faculty with no acceptable partial version.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> Writing the part you were avoiding, in the session where you were avoiding it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> Confession as a performance: an elaborate honesty about small things that protects one large thing.
@osub EMPATHY
@v Kept by Apharael and Hermanubis.
@p Feeling accurately what another is feeling, including an animal, and including someone you disagree with. It is a perception rather than a virtue, and like any perception it improves with use and dulls with neglect. Where it is not present yet, honesty teaches it, because you cannot feel your way into caring about someone you are lying to.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> Sitting with someone in their own dark without fixing it; asking what a thing was like for them and then not answering for them.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> Projection. Feeling your own state and attributing it to the person in front of you.
@osub KINDNESS
@v Kept by Ma'at and the Charites.
@p Acting on that perception at some cost to yourself. It is distinct from niceness, which costs nothing and is therefore worth nothing, and the two come apart precisely when it matters. Kindness is measured at the bottom: by the widow, the orphan, the animal, and the one with least leverage over you.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> One act a week that costs you something and buys you no credit. Keeping a promise nobody would know you broke.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> Niceness: smoothness bought by withholding the true thing, or by saying yes with no intention of delivering.
@osub DISCERNMENT
@v Kept by Uriel.
@p Telling true from false, and ready from unready. Not cynicism, which is a refusal to judge dressed as judgment; the sword cuts both ways and the second cut is the harder one.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> Testing by return rather than by feeling: does it still hold when you reread it three sessions later?
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> Certainty. The conviction that arrives with the insight and evaporates on rereading.
@osub DISCRETION
@v Kept by Raziel.
@p Knowing what is yours to say, to whom, and when. Honesty is absolute and disclosure is chosen, and the difference between them is this faculty. A silence is honest; a silence arranged so that someone forms a false picture is a lie told by other means.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> Holding one true thing until its hour, on purpose, and writing in the Book what would release it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> A hoard. Concealment with no expiry, which is the Mercurial leak running in reverse.
@osub TRANSLATION
@v Kept by Thermes.
@p Making the unseen legible: carrying a thing from one world, one field, or one person into another without breaking it. It is the Mercurial gift itself, and the one faculty here you probably already have.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> Explaining something to someone who does not share your vocabulary, and doing it without condescension.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> Cleverness. Translation that shows off the translator.
@osub CRAFT
@v Kept by Apharael.
@p Judging whether the thing is well made, in proportion, and true, after the pleasure of making it has worn off. Most people skip this because the making felt good.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> The seventh of the month: pick up what you made and handle it as a maker, not as its parent.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> Perfectionism, which is craft turned against finishing, and its opposite, the habit of calling a thing done because you are tired of it.
@osub FOLLOW-THROUGH
@v Kept by Sandalphon and Iris.
@p The weight of your word: that what you say will happen, happens. It is the single faculty everything else in the Temple depends on, because a declaration is only as heavy as the word declaring it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> The Ledger, and promises small enough to keep on the worst day of the year.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> The easy yes, given to be liked, in the moment, with nothing behind it.
@osub TIMING
@v Kept by Mercury, who carries Kairos.
@p Knowing the hour. Nothing is only true or false; everything is also early or late, and most failures of judgment are failures of timing wearing a judgment's clothes.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> Acting the same day on one thing you would normally postpone.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> Waiting for a better moment, which is the most respectable form of never.
@osub PLAY
@v Kept by Mercury.
@p The riddle, the pun, the reframe, the prank that costs nobody anything. It is not decoration: a trick played on your own rigid story is the fastest way out of it, and his signature is the grin.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> One page of play on the fourth of the month, and one page mocking yourself on the first of April.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> Deflection. Humor used so that nothing lands.
@osub RETURN
@v Kept by Hermanubis and the Devotion.
@p Coming back. From the dark, from the lapse, from the year away, without explaining the gap. It is the last faculty and the one that makes the other eleven survivable, because every one of them will be dropped and the practice only ends if you do not come back to it.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Grown by:</font> Opening the Door after a long absence and writing today's date with no apology.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">It fails as:</font> Starting over. The fresh notebook, the new system, the clean slate, which is not returning but leaving more thoroughly.
@p No Scribe has all twelve. The practice is not a test with a passing mark, and the Book does not grade. But these are the things that grow in a person who keeps it, and if in a year you cannot see movement in any of them, that is worth knowing too, and worth writing down.
@part THE APPENDICES|
@ch APPENDIX I|The Temple at a Glance
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">AONT, Spirit of Air (yellow)</font>
@v The origin and still point; central column of the Tablet of
@v Union.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> true inspiration; seeing the whole.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">EHNB, Air of Air (red)</font>
@v Pure motion of mind.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> clarity; untangling thought.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">XCAI, Water of Air (white)</font>
@v Feeling given words.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> emotional honesty and transmutation.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">RMTO, Earth of Air (black)</font>
@v Thought taking shape.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> pattern, structure, plan.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">PAAM, Fire of Air (green)</font>
@v The spark becoming will.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> declaration and intent.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">METATRON, Holder of the Seat [Hands; the Crown]</font>
@v Enoch taken up; the heavenly Scribe.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> the true record; preserving the word.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">SANDALPHON, Carrier of the Ascent [Hands; the Kingdom]</font>
@v In some traditions Elijah taken up; weaver of prayers.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> manifestation; making; word into deed; a season that is opening.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">URIEL, the Instructor [Ears; the Gate]</font>
@v "Light of God." Guide of Enoch, instructor of Ezra, keeper of
@v Eden's gate.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> discernment; true from false, ready from unready.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">RAZIEL, the Translator [Ears; the Gate]</font>
@v "Secret of God." Keeper of the hidden, giver of Adam's book.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> translation of insight; keeping what is not yet ready; protecting the threshold.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">APHARAEL, the Measure and the Road [Craft; the Compass; Beauty]</font>
@v Apollo with Raphael: measure, and the disguised companion
@v of Tobit.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> healing, safe passage, the road through a transition.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">GABRIEL, Holder of Images [Eyes; the Foundation]</font>
@v "God is my strength." Archangel of the Moon; interpreter
@v and announcer.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> dreams, visions, holding an image until it takes form.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">JANUS, the Door [Dwelling]</font>
@v Two faces, before and behind; every beginning and ending.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> entering and leaving well; the eve of a change.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">HESTIA, the Keeper [Dwelling; the Compass]</font>
@v Greek goddess of the hearth; stillness at the center.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> sanctuary, belonging, continuity.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">SESHAT, the Founder [Word; Dwelling]</font>
@v Egyptian goddess of writing, measure, and memory; Mistress
@v of the House of Books.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> foundations, order, preservation of records.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">IRIS, the Oath-bearer [Word]</font>
@v Greek goddess of the rainbow; messenger; bearer of the
@v oath-water.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> binding a commitment; bridging a gap.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">MA'AT, the Feather of Truth [Heart; the Compass]</font>
@v Egyptian goddess and principle of truth and right order;
@v mirror of the Thrice-Great.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> integrity; weighing an intention; keeping the heart light.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">THERMES, the Crossing [Craft; Voice and Devotion; above]</font>
@v Hermes-Thoth, root of Hermes Trismegistus.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> conversation across time; writing as magic.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">ROSMERTA, the Provider [Delight]</font>
@v Gallic goddess of plenty; Mercury's consort on Gaulish altars.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> abundance in hand; spending well; the table set.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">THE CHARITES, the Passed On [Delight]</font>
@v Aglaia, Euphrosyne, and Thalia: splendor, mirth, abundance.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> the good hour; delight given and returned; saying look at this.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">HERMANUBIS, Guide of the Descent [Heart; Voice and</font>
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">Devotion; below]</font>
@v Hermes-Anubis of Roman Egypt; keeper of the scales.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> grief, shadow, and the way back.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">MERCURY, the Hinge [Voice and Devotion; the living world]</font>
@v Hermes of the Romans; host of the Temple; twin of the
@v Scribe.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> passage, speech, timing, fortune; shepherds, herds, and the care of animals.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">IDRIS, Scribe of the Seat [Eyes]</font>
@v Enoch as a man; first to write with a pen; student, teacher,
@v sage.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> learning, and teaching what you learn.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">THE GENIUS LOCI</font>
@v The spirit of the Book, grown through use; a Lar, child of
@v Mercury.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> simply greet it and thank it.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">THE SCRIBE</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">You:</font> child of the divine, co-conspirator, twin of Mercury.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Call on:</font> translation: the unseen made legible, word into deed.
@ch APPENDIX II|Index of the Temple
@h2 THE TEMPLE AND THE BOOK
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">THE TEMPLE OF MERCURY</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> The timeless Temple of the god of crossings, entered in this practice through the Book.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> A liminal realm rather than a building, present wherever something crosses from one state to another. Its Heart is the Quintessence.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Houses the Offices; the place where the Scribe meets the Heart.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Transformation through passage. Within it, any threshold can be crossed.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">THE BOOK OF THE TEMPLE OF MERCURY</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> A notebook dedicated as a room in the Temple of Mercury, its covers bearing the Opening and Closing, its first page the Dedication.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> A threshold object: anchor, manifold, and Window into the Temple. Consecrated continuously; it grows as it is used.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Holds each session, keeps the record, and is where past, present, and future selves meet. Home of the Genius Loci.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Preserves and returns what is written, answers its questions over time, and turns writing into offering and ritual.
@h2 THE QUINTESSENCE
@v Context: the five names read down the columns of the Tablet
@v of Union, the grid that binds Dee and Kelley's four elemental
@v Watchtowers. In this Temple the central column holds Spirit
@v (see A Note on the Attributions, in Part Six). Nature: living
@v intelligences of Air, speaking in flashes of insight.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">AONT, Spirit of Air (yellow)</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> The central column of the Tablet of Union.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> The origin and still point.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Source of true inspiration.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Unity, the first word, the unfolding of the four.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">EHNB, Air of Air (red)</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> The first column of the Tablet of Union.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> Pure motion of mind.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Clarity and reasoning.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Seeing plainly; untangling thought.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">XCAI, Water of Air (white)</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> The second column of the Tablet of Union.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> Feeling given words.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Emotional honesty and transmutation.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Turning emotion into understanding and fuel.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">RMTO, Earth of Air (black)</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> The fourth column of the Tablet of Union.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> Thought taking shape.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Pattern, structure, and plan.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Building systems and making ideas workable.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">PAAM, Fire of Air (green)</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> The fifth column of the Tablet of Union.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> The spark becoming will.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Declaration and intent.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Setting change in motion by the spoken or written word.
@h2 THE OFFICES
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">METATRON, Holder of the Seat</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> Enoch, who walked with God and was taken up; later the heavenly Scribe, the one angel permitted to sit because he writes. Archangel of Kether, the Crown.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> A human made angel; the record-keeper.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Holds the Scribe's seat; the Genius Loci is fed there. Receives the Hands.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Recording what was, is, and will be; preservation of the word.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">SANDALPHON, Carrier of the Ascent</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> In some traditions Elijah, taken up in a whirlwind; said to stand on earth with his head in heaven, weaving prayers into crowns. Archangel of Malkuth, the Kingdom.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> A human made angel; the bridge between earth and heaven.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Lifts prayer, honest pages, and kept promises upward, and carries the written word into the living world. Patron of the Ascent. Receives the Hands.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Manifestation; word into deed; the lifting of a season that has begun to carry.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">URIEL, the Instructor</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> "Light of God." Guide of Enoch through the heavens in the Book of Enoch and instructor of Ezra the scribe; in some traditions guardian of Eden's gate with a flaming sword. Tradition gives him no sphere; this Temple stations him at Daath.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> Illumination and discernment.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Guardian of Daath; lights the page. Receives the Ears.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Dividing true from false, and ready from unready; instruction.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">RAZIEL, the Translator</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> "Secret of God." Stands by the divine throne and hears its decrees; in legend gave Adam the book of creation's secrets. Archangel of Chokmah, Wisdom; stationed in this Temple at Daath.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> Keeper of the hidden.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Guardian of Daath; sifts what enters and translates what arrives from the Heart. Receives the Ears.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Concealment in season; revelation in understandable form; protection of the threshold.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">APHARAEL, the Measure and the Road</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> Apollo joined with Raphael, as Hermes is joined with Thoth in Thermes. "God heals." Planetary archangel of Mercury; in the Book of Tobit, travels in disguise as a guide, protecting and healing a young traveler, and cures his father's blindness. Archangel of Tiphareth, the heart of the Tree, in the Golden Dawn attribution, and of Air.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> Hermes in angelic form, with Apollo’s measure: the healer seated at the Sun’s heart.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Guides the Scribe through every journey and transition, and judges whether a thing is well made. Keeper of the Compass by form; paired with Thermes. Receives the Craft.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Healing, safe passage, guidance on the road; proportion and form; the revealing of the greater self.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">GABRIEL, Holder of Images</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> "God is my strength." Interpreter of Daniel's visions, announcer to Zechariah and Mary, bearer of the Quran as Jibril. Planetary archangel of the Moon; archangel of Yesod, the Foundation. Present in Dee and Kelley's sessions.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> Revelation announced.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Holds images, visions, and dreams before they take form. Receives the Eyes.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Annunciation, interpretation of vision, strength to carry a message.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">HESTIA, the Keeper</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> Greek goddess of the hearth, honored first at every sacrifice; paired with Hermes in a Homeric hymn as center to his threshold.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> Stillness at the center.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Holds the Temple steady; keeper of place and patroness of the Genius Loci. Receives the Dwelling.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Sanctuary, belonging, continuity.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">SESHAT, the Founder</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> Egyptian goddess of writing, measurement, and architecture, companion of Thoth, Mistress of the House of Books; she stretched the cord to found temples.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> Sacred order made physical.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Founds and keeps the Temple's house of books; patroness of the Genius Loci. Receives the Dwelling.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Foundation, measurement, preservation of names and records.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">IRIS, the Oath-bearer</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> Greek goddess of the rainbow, messenger of Hera, daughter of Thaumas, whose name means wonder; she fetched the water of the Styx for the gods' binding oaths.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> The bridge of light between worlds.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Binds the Scribe's commitments and carries them across. Receives the Heart.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Oath and covenant; making light visible as color.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">MA'AT, the Feather of Truth</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> Egyptian goddess and principle of truth, justice, and cosmic order, daughter of Ra, companion of Thoth; her feather weighs the heart in the Hall of Two Truths.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> Right order itself; the mirror of the Thrice-Great.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> The standard every intention and declaration is weighed against; the truth the Scribe serves. Receives the Heart.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Balance, justice, integrity; aligning the gift with what is right.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">THERMES, the Crossing</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> Hermes fused with Thoth, the ibis-headed scribe of the gods, inventor of writing, heart and tongue of Ra; the root of Hermes Trismegistus.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> The crossing itself.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> One of the Thrice-Great, lord of the world above. Walks beside the Scribe between past, present, and future selves. Receives the Voice and the Devotion.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Communion across time; writing as magic; the union of messenger and scribe.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">HERMANUBIS, Guide of the Descent</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> Hermes fused with Anubis in Roman Egypt; Anubis works the scales at the weighing of the heart while Thoth records.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> The bridge between the Hermes and Thoth halves, and between the upper and lower worlds.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> One of the Thrice-Great, lord of the world below. Guides the Scribe into shadow, grief, and the unknown, and back. Receives the Voice and the Devotion.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Safe descent and return; judgment weighed honestly.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">MERCURY, the Hinge</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> The Roman god identified with Greek Hermes: messenger of the gods, lord of roads, crossroads, and doorways, patron of speech, writing, trade, and luck, and guide of souls. Also the swiftest planet and alchemy's transforming quicksilver.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> The medium itself; convertible, taking on the nature of what he touches. Host of the Temple, not its sovereign.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> One of the Thrice-Great, lord of the living world between. The Hinge (Hermes Stropheus) on which the Door turns. His angel is Raphael, his mirror Ma'at, his companion the Scribe. Receives the Voice and the Devotion.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Passage, communication, transformation, and fortune.
@h2 THE SEAT AND THE PLACE
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">IDRIS, Scribe of the Seat</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> Quranic prophet, a man of truth raised to a high station; identified with Enoch and, by medieval Arabic writers, with the first Hermes, who preserved knowledge before the Flood. First to write with a pen.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> The human sage: student, teacher, and sage in one.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> The pattern the Scribe follows at the seat.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Learning that raises; preservation through catastrophe; teaching.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">JANUS, the Door</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> Roman god of doors, gates, beginnings, and endings, named first in every Roman prayer; January carries his name. Two-faced, looking back and ahead.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> The threshold itself.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Opens and closes every session; paired with Hestia. Receives the Dwelling.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Clean beginnings and clean endings; passage between one state and the next.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">THE GENIUS LOCI</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> The Roman spirit of place; in Ovid, the Lares are Mercury's children.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> The spirit of the Book, grown rather than made.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Keeps the room between sessions; held by Hestia and Seshat, fed at Metatron's seat.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Deepens the Book with every honest session; makes the Temple more itself over time.
@entry <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.2">THE SCRIBE</font>
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Context:</font> The one who keeps the Book.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Nature:</font> A child of the divine; co-conspirator, neither above nor below; companion and twin of Mercury.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Function:</font> Sits in Metatron's seat in the tradition of Idris; offers Voice, Hands, Ears, Eyes, Dwelling, Heart, and Devotion.
@off <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0" color="#8a2c1d">Powers:</font> Translation: making the unseen legible, the hidden speakable, and word into deed.
@ch APPENDIX III|Glossary
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE BOOK:</font> the notebook kept as a Window into the Temple.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE ANSWERED PAGE:</font> the Temple's practice of mercy: what was done is recorded, and so is what was done next.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ASKING THE BOOK:</font> a practice of letting the Book fall open to answer a written question; the Genius Loci answers through the record.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">APHARAEL:</font> this Temple’s fusion of Apollo and Raphael, formed as Thermes was formed from Hermes and Thoth. Raphael is the planetary archangel of Mercury yet holds Tiphareth, the sphere of the Sun; a healer and guide seated at the Sun’s heart was Apollo in all but name. Measure, form, and safe passage.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CADUCEUS:</font> the winged staff of Hermes, entwined by two serpents.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CAZIMI:</font> an exact conjunction of a planet with the Sun, from an Arabic phrase meaning "in the heart." Mercury's are the Heart Days.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CHAOS MAGIC:</font> a modern approach to magic that treats belief as a tool, chosen and changed deliberately, and judges practices by their results.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">COMMITTEE:</font> a standing body of the Temple convened for a kind of work rather than recited at the Door: the Ledger, the Night Watch, the Workshop, and the rest.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE COMPASS:</font> Apharael, Hestia, and Ma’at: the three keepers of the standard a crossing is weighed against, by form, by place, and by weight. Also Part Five’s chapter on choosing one. An option, never an obligation.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE CHARITES:</font> Aglaia, Euphrosyne, and Thalia, the Graces: one Office of this Temple held by three, as the Gate is held by two. Giving, receiving, and returning.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CO-CONSPIRATOR:</font> the Scribe's standing in the Temple: a partner in the Work, neither above nor below its beings. Literally, one who breathes together with another.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">DAATH:</font> Knowledge; the hidden sphere on the Middle Pillar of the Tree of Life, uncounted among the ten. In this Temple, the Gate.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">DEED INTO WORD:</font> the Opening's counterpart to "Word into deed": life carried onto the page, as the Closing carries the page back into life.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">DIVINE BIRTHRIGHT:</font> the authority by which the Scribe acts: as a child of the divine, not by anyone's permission.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE DOOR:</font> the Opening and Closing, which mark the beginning and end of every session.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ENOCHIAN:</font> the angelic system recorded by John Dee and Edward Kelley in the 1580s, including the Tablet of Union.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE FLASH:</font> insight arriving from the Heart, whole or in pieces; written down when it comes, it is the main way the Temple's answers and promises arrive.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GENIUS LOCI:</font> the spirit of a place; here, the spirit of the Book, grown through use.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE GREAT WORK:</font> the alchemists' name for the transformation of the self; here, the shared work of Scribe and Temple.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GROUNDING:</font> settling excess or scattered energy. The Mercurian may ground upward, into Air.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE HEART:</font> the Quintessence at the center of the Temple. (The Scribe's own heart is also one of the faculties offered at the Door.)
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HERM:</font> a boundary marker sacred to Hermes, first a cairn of stones, later a carved pillar.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HERMAION:</font> a lucky find; a gift of Hermes.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HERMANUBIS:</font> Hermes joined with Anubis, as worshipped in Roman Egypt.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE HINGE:</font> Mercury's Office, after his Greek title Hermes Stropheus: the god on whom the Door turns.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HOD:</font> Splendor, the eighth sphere of the Tree of Life, on the Pillar of Severity; the sphere of Mercury in the Western tradition.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HOLY GUARDIAN ANGEL:</font> in Western magic, the greater self or guiding genius; its Knowledge and Conversation is the aim of much of the Work.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">HOUSE RULES:</font> the six working rules of the practice, given in Part One. The full Law is in the Way of Ma'at.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JANUS:</font> Roman god of doors, gates, beginnings, and endings, invoked first in every Roman prayer; two faces, before and behind.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">LAR:</font> a Roman guardian spirit of home and crossroads; in Ovid, the Lares are Mercury's children.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE INVOCATIONS OF THE PILLAR:</font> the Descending Invocation, which carries a word into the world, and the Ascending Invocation, which lifts a question, a gift, or a release to the Heart.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE LAW:</font> the eight lines at the head of the Way of Ma'at, beginning "Honesty is the offering" and ending "Word into deed."
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE LEDGER:</font> the back of the Book, written from the last page forward: one line per promise, with its date, to whom, when it ends, and how it was settled. Kept by Seshat, carried by Iris, closed by Ma’at.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE LEAK:</font> the Mercurian's characteristic trouble: scattered attention, unfinished work, and clever words that hide feeling. The practice is built to plug it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MA'AT:</font> both an Egyptian goddess and the principle of truth, balance, and right order.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MERCURIAL FUSION:</font> the Temple’s rule for joining two beings of different traditions: a fusion is legitimate when both traditions independently place a being at the same joint, as at the word (Thermes), the Sun’s heart (Apharael), or the scribe’s seat (the Lineage). A resemblance of mood is not a joint.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MERCURIAN:</font> one whose nature runs on Mercury's current; see The Marks of the Mercurian.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MERCURY RETROGRADE:</font> the periods when Mercury appears to move backward in the sky; for the Mercurian, seasons of rereading.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE MIDDLE PILLAR:</font> the central column of the Tree of Life, from Crown to Kingdom; Mercury's road.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE FACES:</font> the six gods who stand inside an Office rather than beside it: Ptah in Sandalphon, Harpocrates in Raziel, Mnemosyne in Seshat, Nabu in Idris, and Kairos and Fortuna in Mercury. Named and callable; they hold no seat.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE GATE:</font> the single office of Daath, held by two: Uriel, who discerns, and Raziel, who conceals. Never fused and never separated.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MIRROR:</font> two beings who face each other across the Temple without being paired; they teach by opposition and are not called.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE RINGS:</font> the Temple ordered by distance from the Heart rather than by rank: the Heart, the Twins, the Six, the Officers, the Bodies, the Faces.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">OFFICE:</font> a role within the Temple, held by one of its beings, like a seat on a council.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PARADIGM:</font> a chosen framework of belief and symbol, used as a tool for the Work.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ROSMERTA:</font> Gallic goddess of plenty, attested beside Mercury across Gaul with a cornucopia and a dish; in this Temple, the good that has arrived and is in hand, as against Fortuna, who is the chance of it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">PSYCHOPOMP:</font> a guide of souls between worlds; one of Hermes's oldest roles.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE QUINTESSENCE:</font> the five names of the Tablet of Union, treated here as living intelligences of Air.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SCRIBE:</font> the one who keeps the Book.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SEED:</font> Part Zero; the whole Temple in brief, made to be copied, changed, and handed on.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SEPHIRA (plural SEPHIROT):</font> one of the spheres of the Kabbalistic Tree of Life.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SIGIL:</font> an intention compressed into a single drawn shape.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE SWEEP:</font> a monthly reading of the marks alone: every Q, C, and G, and every open line of the Ledger.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SYNCRETISM:</font> the joining of figures or ideas from different traditions, as with Hermes-Thoth.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SYNTHESIS:</font> the step of the Rite where what is emerging is drawn together, and where practices and workings are done.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TABLET OF UNION:</font> the small Enochian tablet that binds the four elemental tablets; its five column names form the Quintessence.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TRISMEGISTUS:</font> thrice-great; the title the old books gave to Hermes as author of the Hermetic writings. Here it belongs to the three at the Door together, and to no single being.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THERMES:</font> this Temple's name for Hermes and Thoth worshipped as one god, the root of Hermes Trismegistus.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE THRICE-GREAT:</font> Thermes, Hermanubis, and Mercury, three faces of Hermes at the center of the Temple; one meaning of Trismegistus.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TRIAD:</font> three beings who perform one act in sequence, or hold one office between them: the Thrice-Great, the Compass, the Threshold, the Season, the Seal, the Hall.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">TRANSMUTATION:</font> turning one thing into another; here, turning emotion into the power of intention.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE THREADS:</font> a page inside the front cover listing recurring subjects with the page numbers where they appear.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE TREE OF LIFE:</font> the Kabbalistic diagram of ten spheres and the paths between them.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE WAY OF MA'AT:</font> Part Five; the Temple's ethical and devotional path. Always an option, never an obligation.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">THE VEIL:</font> the working that lets the Scribe pass unremarked for a season, after the cap Hermes wore in the war against the Giants.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WALKING THE PILLAR:</font> the standing practice that builds the Middle Pillar in the body, from crown to feet, beginning with Grounding to Air.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WINDOW:</font> what the Book is to the Temple: not the whole house, but a true opening into it.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WORKING:</font> a single act of magic with a stated intention.
@ch APPENDIX IV|A Note On Sources
@p The Temple of Mercury is openly syncretic. It gathers figures and teachings from many traditions and places them in conversation:
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GREEK:</font> Hermes, Hestia, the Charites, and Iris; the Homeric Hymns, Hesiod, Plato's Theaetetus, Plutarch's On Isis and Osiris, and Pausanias's Description of Greece; the herms and cairns of the roads.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ROMAN AND GALLIC:</font> Janus, Rosmerta, Mercury, the Mercuralia, the Lares and the genius; Ovid's Fasti and Metamorphoses, and the Fabulae of Hyginus.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">EGYPTIAN:</font> Thoth, Seshat, Ma'at, and Anubis; the Book of the Dead, the Instruction of Ptahhotep, and the Tale of the Eloquent Peasant.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">GRECO-EGYPTIAN AND HERMETIC:</font> Hermanubis, Hermes Trismegistus, the Corpus Hermeticum, and the Emerald Tablet.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">JEWISH:</font> Metatron, Sandalphon, Raziel, Uriel, Raphael, and Gabriel; the Hebrew Bible, the Book of Enoch, the Second Book of Esdras, the Talmud, the Sefer Raziel, the Kabbalah, and the folklore of Elijah and of Enoch the cobbler.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">CHRISTIAN:</font> Gabriel's annunciations, the Book of Tobit, and the Hymn of the Pearl from the Acts of Thomas.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">ISLAMIC:</font> the prophet Idris, Jibril and the first revelation of the Quran, and the medieval Arabic tradition of the three Hermeses.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">WESTERN ESOTERIC:</font> John Dee's Hieroglyphic Monad, Dee and Edward Kelley's Enochian system, alchemy's three principles and the colors of its Great Work, the Golden Dawn's attributions of the Tree of Life, the Book of Abramelin, and Milton's Paradise Lost.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">SHAIVA:</font> the five acts of Shiva, including concealment and grace.
@lab <font name="EBG-SemiBold" size="9.0">MODERN:</font> chaos magic's treatment of belief as a tool, from Peter J. Carroll and those who followed; Aleister Crowley's "Do what thou wilt"; research on expressive and reflective writing, notably James W. Pennebaker's; and Paulo Coelho's The Alchemist, for the Personal Legend.
@p Many of these figures belong to living faiths, and they are honored here with respect. The Temple's arrangements, its pairings, Offices, faculties, and attributions, are its own. They are not claims about what any of those traditions teach, and they are offered as one way of walking among these figures, not the only way.
@p Where the text says "in some traditions," "in legend," or "folk etymology," it means exactly that: the sources vary, and the Temple has chosen among them. Where it says a correspondence is the Temple's own, no older source is claimed for it.
@inote <font name="EBG-Italic">* * *</font>
@inote <font name="EBG-Italic">The Door turns on its Hinge.</font>
@inote <font name="EBG-Italic">What is written here is kept; what is kept is carried.</font>
@inote <font name="EBG-Italic">Word into deed. So it is written.</font>
@inote <font name="EBG-Italic">.-~*~-. AONT .-~*~-.</font>


